    rateTableTitle: "Retirement planning rules at a glance",
    rateTableNote: "The expense inflation, the growing-annuity corpus, the real return after retirement, the 4% and 3% cross-checks, the savings growth, the step-up SIP and the retirement-age effect. Every figure is traced to its source in the methodology section.",
    rateTableConclusion: "Inflation decides what your expenses become, the real return after retirement decides the corpus, and starting early decides the SIP.",
    calloutTitle: "The ₹50,000-a-month plan, worked out in full",
    calloutNote: "Blogbanking's published headline scenario: a 30-year-old spending ₹50,000 a month today, with ₹5,00,000 saved, retiring at 60 with the money lasting to 85, 6% inflation, 12% return before retirement and 8% after. The expenses become ₹2,87,175 a month at 60. The corpus is ₹6,94,68,933 — 20.2 times the first year's expenses, or ₹1,20,95,245 in today's money. The ₹5,00,000 grows to ₹1,49,79,961, leaving a shortfall of ₹5,44,88,972. The flat SIP is ₹15,436 a month; with a 10% yearly step-up the first SIP is ₹6,168. Or invest ₹18,18,729 once today. The first-year withdrawal is 4.96% of the corpus — about 5%. One honest difference: if the return after retirement is 1% lower (7%), the same plan needs ₹76.8 lakh more.",
    faq: [
      ["How much money do I need to retire in India?", "It depends on your expenses, years to retirement and how long the money must last. A 30-year-old spending ₹50,000 a month today will spend about ₹2,87,175 a month at 60 if inflation averages 6%. To fund that, rising with inflation, until 85 at an 8% return needs a corpus of about ₹6.95 crore."],
      ["How is the retirement corpus calculated?", "Today's expenses are inflated to your retirement age. The corpus is the present value, on the day you retire, of a yearly expense that keeps rising with inflation until your life expectancy, discounted at your post-retirement return. That is a growing annuity: corpus = first year's expenses × (1 − q^n) ÷ (1 − q), where q = (1 + inflation) ÷ (1 + post-retirement return) and n is the years in retirement. Expenses are drawn at the start of each year."],
      ["What is the 4% rule, and does it work in India?", "The 4% rule says you can withdraw 4% of your corpus in the first year, then raise it with inflation, and the money should last about 30 years. It comes from US market data (Bengen 1994; the Trinity study, 1998) with inflation near 3%. Indian inflation has averaged closer to 5–6%, and early retirees need money for 40 years or more, so many Indian planners use 3–3.5%, which means a corpus of about 29–33 times yearly expenses. This page shows both the 25× and 33× checks."],
      ["What monthly SIP do I need to retire?", "For the headline plan — 30 years old, ₹50,000 a month of expenses today, ₹5,00,000 saved, retiring at 60 — the SIP is ₹15,436 a month. Raising the SIP 10% every year lets you start at ₹6,168 a month instead. Starting at 25 instead of 30 cuts the flat SIP to ₹14,313 for the same ₹50,000-a-month plan; starting at 45 raises it to ₹57,448."],
      ["Do EPF, PPF and NPS count towards my retirement savings?", "Yes. Add your EPF, PPF, NPS and retirement-earmarked mutual fund balances to savings so far. Leave out your home and your emergency fund. Remember that part of the NPS corpus has to be used to buy an annuity, which pays a taxable pension — see the NPS calculator for the split."],
      ["What return should I assume after retirement?", "After retirement most people move to a safer mix of debt and some equity, so returns are lower. 7–8% is a common planning assumption. What matters most is the real return: an 8% return with 6% inflation leaves only about 1.9% of real growth, which is why the corpus needs to be so large. At 7% the headline plan needs ₹76.8 lakh more."],
      ["What inflation rate should I use?", "6% is the long-run Indian figure used across the published calculators. The RBI's inflation target is 4% within a band of 2% to 6% (renewed for 1 April 2026 to 31 March 2031). Medical costs have often risen faster than headline inflation, so a separate health cover — and a higher inflation figure for medical spending — are common ways to plan for it."],
      ["What if I retire at 65 instead of 60?", "Retiring five years later changes the result twice: five more years of saving and five fewer years of withdrawing. For the headline plan the SIP falls from ₹15,436 to ₹7,894 a month — even though the corpus itself is larger (₹7,76,75,905), because the expenses have five more years to inflate."],
      ["Is this calculator investment advice?", "No. It is an educational estimate: the corpus and SIP follow from the assumptions you enter, and the returns are long-term averages, not promises. Tax is not modelled, and no pension, rent or other income in retirement is assumed. Change the assumptions to match your own plan, re-run the numbers every year, and talk to a SEBI-registered adviser before acting on them."]
    ]
