    goalTitle: "What starting SIP does each goal need",
    goalNote: "The starting monthly SIP each target needs in 10 years at an assumed 12% a year with a 10% yearly step-up. The starting SIP is rounded up to the next rupee, so the value lands just above the target.",
    goalConclusion: "A ₹1 crore goal needs ₹29,636 a month to start — not ₹30,000, and not a flat ₹19,819 a month, because the step-up starts lower and rises. Doubling the goal doubles the starting SIP: the maths is linear in the amount.",
    faq: [
      ["What is a step-up SIP?", "A step-up SIP — also called a top-up SIP — raises the monthly amount by a fixed percentage every year, usually in line with a salary rise. Starting at ₹5,000 a month and raising it 10% every year makes ₹16,87,163 in 10 years at 12%, on ₹9,56,245 invested — against ₹11,61,695 for a flat ₹5,000 SIP. The extra ₹5,25,468 comes from investing more as your income grows, not from a higher return. Most fund houses let you set the step-up when you start the SIP."],
      ["Why does another step-up SIP calculator show a different figure?", "Mostly the instalment timing. This calculator counts each instalment from the start of its month — the convention Groww, ET Money, ClearTax and most Indian fund-house calculators use — which adds one month of growth to every instalment. A calculator that counts instalments from the end of the month shows ₹16,70,459 for the same ₹5,000 a month rising 10% a year at 12% over 10 years, ₹16,704 less. A smaller difference can also come from rounding the monthly SIP each year: this calculator keeps the step-up unrounded inside the calculation and rounds only for display. Check the convention before comparing two calculators."],
      ["Step-up SIP or a bigger flat SIP — which is better?", "They reach the same value by different routes. A ₹10,000-a-month SIP rising 10% a year makes ₹33,74,326 in 10 years at 12%; a flat SIP needs about ₹14,524 a month to reach the same value. The flat SIP costs more in the early years, when your salary is lower; the step-up starts at ₹10,000 and rises with your income, so it is easier to keep paying. If your income will not rise, a flat SIP you can sustain beats a step-up you abandon — the plan you keep is the one that works."],
      ["What step-up percentage should I choose?", "Match it to the rise you actually expect in your income. 10% a year is the common choice for a salaried investor whose pay rises about that fast; 5% is the cautious choice if your rises are smaller or uncertain. Do not pick 15% or 25% unless your income really grows that fast — a step-up you cannot afford in year five is worse than a smaller one you keep for ten years. You can also start at 0% — a flat SIP — and add the step-up later."],
      ["Can I change or stop the step-up?", "Yes. Most fund houses let you modify or cancel the top-up without stopping the SIP itself: the monthly amount stays at its last level, or you set a new step-up. The units you already bought stay invested and keep growing or falling with the market; only the future instalments change. If you pause or stop the SIP entirely, there is no penalty for stopping itself — but redeeming units within a year of buying them may attract an exit load, usually 1% on equity funds, and the gains are taxed as short-term gains."],
      ["How are step-up SIP gains taxed?", "The figures on this page are before tax. On equity funds — at least 65% in Indian shares — gains on units held up to 12 months are taxed at 20% as short-term gains; after 12 months, 12.5% on your total long-term gains above ₹1.25 lakh in a year, under the Finance Act 2024 (from 23 July 2024), plus 4% cess. Debt funds bought from 1 April 2023 are taxed at your income-tax slab rate. Each instalment's units count from the month you bought them, so a step-up SIP's gains are a mix of short- and long-term. Confirm the figures with your Chartered Accountant before you file."],
      ["Does a step-up SIP beat inflation better than a flat SIP?", "Yes, in the sense that matters: the amount you invest rises, so the later instalments keep pace with prices instead of shrinking in real terms. A flat ₹5,000 a month buys less every year; a ₹5,000 SIP rising 10% a year is ₹11,790 a month by year 10. But the step-up does not change the return — the value is still market-linked, and it can end below what you invested over short periods. The step-up protects the amount you invest; only time and the market decide what it becomes."],
      ["What return should I assume?", "For long-term equity planning in India, 10–12% a year is the range most planners use — roughly the long-run record of diversified Indian equity, before tax and expenses. For debt funds, 6–7% is the usual planning range. Never assume the best year a fund ever had, and never use a past return as a promise: use the returns table on this page to see how much the assumption moves the outcome, then pick the lower end for a plan you must be able to keep."],
      ["Does this calculator send my figures anywhere?", "No. The starting amount, step-up, return and tenure stay in your browser — the calculation runs entirely on this page, nothing is sent to a server, and reloading the page clears your recent checks. This tool is a planning estimate, not investment advice; for advice on which funds suit you, talk to a SEBI-registered investment adviser. Mutual fund investments are subject to market risk; read the scheme documents before investing."]
    ]
