    indicativeTitle: "Indicative bike loan interest rates",
    indicativeNote: "A 2026 snapshot of indicative rates across leading Indian banks and NBFCs, compiled from lender disclosures. Use them as a starting point in the calculator — your actual rate depends on your profile, credit score, the bike and the lender. Public-sector banks typically offer 8.5%–12%, private banks 10%–15% and NBFCs 12%–24%.",
    faq: [
      ["How is bike loan EMI calculated?", "With the reducing-balance formula EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1), where P is the loan amount — the bike's on-road price minus your down payment — r is the monthly interest rate (annual rate ÷ 12 ÷ 100) and n is the number of monthly instalments. On a ₹1,20,000 bike with ₹20,000 down, P is ₹1,00,000, and at 12% over 3 years (36 months) the EMI is ₹3,321 a month."],
      ["How does the down payment change my EMI?", "It shrinks the loan rupee for rupee, so the EMI and the total interest both fall. On a ₹1,50,000 bike at 12% over 3 years, no down payment means borrowing ₹1,50,000: an EMI of ₹4,982 a month and ₹29,357 of interest. Pay ₹45,000 — 30% — and you borrow ₹1,05,000: the EMI is ₹3,488 a month and the interest ₹20,550, saving ₹8,807. Most lenders fund 85% to 90% of the on-road price, so expect to pay at least 10% to 15% upfront."],
      ["What is the EMI on a ₹1 lakh bike loan?", "It depends on the rate and the tenure. Borrow ₹1,00,000 at 12% over 3 years and the EMI is ₹3,321 a month (₹1,19,572 in all). Borrow ₹80,000 of it — a ₹1,00,000 bike with ₹20,000 down — at 14% over 2 years and the EMI is ₹3,841 a month (₹92,185 in all). At 10.5% over 3 years, a ₹60,000 loan costs ₹1,950 a month."],
      ["What is a good interest rate for a bike loan?", "Two-wheeler loan rates in India typically run from 9% to 18% a year. Public-sector banks usually offer the lowest rates, around 8.5% to 12%; private banks run about 10% to 15%; NBFCs about 12% to 24%; and manufacturer finance arms about 9% to 16%. A rate above 13% is high for a borrower with a strong credit score — a CIBIL score above 750 generally qualifies for the lower rates. The indicative table on this page, for November 2026, runs from HDFC Bank at 10.50%–16.00% to Hero FinCorp at 11.00%–18.00%."],
      ["What is the difference between a flat rate and a reducing-balance rate?", "A flat rate charges interest on the original loan amount for the whole tenure, so it costs far more than the same percentage on a reducing balance. A flat 8% on ₹1,00,000 over 3 years charges ₹24,000 of interest — the same as a reducing-balance rate of about 14.5%. This calculator uses reducing balance, the standard for EMI loans in India. If a dealer's quote looks unusually cheap, check whether it is flat or reducing before comparing it with anything else."],
      ["Can I prepay my bike loan without charges?", "On a floating-rate loan, yes — under the RBI (Pre-payment Charges on Loans) Directions, 2025, no pre-payment or foreclosure charge may be levied on a floating-rate loan to an individual for a non-business purpose, sanctioned or renewed on or after 1 January 2026, with no minimum lock-in. But most two-wheeler loans in India are fixed-rate, and fixed-rate loans follow the lender's own policy — many charge 2% to 5% of the outstanding principal, and some bar prepayment in the opening months. Check the foreclosure terms in your Key Facts Statement before you prepay. The RBI directions page itself could not be read directly while this page was prepared, so this wording follows three independent faithful reproductions of the directions — see the sources below."],
      ["Is there any tax benefit on a bike loan?", "Not if the bike is for personal use. The Income Tax Act gives no deduction on the interest you pay on a two-wheeler loan for a personal commute — unlike a home loan, there is no section for it. Two exceptions exist: if you are self-employed and the bike is genuinely used for business, the interest can be claimed as a business expense; and if you bought an electric two-wheeler with a loan sanctioned between 1 April 2019 and 31 March 2023, section 80EEB lets an individual deduct up to ₹1.5 lakh of interest — but that window has closed for new loans, so a bike loan taken in 2026 carries no tax benefit on its interest. The figures in this calculator are before any tax effect."],
      ["How long should I take to repay a bike loan?", "As short as your budget allows. On a ₹1,00,000 loan at 12%, 1 year costs ₹8,885 a month and ₹6,619 of interest; 3 years costs ₹3,321 a month and ₹19,572 of interest; 5 years costs ₹2,224 a month but ₹33,467 of interest — ₹13,895 more than the 3-year loan. Most lenders offer 12 to 60 months, and a bike loses value fast, so a 5-year loan can leave you owing more than the bike is worth. Pick the shortest tenure whose EMI fits your monthly budget."],
      ["Does this calculator send my figures anywhere?", "No. Your bike price, down payment, rate and tenure stay in your browser — the calculation runs entirely on this page, nothing is sent to a server, and reloading the page clears your recent checks. This tool is an estimate, not financial advice: your lender sets the actual rate, and the figures leave out the processing fee, insurance and registration."]
    ]
