    overviewTitle: "What does a credit card EMI calculator do?",
    overview: "It works out what converting a credit card purchase into monthly instalments really costs — not just the EMI, but the 18% GST on the interest and on the processing fee, which many calculators leave out. A ₹50,000 purchase at 14% a year over 12 months, with a ₹199 processing fee, has an EMI of ₹4,489 a month — but the interest is ₹3,872, the GST on it is ₹697, and the fee with its GST is ₹235, so you pay ₹54,804 in all: ₹4,804 more than the cash price, 9.6% extra. And the EMI is not what leaves your account each month: the GST on that month's interest is charged on top, so the first month's outgo is ₹4,594, not ₹4,489.",
    inputNote: "Enter the purchase amount you want to convert — from ₹2,500 to ₹5,00,000 — and the annual interest rate from your issuer's EMI offer, from 0% to 36% a year. Pick a tenure of 3, 6, 9, 12, 18 or 24 months, add the processing fee in rupees (₹0 if it is waived), and leave the 18% GST switch on unless your issuer tells you GST does not apply.",
    modelNote: "The EMI uses the standard reducing-balance formula: EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1), where P is the purchase amount, r is the monthly interest rate (annual rate ÷ 12 ÷ 100) and n is the tenure in months. The total EMI repayment comes from the unrounded EMI — total = EMI × n, rounded to the nearest rupee — so the rounded EMI times the tenure can differ from the total by a few rupees. GST at 18% applies to the interest and to the processing fee, never to the principal: entry 27 of Notification No. 12/2017–Central Tax (Rate) exempts interest on loans and advances but expressly excludes interest involved in credit card services. The GST on the interest is charged on top of the EMI each month, so the monthly outgo is the EMI plus that month's GST on interest.",
