    modelNote: "BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age in years) + 5 for men, and − 161 instead of + 5 for women. That is the Mifflin-St Jeor equation (1990). TDEE = BMR × an activity multiplier: 1.2 for sedentary, 1.375 for lightly active, 1.55 for moderately active, 1.725 for very active and 1.9 for extra active. The weight-loss target is TDEE − 500 kcal, floored at the BMR — when 500 below the TDEE would fall under the BMR, the target is the BMR itself and the page says so. The weight-gain target is TDEE + 300 kcal. Weekly figures are the daily figure × 7, monthly figures × 30. The BMI shown uses the same WHO categories as the site's BMI calculator. Harris-Benedict (revised 1984) is shown only for comparison: it runs about 5% higher than Mifflin-St Jeor and is no longer the recommended formula.",
