    notice: "XIRR annualises short periods aggressively — a 5% gain in one month reads as about 80% a year. It is most meaningful over a year or more, and it ignores tax, charges and exit load. Past performance is not a guarantee.",
    overviewTitle: "What does an XIRR calculator do?",
    overview: "It finds the single yearly rate that makes the present value of all your dated cash flows add up to zero. On the sample, ₹1,70,000 went in across five investments, ₹20,000 came out once, and the holding is worth ₹2,10,000 — the absolute return is 35.29%, but the money was invested at different times over 4.54 years, so the annualised XIRR of 10.16% a year is the figure that compares with anything else. The same flows in an FD at 7% would be worth ₹1,89,548, so this portfolio is ahead by ₹20,452 before tax. And when there is only one investment and one value, XIRR is the same as CAGR — ₹1,00,000 growing to ₹1,33,100 over exactly three 365-day years is 10.00% by either measure.",
    inputNote: "Enter each cash flow with its date: choose Invest for money going in and Withdraw for money coming out, and enter the amount. Add the current value of the holding and the date it is valued on. The calculator works out your XIRR, total invested, gain, absolute return and the FD comparison as you type.",
    modelNote: "XIRR is the rate r that makes Σ Ci ÷ (1 + r)^((di − d0) ÷ 365) = 0, where Ci is each cash flow — negative when you invest, positive when you receive — di its date and d0 the first date. There is no closed form, so the calculator solves for r with Newton–Raphson from a 0.1 guess and falls back to bisection inside the bracket −0.999999 to 10. Total invested is the sum of the money put in; total withdrawn is the sum taken out; net invested is invested minus withdrawn; total gain is current value plus withdrawn minus invested; absolute return is total gain ÷ total invested. The FD comparison grows every flow except the final value at 7% a year to the valuation date. Years held is (valuation date − first date) ÷ 365. For a single lumpsum, XIRR equals CAGR when the holding period is a whole number of 365-day years; a span containing 29 February gives an XIRR a fraction lower than the calendar-year CAGR.",
