    referenceTitle: "XIRR against CAGR on single lumpsums",
    referenceNote: "One investment, one value — the only case where CAGR applies. XIRR equals the calendar-year CAGR when the span is a whole number of 365-day years; a span containing 29 February gives an XIRR a fraction lower.",
    referenceConclusion: "The first row is the exact match: ₹1,00,000 to ₹1,33,100 over 1,095 days is 10.00% by both measures. Every other row carries leap days — 731 or 1,827 days — and the XIRR sits 0.01–0.02 points below the CAGR. The gap is the calendar, not an error.",
    faq: [
      [
        "What is XIRR?",
        "XIRR (extended internal rate of return) is the single yearly rate that makes the present value of all your cash flows add up to zero, using the actual date of each one. It is the standard way to measure returns on SIPs, top-ups and partial withdrawals, because a simple gain ÷ invested figure ignores when each rupee went in. On the sample it is 10.16% a year, against an absolute return of 35.29%."
      ],
      [
        "How do I calculate XIRR in Excel?",
        "List investments as negative amounts and withdrawals or the current value as positive amounts in one column, their dates in another, then use =XIRR(values, dates). This calculator uses the same 365-day convention and gives the same answer — Microsoft's own example gives 37.34% in both. In Google Sheets the same function works."
      ],
      [
        "What is the difference between XIRR and CAGR?",
        "CAGR measures growth between one start value and one end value, so it only fits a single lumpsum with no other cash flows. XIRR handles many cash flows on different dates, which is what a SIP is. For a single lumpsum held throughout, the two are the same — ₹1,00,000 growing to ₹1,33,100 over exactly three 365-day years is 10.00% by either measure. A span containing 29 February makes the XIRR a fraction lower than the calendar-year CAGR."
      ],
      [
        "What is the difference between XIRR and absolute return?",
        "Absolute return is total gain ÷ total invested, with no regard to time: 35.29% on the sample. XIRR spreads that gain over how long each instalment was invested, so it is 10.16% a year. In a SIP the last instalments have been invested for only a short time, so the same gain over a shorter average period means a higher annualised XIRR."
      ],
      [
        "What is a good XIRR for a SIP in India?",
        "Judge it against the right yardstick, over the same dates. fundulator.in puts it this way: below 6% is poor (losing to inflation), 6–8% is comparable to an FD or PPF, 8–12% is a decent average equity SIP, 12–15% is good for a consistent large-cap fund, 15–20% is excellent, and above 20% is exceptional — but verify it, because a short period can inflate it. The Nifty 50 SIP XIRR over 10+ years has historically been about 12–14%."
      ],
      [
        "Can XIRR be negative?",
        "Yes. If the current value plus withdrawals is less than the total invested, the XIRR is negative — the annual rate at which the money lost value. A ₹1,00,000 investment worth ₹80,000 after three years has an XIRR of −7.16% a year. A negative XIRR is a correct answer, not an error."
      ],
      [
        "Why does the calculator say there is no XIRR?",
        "XIRR needs at least one money-out and one money-in entry on different dates. If all amounts have the same sign, if every flow falls on the same date, or if the flows cannot balance at any rate — for example a withdrawal first, then investments, then a value that never covers them — there is no answer, and the calculator says so instead of inventing one."
      ],
      [
        "Why is my SIP XIRR so high after only a few months?",
        "XIRR annualises whatever happened, however short the period. A 5% gain in one month reads as about 80% a year, and a small market move in a three-month-old SIP can show 50% or more. The figure becomes meaningful over a year or more — fundulator.in warns that short-duration SIPs show annualised rates that look extreme for small actual gains."
      ],
      [
        "Does XIRR account for tax, charges and exit load?",
        "No. XIRR works on the cash flows you enter: the current value already reflects the fund's expenses through the NAV, but exit load, transaction charges and tax on redemption are not in the figure unless you enter the value net of them. The FD comparison is before tax too."
      ],
      [
        "Does this calculator send my figures anywhere?",
        "No. Your cash flows and current value stay in your browser — the calculation runs entirely on this page, nothing is sent to a server, and reloading the page clears your entries. Past performance is not a guarantee of future returns."
      ]
    ]
