    overview: "It works out how much tax the payer must withhold before making a payment in India, and how much the payee actually receives. On the sample, a company paying a consultant ₹1,00,000 in professional fees during the year crosses the ₹50,000 Section 194J threshold, so TDS at 10% is ₹10,000 and the consultant receives ₹90,000 — claiming the ₹10,000 as credit in the income tax return. The same ₹1,00,000 as a single lottery win would lose ₹30,000, and as a purchase of goods it would lose nothing at all, because the ₹50 lakh threshold is nowhere near crossed. The payment type decides everything: the rate, the threshold and whether the threshold is tested per year, per month or per transaction.",
    inputNote: "Pick the payment type first — the rate and threshold for that type appear below the selector. Enter the amount the row asks for: the year's total for most types, the monthly rent for rent, a single win for winnings, the consideration for a property purchase. Then say whether the payee furnished a PAN, and tick the senior-citizen box for bank interest where it applies. The TDS, the net amount and the working appear as you type.",
    modelNote: "TDS = taxable base × section rate. For most sections the taxable base is the whole amount, once it crosses the threshold — a ₹1,00,000 professional fee above the ₹50,000 limit is taxed on the full ₹1,00,000, not just the ₹50,000 above the limit. Two sections are the exception: purchase of goods (194Q) and cash withdrawals (194N) are taxed only on the amount above the threshold. Salary has no fixed rate: the employer estimates the year's tax on the new-regime slabs — ₹75,000 standard deduction, the Section 87A rebate up to ₹60,000 for taxable income up to ₹12,00,000 with marginal relief just above it, surcharge above ₹50 lakh and 4% health and education cess — and deducts it in twelve equal monthly instalments. Without a PAN, Section 397(2) requires the higher of the section rate and 20% (5% for purchase of goods and e-commerce payments). No surcharge or cess is added to TDS on payments to residents.",
