    rateTitle: "The full TDS rate chart, FY 2026-27",
    rateNote: "Every rate, threshold and section reference below is from the CBDT TDS rate chart for tax year 2026-27 and the Income-tax Act, 2025 (Sections 392, 393 and 397, as amended by the Finance Act, 2026). Rates are for payments to residents with a valid PAN. The payment code is the number used on the challan and in the TDS return; rows marked Form 141 are paid through the challan-cum-statement instead.",
    rateConclusion: "Five rows are in the table only, not in the calculator: the specified senior citizen's bank deduction and the four trust and fund distributions, because their amounts depend on the person's full income or on the trust's own statement. Everything else on this page can be worked out with the calculator above.",
    faq: [
      [
        "What is TDS?",
        "TDS (tax deducted at source) is the tax the payer withholds before making a payment in India — salary, interest, rent, professional fees, dividends and more — and deposits with the government against the payee's PAN. The payee claims it as credit when filing the income tax return, so it is a withholding, not the final tax: any excess comes back as a refund. On the sample here, ₹1,00,000 of professional fees loses ₹10,000 to TDS and the consultant receives ₹90,000."
      ],
      [
        "How is TDS calculated?",
        "TDS = payment × section rate, once the payment crosses the section's threshold. For most sections the whole amount is taxed once the threshold is crossed — a ₹1,00,000 professional fee above the ₹50,000 limit is taxed on the full ₹1,00,000, not just the ₹50,000 above the limit. Two sections are the exception: purchase of goods (194Q) and cash withdrawals (194N) are taxed only on the amount above the threshold. Salary has no fixed rate: the employer estimates the year's tax on the new-regime slabs and deducts it in twelve equal monthly instalments."
      ],
      [
        "What changed on 1 April 2026?",
        "The Income-tax Act, 2025 replaced the 1961 Act. Salary TDS moved from Section 192 to Section 392, and almost every other TDS provision moved into the tables of Section 393, with the no-PAN rule in Section 397(2). The rates and thresholds are unchanged from FY 2025-26, so a 194J fee is still 10% above ₹50,000 — only the section number on the challan and the return changed. TDS returns now use numeric payment codes (1001 to 1067) instead of the old section numbers, and the forms were renumbered too: Form 24Q is now Form 138, Form 26Q is Form 140, Form 16A is Form 131 and Form 26AS is Form 168."
      ],
      [
        "Is surcharge or cess added to TDS?",
        "No — not on payments to residents under the sections on this page. The flat section rate is the final deduction: 10% on professional fees means ₹10,000 on ₹1,00,000, not ₹10,400. Salary is the exception, because its TDS is the full slab-based tax, which includes 4% health and education cess (and surcharge above ₹50 lakh of taxable income). Some calculators add 4% cess to every section — that is wrong for resident payments, and this calculator does not do it."
      ],
      [
        "What is the TDS rate when the payee has no PAN?",
        "Section 397(2) requires the higher of the section rate, the rate in force and 20%. In practice that means 20% for most sections: professional fees jump from 10% to 20%, FD interest from 10% to 20%, and a contractor from 1% to 20%. Two sections are the exception: purchase of goods (194Q) and e-commerce payments (194O) jump to 5%, not 20%. Winnings stay at 30%, which is already higher than 20%. For a premature EPF withdrawal the general 20% rule applies; some sites still quote the old 34.608% maximum-marginal-rate figure, but the provision that produced it was omitted from 1 April 2023."
      ],
      [
        "How is TDS on salary calculated?",
        "Your employer estimates the tax for the full year on the new regime by default — the ₹75,000 standard deduction, the Section 87A rebate of up to ₹60,000 where taxable income is ₹12,00,000 or less (with marginal relief just above that limit), surcharge above ₹50 lakh and 4% cess — and deducts it in twelve equal monthly instalments. That is why a salary of up to ₹12,75,000 attracts no TDS at all: ₹12,75,000 minus the ₹75,000 standard deduction is exactly ₹12,00,000, and the rebate cancels the ₹60,000 slab tax. On ₹15,00,000 the tax is ₹97,500 a year, or ₹8,125 a month. The old regime can be chosen instead, but the employer needs the declaration and the proofs."
      ],
      [
        "What is the TDS on FD interest?",
        "10%, once the interest from one bank exceeds ₹50,000 in the year (₹1,00,000 for senior citizens aged 60 and above). Below that threshold, no TDS is deducted. On ₹60,000 of interest the TDS is ₹6,000 and the depositor receives ₹54,000. The threshold is per bank, per year, across all its branches and deposits — splitting deposits across branches of the same bank does not avoid it. If your total income is below the taxable limit, Form 121 (which replaced Forms 15G and 15H) tells the bank not to deduct at all."
      ],
      [
        "What is the TDS on rent?",
        "It depends on who is paying. A business or a specified person paying rent for land or a building deducts 10% once the rent exceeds ₹50,000 a month; rent for plant or machinery is 2%. An individual or HUF who is not required to get their accounts audited deducts 2% under Section 194IB, also above ₹50,000 a month, through the challan-cum-statement. On ₹60,000 a month for office space the TDS is ₹6,000 a month — ₹72,000 over the year. The rate was 5% for individuals before Finance Act 2025 cut it to 2%."
      ],
      [
        "When must TDS be deposited, and which forms are used?",
        "TDS deducted in a month must be deposited by the 7th of the following month (30 April for March deductions). The quarterly returns are Form 138 for salary and Form 140 for other payments to residents, and the TDS certificate is Form 131 (Form 130 for salary). The deductee sees every deduction in Form 168 (earlier Form 26AS) and the Annual Information Statement on the e-filing portal. Late deduction attracts interest at 1% a month, and late deposit at 1.5% a month."
      ],
      [
        "Does this calculator send my figures anywhere?",
        "No. Your payment type, amount and PAN choice stay in your browser — the calculation runs entirely on this page, nothing is sent to a server, and reloading the page clears your entries. The figures are an estimate for the payment types listed: check the section reference against the Income-tax Act, 2025 or a chartered accountant for complex or high-value transactions, non-resident payees and DTAA rates."
      ]
    ]
