    "overviewTitle": "What does an ovulation calculator do?",
    "overview": "It counts forward from the first day of your last period. Ovulation usually happens about 14 days before your next period, so for a regular 28-day cycle it falls on day 14. On the sample — a last period on 1 November 2026 with a 28-day cycle — ovulation is Sunday 15 November 2026, and the fertile window runs Tuesday 10 November 2026 to Sunday 15 November 2026: the five days before ovulation plus ovulation day itself, six days in all. The calculator also gives your next period (Sunday 29 November 2026), the earliest day to take a pregnancy test (the same day), and the due date if you conceive this cycle (Sunday 8 August 2027).",
    "inputNote": "Start with the first day of your last period and your usual cycle length — 28 days is the default, but enter your real length if it is longer or shorter. The luteal phase — the days from ovulation to your next period — is 14 days for most people; change it only if you know yours is different. The sample — a last period on 1 November 2026 — is filled in.",
    "modelNote": "The calendar method: ovulation day = the first day of the last period + (cycle length − luteal phase) days. For a 28-day cycle with a 14-day luteal phase that is day 14. The fertile window is the 5 days before ovulation plus ovulation day itself — six days — because sperm can survive up to 5 days while the egg lives only 12–24 hours after release. The next period is the last period + cycle length days. The due date if conception happens this cycle is ovulation + 266 days (38 weeks from conception), and the earliest pregnancy-test day is the expected next period.",
