    "checklistTitle": "Before you act on the date",
    "checklist": [
      "Is the first day of your last period certain? If you are guessing at the month, the date is a guess too — track a full cycle first.",
      "Is your cycle regular? If it varies by more than a few days, use the average of your last three cycles — or an ovulation test.",
      "Did you enter your real cycle length? A 35-day cycle puts ovulation a week later than the 28-day default.",
      "Is your luteal phase really 14 days? Change it only if you know yours — from temperature charts or a doctor — is different.",
      "Are you counting six fertile days, not seven? The window ends on ovulation day; the day after is not fertile.",
      "Do you know the best days to try? The two days before ovulation give the highest chance per cycle.",
      "Are you testing at the right time? The earliest test day is your expected next period — a test before then can miss a pregnancy.",
      "Are you using this to avoid pregnancy? Stop — the calendar method is not contraception. Use a reliable method instead.",
      "Is this page your only source? It is an estimate, not medical advice. Confirm ovulation with a test, and see a doctor if cycles are irregular or conception is not happening."
    ],
    "faq": [
      [
        "When do I ovulate?",
        "About 14 days before your next period. For a regular 28-day cycle that is day 14. On the sample — a last period on 1 November 2026 with a 28-day cycle — ovulation is Sunday 15 November 2026. A longer cycle means later ovulation: day 21 for a 35-day cycle."
      ],
      [
        "What is the fertile window — and why six days, not seven?",
        "The fertile window is the five days before ovulation plus ovulation day itself — six days. Sperm can survive up to five days inside the body, so intercourse in the days before ovulation can still lead to conception; the egg lives only 12–24 hours after release, so the day after ovulation is not fertile. Several calculators publish a seven-day window that adds the day after ovulation — this page does not follow that variant, because that day is not fertile. The medical standard is six days (Wilcox et al., 1995; NHS; ACOG)."
      ],
      [
        "How is my ovulation date calculated?",
        "By the calendar method: ovulation day = the first day of your last period + (cycle length − luteal phase) days. With the usual 14-day luteal phase, a 28-day cycle gives day 14 and a 32-day cycle gives day 18. The fertile window is the five days before that date plus the date itself, the next period is the last period + cycle length days, and the due date if you conceive this cycle is ovulation + 266 days."
      ],
      [
        "My cycle is 35 days, not 28. Does that change my ovulation date?",
        "Yes — by a week. A longer cycle means later ovulation, so the whole window moves later by the difference. A last period on 1 March 2026 with a 35-day cycle gives Sunday 22 March 2026 instead of Sunday 15 March 2026, with a fertile window of 17 to 22 March. Enter your real cycle length."
      ],
      [
        "What is the luteal phase, and why can I change it?",
        "The luteal phase is the days from ovulation to your next period — 14 days for most people, but it varies from about 10 to 16 days. The calculator lets you change it because it is the part of the cycle that is most stable for each person: if you know yours is 12 days, ovulation falls two days earlier than the default. Leave it at 14 unless you know yours is different."
      ],
      [
        "When is the best time to try to conceive?",
        "The two days before ovulation give the highest chance per cycle, followed by ovulation day itself. On the sample the best days are Friday 13 November 2026 and Saturday 14 November 2026, with ovulation on Sunday 15 November 2026. Intercourse in the fertile window, every day or every other day, covers the window."
      ],
      [
        "When can I take a pregnancy test?",
        "On the day your next period is due — that is the earliest test day this calculator gives. On the sample that is Sunday 29 November 2026. A test taken earlier can miss a pregnancy, because the hormone it detects may not be high enough yet. If the test is negative and your period still does not come, test again a few days later."
      ],
      [
        "Can I use this calculator as contraception?",
        "No. The calendar method has a high failure rate as birth control: ovulation can come early or late in any cycle, and sperm survive up to five days. This calculator is for planning a pregnancy, not preventing one. Use a reliable contraceptive method instead."
      ],
      [
        "How accurate is the ovulation date?",
        "It is an estimate from averages, and it is only as regular as your cycles. It is unreliable for irregular cycles, PCOS, thyroid conditions and recent contraceptive use. An ovulation (LH) test, which detects the hormone surge 24–36 hours before ovulation, is the most reliable home check for timing — use the calendar to plan the week and the test to pin down the day."
      ],
      [
        "Is this calculator medical advice?",
        "No. It is an estimate for planning, worked out from the dates you enter. It cannot know when you ovulated, and it cannot replace a test, a scan or a doctor. Confirm ovulation with a test, and see a doctor if your cycles are irregular, if ovulation does not show up, or if conception is not happening after a year of trying (six months if you are over 35)."
      ]
    ]
