    "overviewTitle": "What does a stamp duty calculator do?",
    "overview": "It works out the two government charges on buying a property, and what they add up to. On the sample — a ₹50,00,000 flat in Mumbai bought by a man — the stamp duty is ₹3,00,000 (5% base duty plus 1% metro cess), the registration fee is ₹30,000 (1%, capped), and the total is ₹3,30,000. The same flat in Delhi bought by a woman costs ₹2,50,100 in all, and in Tamil Nadu it costs ₹5,50,000 — the state decides the rate, so there is no single all-India figure. The Home Loan Eligibility Calculator shows stamp duty and registration only as a range (about ₹3,67,000–₹4,89,000 on its sample price); this tool computes the exact duty and fee for your state and buyer category. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "inputNote": "Pick the state first, then who is buying — a man, a woman, or a man and a woman jointly — then enter the property value. Enter the higher of the agreement value and the circle rate for the property: if the circle rate is higher, the duty is charged on the circle rate. The sample — ₹50,00,000 in Mumbai, bought by a man — is filled in.",
    "modelNote": "Stamp duty = property value × the state's base duty rate for the buyer category. On top of the duty, some states add a surcharge: Gujarat adds 40% on the duty (3.5% becomes 4.9%), Karnataka adds 12% on the duty (10% cess + 2% surcharge), Maharashtra adds 1% of the value as metro cess (2% in Pune, Thane and Nagpur, where 1% LBT also applies), and Telangana adds 1.5% of the value as transfer duty. Registration fee = property value × the state's registration rate, subject to the state's cap — Maharashtra caps it at ₹30,000 — and Delhi adds a ₹100 pasting fee. The total is the stamp duty plus the registration fee. Money is rounded to paise; the total percentage to one decimal. Karnataka's base duty is a slab on the value (2% to ₹20 lakh, 3% to ₹45 lakh, 5% above), applied to the whole value — strictly, the 2% and 3% slabs are for the first sale of a flat, but the competing calculators apply them generally, and so does this tool.",
