    "overviewTitle": "What does a fuel cost calculator do?",
    "overview": "It turns three figures — the distance, your vehicle's mileage and the fuel price — into the answer that matters before a trip: how much fuel you will burn and what it will cost. On the sample — Delhi to Jaipur, 280 km, at 15 km/l and ₹95 a litre — the answer is 18.67 litres and ₹1,773.33 one way, ₹6.33 a km, and ₹3,546.67 for the return leg. The same three figures also give the monthly cost of a daily commute: 40 km a day for 22 days is 880 km a month, 58.67 litres and ₹5,573.33. The Car Loan EMI calculator works out what the car itself costs each month; this tool works out what running it costs. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "inputNote": "Pick a trip or a monthly commute. For a trip, enter the distance in km, the mileage in km per litre (km per kg for CNG), the fuel price and the fuel type, then the trip type and how many people are sharing the cost. For a commute, enter the km you drive each day and the days you drive each month instead. The sample — Delhi to Jaipur, 280 km at 15 km/l and ₹95 a litre, costing ₹1,773.33 — is filled in.",
    "modelNote": "Fuel needed = distance ÷ mileage. Trip cost = fuel needed × price. Cost per km = price ÷ mileage. A round trip doubles the distance, so the return leg costs the same as the way out. Cost per person = the trip cost ÷ passengers. Monthly distance = km per day × days driven per month; monthly fuel = monthly distance ÷ mileage; monthly cost = monthly fuel × price. CNG is priced per kg, never per litre, and its mileage is km per kg. Money and fuel are rounded to two decimals. The price is entered by you — the page never asserts a live price.",
