    "notice": "This is a calculation for planning only, not investment advice. Brokerage, STT, stamp duty and other charges are NOT included: the average here is the price average before charges, so a broker's all-in figure or a contract note can differ slightly. For Indian capital-gains tax, demat sales are matched first-in first-out, not by average — the average is for tracking a position, not for a tax return. Averaging down adds size to a losing position and increases the risk: a lower average is not a lower risk.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a share average calculator do?",
    "overview": "It answers one question: what did I pay on average for this share across my buys? Each purchase counts in proportion to how many shares it bought, so the answer is the total invested divided by the total shares. On the sample — 40 shares at ₹250 and 30 at ₹220 — the total invested is ₹16,600 for 70 shares, so the average is ₹237.14. At today's price of ₹210 the holding is worth ₹14,700, a loss of ₹1,900 (−11.45%), and the price must rise 12.92% to reach the average. The XIRR, CAGR and Lumpsum calculators measure returns over time; this tool answers the single question of what you paid on average for this share across your buys. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "inputNote": "Pick average price or the averaging-down planner. For the average, enter the price and quantity of each purchase — up to five lots — and today's price if you want the profit or loss. For the planner, enter the shares you hold, your current average, the price you would buy at and the average you want. The sample — 40 shares at ₹250 and 30 at ₹220, averaging ₹237.14 — is filled in.",
    "modelNote": "Average price = total invested ÷ total shares, where total invested is the sum over the lots of price × quantity. Current value = total shares × current market price. Profit or loss = current value − total invested, in rupees and as a percentage of the total invested. The rise to the average = (average price − current price) ÷ current price, from the average as shown. Shares to buy for a target average = current quantity × (current average − target) ÷ (target − new price), rounded UP to a whole share — and only when the target lies strictly between the current average and the new price. Money is rounded to paise; shares are whole numbers. Brokerage, STT, stamp duty and other charges are NOT included.",
