    "modelNote": "Encashment = (Basic + DA) ÷ divisor × unused leave days, where the divisor is 30 by default or 26 if your employer's policy says so. The exemption for a non-government employee on retirement or resignation is the least of four amounts: (1) the amount actually received, (2) 10 months' average salary, (3) ₹25,00,000 less exemption already claimed in earlier years, (4) the cash equivalent of leave standing to credit, capped at 30 days per completed year of service and valued at (Basic + DA) ÷ 30 per day — always ÷ 30, even when the payout itself uses ÷ 26. Government employees on retirement are fully exempt with no ceiling and no four-limb test. Encashment during service is fully taxable as salary for everyone. Leave salary paid to legal heirs on the death of an employee is not taxable (CBDT Circular No. 309, 3 July 1981). Money is rounded to paise.",
    "notice": "This is an estimate for planning only, not tax advice. Rules as of November 2026: the ₹25,00,000 ceiling from CBDT Notification No. 31/2023 (24 May 2023, w.e.f. 1 April 2023); the exemption in s.10(10AA) of the Income-tax Act, 1961, carried forward in s.19 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 (from 1 April 2026). The exemption is available under both the old and the new tax regime — only the tax rate on the taxable balance depends on the regime, and this calculator does not work out that tax (see the Income Tax calculator). Confirm the final figure with your payroll team or a chartered accountant. Leave salary paid to legal heirs on the death of an employee is not taxable (CBDT Circular No. 309, 3 July 1981).",
