    "modelNote": "Suggested size = yearly units ÷ 1,460, rounded up to the next 0.25 kW and capped at 10 kW (the residential scheme limit). Generation = system size × 4 units a day × 365 days — 1 kW makes 1,460 units a year. System cost = size × cost per kW + net-meter cost. Subsidy = ₹30,000 per kW for the first 2 kW + ₹18,000 for the third kW, capped at ₹78,000 — or ₹33,000 / ₹19,800, capped at ₹85,800, in special-category states and UTs. Net cost = system cost − subsidy. Yearly savings = units replaced × tariff + surplus units × surplus rate, where the replaced units are the smaller of generation and yearly use. Payback = net cost ÷ yearly savings. 25-year savings = 25 × yearly savings − net cost, with no allowance for panel degradation (about 0.5% a year) or future tariff rises. CO₂ avoided = generation × 0.71 tonnes per MWh (CEA). Money is rounded to paise.",
    "notice": "This is an estimate for planning only, not a quote or engineering advice. Rules as of November 2026: the PM Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana subsidy (Union Cabinet approval, 29 February 2024; MNRE operational guidelines) — ₹30,000 per kW for the first 2 kW and ₹18,000 for the third, capped at ₹78,000 (₹33,000 / ₹19,800, capped at ₹85,800, in special-category states and UTs). The tariff (₹7.00 a unit) and the surplus rate (₹3.00 a unit) are editable assumptions, not state tariffs stated as fact; the cost per kW (₹60,000) is an editable November 2026 benchmark. Apply on the national portal, pmsuryaghar.gov.in, with a registered vendor and DCR panels; the subsidy is paid by direct benefit transfer after inspection and net-meter fitting. Confirm the final figures with your vendor and DISCOM.",
    "overview": "A rooftop solar calculator works out three things: the system size your bill needs, what the PM Surya Ghar subsidy takes off the price, and how fast the system pays for itself. On the sample — a home using 300 units a month, a 2.5 kW system at ₹60,000 a kW, a tariff of ₹7 a unit and a surplus rate of ₹3 — the system costs ₹1,50,000, the subsidy is ₹69,000, the net cost is ₹81,000, it makes 3,650 units a year and saves ₹25,350 a year, paying back in 3.20 years. The EMI calculator works out the scheme loan on the net cost; the Fuel Cost calculator covers the other energy bill most homes watch. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
