    "mistakesTitle": "Common home loan prepayment mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "EMI = balance × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), with r the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12 ÷ 100) and n the remaining months. Each month, interest = balance × r; the EMI pays the interest first and the rest reduces the principal, then the monthly part-prepayment reduces the balance further. A lump sum is applied at the start of the month you name, before that month's interest accrues. The loan closes in the month the balance reaches zero; the final payment is adjusted down and the final month's part-prepayment is only what is still needed. Reduce tenure keeps the EMI and finishes early; reduce EMI recomputes the EMI after each prepayment over the months still left. Interest saved = baseline total interest − new total interest. A prepayment charge, if you enter one, is a % of the prepayments made. Money is rounded to paise.",
    "notice": "Rules as of November 2026: the RBI (Pre-payment Charges on Loans) Directions, 2025 (RBI/2025-26/64, 2 July 2025) bar prepayment and foreclosure charges on floating-rate loans taken by individuals for non-business purposes, for loans sanctioned or renewed from 1 January 2026; earlier rules already barred these charges on floating-rate home loans. A fixed-rate loan may carry a charge — enter the % from your sanction letter and the calculator deducts it from the saving. The rate is assumed fixed for the remaining tenure; a floating rate's actual saving differs if rates change. Prepaying cuts future interest, so the Section 24(b) interest deduction falls under the old tax regime — the figures here are before tax. Confirm the final figures with your lender.",
    "overview": "A home loan prepayment calculator works out what happens when you pay extra towards your home loan — every month, as a lump sum, or both. On the sample — ₹50 lakh still owed at 8.5% with 15 years left, ₹5,000 extra a month — the EMI is ₹49,236.98, the loan ends after 150 EMIs instead of 180, the interest falls from ₹38,62,656 to ₹31,33,032, a saving of ₹7,29,624, and you are debt-free 2 years 6 months sooner, in May 2039 instead of November 2041. The Home Loan EMI Calculator works out the EMI itself; the Home Loan Eligibility Calculator works out the most you can borrow. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
