    "modelNote": "Future cost = cost today × (1 + education inflation) ^ years to go, where years to go = age the course starts − child's age now. Savings grown = saved so far × (1 + expected return) ^ years. Gap = future cost − savings grown, floored at 0. Monthly SIP (flat) = gap ÷ what ₹1 a month grows to by then, with monthly compounding and beginning-of-month payments. Step-up SIP = the first-year monthly SIP when the SIP rises by the step-up % every year. Lumpsum today = gap ÷ (1 + expected return) ^ years. Cost in today's money = future cost ÷ 1.06 ^ years (general inflation 6%). Funded % = savings grown ÷ future cost × 100, capped at 100. Money amounts round to the nearest rupee; the default education inflation is 10% and the default return 12%.",
    "notice": "This is an estimate for planning only, not investment advice. Rules as of November 2026: education inflation has no official figure — planners use 8–10% and this calculator defaults to 10%; the return is an assumption, not a forecast. Course costs on this page are rough examples, not fee quotes. For a daughter, Sukanya Samriddhi pays 8.2% a year for October–December 2026, set every quarter by the government. Mutual fund investments are subject to market risks. Check the fees of the colleges you are considering before relying on any figure.",
