    "mistakesTitle": "Common gold-buying mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "Gold value = weight in grams x rate for the purity. The rate for a purity comes from the 24K rate you enter: 22K is 91.6% of it (BIS fineness 916), 18K is 75% (750) and 14K is 58.3% (583). The 24K rate you enter is the rate for 999-fine gold, so 24K gold is valued at the full entered rate — the 999 is the hallmark figure, and the other purities scale from it. If you enter the rate for the chosen purity directly instead, that rate is used as-is. Wastage = weight in grams x wastage% x rate for the purity. Making = the percentage you enter of the gold value, or the flat amount you enter. Bill = gold value + wastage + making + stone charges; GST = 3% of the bill when switched on, split equally into CGST and SGST (CGST is rounded to the rupee and SGST takes the remainder, so the halves can differ by Re.1 on an odd GST). Resale: pure value = weight x rate for the purity; buyback = pure value x (1 - deduction%). Budget: grams = budget / rate for the purity. Each money line is rounded to whole rupees before the lines are added — that rounding is what makes the published examples reproduce exactly. The rate is never fetched live: the pre-filled Rs.13,762 is the 24K rate goldcalculator.net.in showed on 14 November 2026, and you replace it with your jeweller's rate.",
    "notice": "This is an estimate, not financial advice. The rate is yours to enter and it moves every day: the pre-filled 24K rate of Rs.13,762 a gram is the rate goldcalculator.net.in showed on 14 November 2026, a dated example only — replace it with the rate your jeweller quotes. Making charges and wastage vary by shop, the buyback deduction varies by jeweller, and the jeweller's bill is final. GST is 3% on jewellery; 5% applies only to job work, when you give your own gold to a goldsmith. If the amounts are large, check the bill line by line at the counter.",
    "overview": "A gold price calculator turns the weight, the purity and today's rate into the bill you should expect. The gold value is the weight in grams times the rate for that purity — 22K gold is 91.6% pure, so it costs 91.6% of the 24K rate a gram. Then come the charges: wastage (VA), the gold lost while making the piece, charged as a percentage of the weight at the gold rate; making charges, the labour fee, as a percentage of the gold value or a flat amount; and stone charges, if the piece carries stones. GST at 3% applies to the whole bill. On the sample — 20 g of 22K at Rs.11,500 a gram — the gold value is Rs.2,30,000, 12% wastage adds Rs.27,600, GST adds Rs.7,728, and the bill is Rs.2,65,328: a true cost of Rs.13,266 a gram, 15.4% over the gold value. The <a href=\"/gold-loan-calculator/\">Gold Loan EMI calculator</a> works out what you can borrow against the same gold, the <a href=\"/home-loan-emi-calculator/\">Home Loan EMI calculator</a> works out a loan EMI, and the <a href=\"/salary-calculator/\">Salary calculator</a> works out your monthly pay. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
