    "recoveryTitle": "What it takes to recover, out of 100 classes held",
    "rulesNote": "These six rules are the ones that cost real marks when they are missed. The requirement, the per-subject count and the condonation band are the three things to check in your handbook before you trust any number on this page.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The 75% figure is widely attributed to the UGC's 2003 regulations on minimum standards of instruction, Clause 5.8, reported as requiring universities to prescribe a minimum that ordinarily shall not be less than 75% of lectures, tutorials, seminars and practicals. The wording is reported, not a confirmed verbatim quotation — the primary UGC text was not opened here — and since the 2025 UGC regulations there is no universal threshold: each institution sets its own minimum through its own statutory bodies. What matters in practice is your own institution's rule.",
        "rule": "75% is the figure most colleges quote — but it is not a law"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Plenty of colleges use 70% or 80%, and Delhi University requires two-thirds — 66.67% — under Ordinance VII. Some VTU colleges require 85% in each course. BITS Pilani has no minimum attendance rule at all. Set the required field to whatever your own handbook states, and use the preset buttons only as shortcuts.",
        "rule": "Your college's number wins — 70%, 80% or two-thirds"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Good attendance in one subject does not make up for a shortfall in another. The overall figure is the ratio of your total attended to your total held — not the average of your subject percentages: a student at 90% in a ten-class lab and 50% in a forty-lecture course is not at 70% overall, they are at 58%. Check each subject separately.",
        "rule": "Most colleges count it per subject, not just overall"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Laboratory and practical courses often carry their own minimum — sometimes 80% or higher — and a three-hour lab may be weighted as three periods, so one missed lab can cost as much as three missed lectures. If your portal counts periods rather than sessions, enter periods.",
        "rule": "Practicals and labs may be counted separately"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Most universities let students a few points short apply for condonation on medical or duty grounds — commonly in the band from 65% to 75%, often with a fee and a written application with proof. Below that band, most institutions detain the student from the examination. A medical certificate does not automatically grant attendance; your department office decides.",
        "rule": "Condonation is a decision, not an entitlement"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Absence for an activity the institution itself sanctioned — a sports fixture, a conference, an NSS or NCC camp — is credited as attended by many colleges and excluded from the total held by others. The two are not the same, and which one your college does changes your percentage. Enter the counts as your portal records them.",
        "rule": "Duty leave and approved leave are counted by your college"
      }
    ],
