    "lead": "Enter your essential monthly expenses and how many months of cover you want, and see the size your emergency fund should reach, how much of it you already have, and how long it takes to build the rest at your own saving pace. The rule of thumb is 3 to 6 months of essential expenses — more if your income is irregular or people depend on you. On the sample — Rs.50,000 a month, 6 months of cover, Rs.1,00,000 already saved — the target is Rs.3,00,000, the gap is Rs.2,00,000 and your savings cover 2.0 months today. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "meta": "Emergency fund calculator — work out how big your emergency fund should be, how far your savings go already, and how long it takes to close the gap. Sample: Rs.50,000 a month for 6 months is a Rs.3,00,000 target; Rs.1,00,000 saved leaves a Rs.2,00,000 gap and 2.0 months of cover. Free, in your browser.",
    "methodology": "The rules and examples on this page were checked against nivesh.org.in, blogbanking.com, calcmint.co.in, moneymindtool.com and krishnavriddhicapital.com on 14 November 2026. For related tools, the EMI Calculator works out the loan payments that belong in your essential expenses, the SIP Calculator plans the monthly saving once the fund is built, and the PPF Calculator shows what long-term saving looks like after the safety net is in place. The Home Loan EMI Calculator works out a home loan EMI, and the Percentage Calculator works out any percentage the same way. Last updated 14 November 2026.",
