    "mistakesTitle": "Common emergency fund mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "Target = monthly essential expenses × months of cover. Gap = target − current savings, never below zero; if your savings pass the target the gap is 0 and the extra is the surplus. Cover today = current savings ÷ monthly expenses, shown to one decimal place. Progress = current savings ÷ target × 100, rounded to a whole percent. Months to reach the target = gap ÷ monthly saving, rounded up — at Rs.10,000 a month a Rs.2,00,000 gap takes 20 months. Monthly saving needed = gap ÷ months to build it, rounded to the nearest rupee — Rs.2,30,000 over 12 months is Rs.19,167 a month. No interest is counted while the fund is being built: krishnavriddhicapital.com counts none either, while nivesh.org.in, calcmint.co.in and moneymindtool.com project a return, so their timelines are a little shorter. The calculator counts rupees, not advice: where to keep the money and how much cover you need are your decisions, guided by the rules below.",
    "notice": "This is a planning aid, not financial advice. The 3 to 6 month figure is the widely used rule of thumb, not a regulation, and no interest is counted while the fund is being built — the figures are the plain arithmetic of your own numbers. Your real needs depend on your expenses, your job security and who depends on you, so revisit the fund whenever your expenses or income change. Nothing you enter leaves your browser.",
    "overview": "An emergency fund calculator turns two numbers — your essential monthly expenses and how many months you want covered — into the size your safety net should reach, and then measures what you already have against it. The target is expenses times months of cover. On the sample — Rs.50,000 a month for 6 months — the target is Rs.3,00,000. With Rs.1,00,000 already saved the gap is Rs.2,00,000, your savings cover 2.0 months today, and at Rs.10,000 a month the gap closes in 20 months — or Rs.16,667 a month builds it in 12 months. The EMI Calculator on this site works out the loan payments that belong in your essential expenses, the SIP Calculator plans the monthly saving once the fund is built, and the PPF Calculator shows what long-term saving looks like after the safety net is in place — all three run in your browser too. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does an emergency fund calculator do?",
