    "faq": [
      [
        "How is the price of silver jewellery calculated?",
        "Silver value = weight in grams × rate for the purity, where the rate for a purity is the 999 rate × fineness ÷ 1,000. Add wastage (weight × wastage% × rate), making charges (a percentage of the silver value, or a flat Rs. per gram) and any stone charges, then GST at 3% on the whole bill. On the sample — 10 g of 999 at Rs.195 a gram, 10% making — that is Rs.1,950 + Rs.195 + Rs.64 GST = Rs.2,209."
      ],
      [
        "What is the GST on silver jewellery — 3% or 5% on making?",
        "3% on the whole bill. The CBIC sectoral FAQ on gems and jewellery states that GST is 3% of the total transaction value of jewellery, whether the making charge is shown separately or not. The 5% rate applies to job work — when a jeweller gives metal to a karigar — not to the retail sale of finished jewellery. Several calculators split it as 3% on the metal plus 5% on the making; that gives a higher total and does not match the CBIC retail position."
      ],
      [
        "What is the difference between 999, 925, 916 and 900 silver?",
        "999 is fine silver, 99.9% pure — the standard for coins, bars and investment. 925 is sterling silver, 92.5% silver with 7.5% other metals for strength — the standard for most jewellery. 916 is 91.6% pure, used for utensils and idols. 900 is coin silver, 90% pure, the old standard for coins. At a 999 rate of Rs.195 a gram, the rates are Rs.195.00, Rs.180.38, Rs.178.62 and Rs.175.50 a gram."
      ],
      [
        "What is today's silver rate used here?",
        "The pre-filled 999 rate is Rs.195 a gram on 14 November 2026 — the retail cluster of that day (niftytrader.in Rs.1,950 per 10 g, silverji.in Delhi Rs.194.51 a gram, silvercoins.in Rs.1,940 per 10 g, goodreturns.in calculator Rs.195 a gram). It is a dated example only: silver rates move daily, and other sites quoted Rs.177 to Rs.208 a gram for 999 the same day. Replace it with your jeweller's rate."
      ],
      [
        "How much do jewellers pay for old silver?",
        "Typically 93% to 98% of the pure silver value — 95% to 98% for coins and bars, 93% to 95% for jewellery. This calculator defaults to a 5% deduction, so you get 95%. Making charges and GST paid when you bought the piece are not recovered, and some buyers deduct a further 2% to 3% as melting charges. Visit two or three buyers and take the highest quote."
      ],
      [
        "Why does the rate differ from another site?",
        "Three reasons. First, the rate moves daily — a quote from another day is a different price. Second, sites quote different things: goldtocurrency.com quotes the international spot (Rs.154.29 a gram on 14 November 2026), while Indian retail adds import duty and dealer premium (Rs.195 a gram in the retail cluster, Rs.208.19 on marketshost.com). Third, the purity differs: a 925 quote is 92.5% of the 999 rate. Compare like with like — same day, same purity, same retail-or-spot basis."
      ],
      [
        "What are making charges on silver, and can they be negotiated?",
        "Making charges are what the jeweller charges for turning raw silver into a finished piece. Silver making is usually quoted per gram — Rs.10 to Rs.50 a gram is typical — though some jewellers quote 10% to 40% of the silver value. They are not fixed: compare two or three shops, and bargain, especially for plain designs, bulk purchases or festival sales. Coins and bars carry far lower making charges than jewellery."
      ],
      [
        "Should I buy 999 or 925 silver?",
        "For investment — coins and bars — buy 999 fine silver: it carries the highest resale value and the lowest making charges. For jewellery you will wear, buy 925 sterling: the 7.5% alloy makes it strong enough for daily wear, while 999 is too soft and scratches easily. 916 suits utensils and idols. The calculator prices all four, so the difference is visible in rupees before you decide."
      ],
      [
        "Is this financial advice?",
        "No. This calculator works out figures from the numbers you enter, for education and planning. Silver rates move daily, making charges and buyback percentages vary by jeweller and city, and the pre-filled rate is a dated example only. For advice tailored to your situation, consult a SEBI-registered investment adviser."
      ]
    ],
    "inputNote": "Pick the mode first: the price of silver jewellery, the value of your old silver, or how much silver a budget buys. Enter the weight and the unit — grams, 10-gram units, tolas, kilograms or troy ounces — and the purity: 999 fine, 925 sterling, 916 or 900 coin. Silver is priced by fineness out of 1,000, not by karat out of 24, so 925 means 92.5% pure silver. Enter today's 999 rate per gram, or switch the basis and enter the rate for your purity directly — marketshost.com quotes 925 at Rs.192.76 a gram and 999 at Rs.208.19 a gram on 14 November 2026, and both reproduce exactly here. Making charges can be a percentage of the silver value or a flat amount per gram — silver making is usually Rs.10 to Rs.50 a gram. The sample — 10 g of 999 at Rs.195 a gram, 10% making — is filled in.",
    "key": "spc",
    "kind": "spc",
    "lead": "Enter the weight, the purity and today's rate, and see the full bill — silver value, wastage, making charges and GST — before you pay the jeweller. On the sample — 10 g of 999 silver at Rs.195 a gram with 10% making — the silver is Rs.1,950, making is Rs.195, GST is Rs.64 and the bill is Rs.2,209, or Rs.221 a gram. You can also price your old silver for resale, or work out how many grams a budget buys. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "meta": "Silver price calculator — work out what silver jewellery costs with making charges, wastage and GST, what your old silver is worth on resale, and how much silver your budget buys. Sample: 10 g of 999 silver at Rs.195 a gram with 10% making is Rs.2,209 all in. Free, in your browser.",
    "methodology": "The rules and examples on this page were checked against goodreturns.in, marketshost.com, heyexpense.com, dhan.co, arthgyaan.com and goldtocurrency.com on 14 November 2026, and every worked example on the page reproduces its source exactly except where a convention difference is stated. The GST treatment follows the CBIC sectoral FAQ on gems and jewellery: 3% of the total transaction value, with 5% applying only to job work. For related tools, the GST Calculator works out the tax on any purchase the same way, the Percentage Calculator works out any percentage the same way, the SIP Calculator plans the monthly saving for a silver purchase, and the EMI Calculator works out loan payments. The Income Tax Calculator works out the full year's tax, and the Top Jewellers in India guide lists the big chains. Last updated 14 November 2026.",
