    "mistakesTitle": "Common silver-buying mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "Silver value = weight in grams × rate for the purity, where the rate for a purity is the 999 rate × fineness ÷ 1,000 — except 999 itself, which is valued at the full entered rate, because the entered 999 rate IS the rate for 999-fine silver. Wastage = weight × wastage% × rate for the purity. Making = a percentage of the silver value, or a flat Rs. per gram × weight. Bill = silver value + wastage + making + stone charges, then GST 3% on the whole bill (1.5% CGST + 1.5% SGST). That is the CBIC sectoral FAQ position on gems and jewellery: 3% of the total transaction value, whether making charges are shown separately or not — the 5% rate applies only to job work, when a jeweller gives metal to a karigar, not to the retail sale of finished jewellery. Several calculators split the GST as 3% on the metal plus 5% on the making; that gives a higher total — Rs.11,875 instead of Rs.11,845 on the heyexpense.com example, and Rs.26,570 instead of Rs.26,524 on the dhan.co example — and it does not match the CBIC retail position. Resale: pure value = weight × rate for the purity, buyback = pure value × (1 − deduction%). Budget: grams = budget ÷ rate for the purity. Each money line is rounded to whole rupees before the lines are added.",
    "notice": "This is an estimate, not financial advice. The pre-filled 999 rate of Rs.195 a gram is the retail rate on 14 November 2026 from niftytrader.in, silverji.in, silvercoins.in and goodreturns.in — a dated example only. Silver rates move daily, making charges and wastage vary by jeweller and city, and the buyback percentage is a typical range, not a guaranteed price. Always confirm the rate with your jeweller before you pay. GST is 3% on jewellery, and 5% applies only to job work. Nothing you enter leaves your browser.",
    "overview": "A silver price calculator turns the weight, the purity and today's rate into the amount you will actually pay — or receive. The silver value is the weight in grams times the rate for that purity. On the sample — 10 g of 999 silver at Rs.195 a gram — the silver value is Rs.1,950. Making charges, wastage and any stone charges are added, then GST at 3% on the whole bill, giving Rs.2,209 in all, or Rs.221 a gram. The same calculator prices old silver for resale — 100 g of 925 at the same rate is worth Rs.18,038 as pure silver, and Rs.17,136 at a 5% buyback deduction — and works out how much silver a budget buys: Rs.50,000 buys 277.19 g of 925. The GST Calculator on this site works out the tax on any purchase the same way, the Percentage Calculator works out any percentage the same way, and the Top Jewellers in India guide lists the big chains whose making charges and buyback policies are quoted on this page. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
