    "purityTitle": "Purity, fineness and the rate a gram — at Rs.195",
    "rateNote": "The pre-filled 999 rate of Rs.195 a gram is the retail rate on 14 November 2026 from niftytrader.in (Rs.1,950 per 10 g), silverji.in Delhi (Rs.194.51 a gram), silvercoins.in (Rs.1,940 per 10 g) and goodreturns.in (calculator rate Rs.195 a gram). marketshost.com quotes a higher retail Rs.208.19 a gram for 999 (925 Rs.192.76, 916 Rs.190.89, 900 Rs.187.56) the same day, and its own second page quotes fine silver at Rs.192.76 a gram — retail quotes for 999 ranged from Rs.177 to Rs.208 a gram across the sites fetched. goldtocurrency.com quotes the international spot of Rs.154.29 a gram (Rs.4,799 a troy ounce) before import duty and dealer premium. The heyexpense.com example prints Rs.11,875 under its split GST (3% metal + 5% making); this calculator gives Rs.11,845 under the CBIC retail position. The dhan.co example prints Rs.26,570 under its split GST; this calculator gives Rs.26,524 — and its stated base of Rs.23,410 implies a rate of Rs.234.10 a gram, not the Rs.234 a gram it states. marketshost.com's three popular calculations print their pre-GST subtotals (Rs.21,740, Rs.1,12,218, Rs.10,624); with GST 3% the totals are Rs.22,392, Rs.1,15,585 and Rs.10,943. goldtocurrency.com's kilogram figure of Rs.1,54,291.18 uses its unrounded spot; at the displayed Rs.154.29 a gram it is Rs.1,54,290. arthgyaan.com states no numeric worked example in the fetched page text. Last updated 14 November 2026.",
    "rulesNote": "These six rules are the ones that cost real money when they are missed. The rate, the purity and the GST treatment are the three things to get right before you trust any number on this page.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Silver rates move every day with the international spot, the rupee-dollar rate and local demand. On 14 November 2026 the retail quotes for 999 silver ranged from Rs.177 to Rs.208 a gram across the sites fetched for this page — a Rs.31 spread on the same metal on the same day. The pre-filled rate of Rs.195 a gram is a dated example only. Always replace it with your jeweller's rate, and check the rate on the day you buy.",
        "rule": "The rate moves daily — and it is the biggest input"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Gold is priced by karat out of 24; silver is priced by fineness out of 1,000. A 925 piece is 92.5% silver, so it is priced at 92.5% of the 999 rate — Rs.180.38 a gram on a Rs.195 rate. A 900 piece is 90% silver, Rs.175.50 a gram. If a shop quotes a 925 piece at the 999 rate, you are overpaying by 7.5% on the metal alone.",
        "rule": "Silver is priced by fineness, not by karat"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The CBIC sectoral FAQ on gems and jewellery states that GST is 3% of the total transaction value of jewellery, whether the making charge is shown separately or not. The 5% rate applies to job work — when a jeweller gives metal to a karigar to make — not to the retail sale of finished jewellery. Several calculators split the GST as 3% on the metal plus 5% on the making; that gives a higher total (Rs.11,875 instead of Rs.11,845 on the heyexpense.com example) and does not match the CBIC retail position.",
        "rule": "GST is 3% on the whole bill — 5% is for job work only"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Silver making is usually quoted per gram — Rs.10 to Rs.50 a gram is typical, and Kolkata and Jaipur are cheaper than Mumbai and Delhi — though some jewellers quote a percentage, usually 10% to 40%. Wastage is typically 3% to 8%. On the 100 g sterling case above, making and wastage together add Rs.2,402 to a Rs.18,038 silver value. Compare the true cost a gram between shops, not the making percentage alone.",
        "rule": "Making and wastage are where shops differ"
      },
      {
        "detail": "When you sell, the jeweller pays for the silver weight at today's rate, less a deduction — typically 95% to 98% for coins and bars, 93% to 95% for jewellery. The making charges and GST you paid are not recovered, and some buyers deduct a further 2% to 3% as melting charges. On the 100 g sterling case, Rs.17,136 comes back on a Rs.21,053 bill. Visit two or three buyers and take the highest quote.",
        "rule": "Buyback pays for the metal only — 93% to 98% of it"
      },
      {
        "detail": "BIS hallmarking is mandatory for silver jewellery in India since June 2021. The piece must carry the BIS mark, the purity number (999, 925, 916 or 900) and the jeweller's mark, and a HUID number you can verify on the BIS website. No hallmark means you are trusting the jeweller's word on the purity — and the purity is what the whole price is built on. Always demand an itemised bill: weight, rate per gram, making charges and GST, all separate.",
        "rule": "Hallmark first, then the bill"
      }
    ],
