    "factorTitle": "Every factor in the load, and how it is worked out",
    "faq": [
      [
        "How is AC tonnage calculated?",
        "Tonnage = room area in sq ft / 120, times ceiling height / 10, times a sun factor (normal 1.00, sunny 1.10, west-facing 1.20, top floor 1.20), plus 0.1 ton for each person beyond two, plus 0.5 ton for cooking heat. Then round UP to the nearest standard size \u2014 0.8, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 or 3 ton. On the sample \u2014 a 12 x 12 ft bedroom, 10 ft ceiling, normal sun, two people, no kitchen \u2014 that is 144 / 120 = 1.2 ton, so a 1.5-ton AC."
      ],
      [
        "What size AC do I need for a 12 x 12 room?",
        "A 1.5-ton AC. The room is 144 sq ft, the load is 1.2 ton (14,400 BTU an hour, 4.22 kW), and the smallest standard size at or above 1.2 ton is 1.5 ton. Calcvind.com works the same room and gives the same answer."
      ],
      [
        "Is a 1.5 ton AC enough for 180 sq ft?",
        "Yes, by the 1-ton-per-120-sq-ft rule: 180 / 120 = 1.5 ton exactly, so a 1.5-ton AC. Optimist.in says a 2-ton AC for the same room, because it works at 120 BTU per sq ft (area divided by 100, giving 1.8 ton) instead of area divided by 120. Desiutility.com, calcvind.com and bajajfinserv.in all say 1.5 ton for 180 sq ft. If the room is on the top floor or faces west, the answer becomes 2 ton."
      ],
      [
        "What size AC for a top-floor room?",
        "Add about 20% to the load. A 150 sq ft top-floor room needs 1.5 ton (desiutility.com: it needed 1.5 ton, not 1 ton), and a 12 x 12 ft top-floor bedroom needs 1.44 ton \u2014 still a 1.5-ton AC, but with almost no margin. Coolinginsights.com gives a larger answer for the same room (2.25 ton, so a 2.5-ton unit) because it adds flat tons to a divided-by-100 base; this calculator multiplies by 1.20 on a divided-by-120 base. If the room is genuinely hot in the afternoon, the larger size is the safer buy."
      ],
      [
        "How much does ceiling height matter?",
        "A lot. The load scales with height divided by 10: a 12 x 12 ft room at 8 ft needs 0.96 ton (a 1-ton AC), at 10 ft it needs 1.2 ton (a 1.5-ton AC), and at 12 ft it needs 1.44 ton (still a 1.5-ton AC, but with less margin). Bajaj Finserv puts it as roughly 10% more cooling for every foot above 10 ft."
      ],
      [
        "Does the number of people in the room matter?",
        "Yes \u2014 0.1 ton for each person beyond two. Two people are already in the baseline. Six people in a 12 x 12 ft room add 0.4 ton, taking the load from 1.2 ton to 1.6 ton and the answer from a 1.5-ton AC to a 2-ton AC. Optimist.in is more cautious and says 0.1 ton per additional two people; this calculator follows the 0.1-per-person figure that calcvind.com, bajajfinserv.in and desiutility.com use."
      ],
      [
        "What does 1 ton mean in BTU and kW?",
        "1 ton of cooling removes 12,000 BTU of heat an hour, which is 3.517 kW of cooling \u2014 the ASHRAE definition, stated by calcvind.com, optimist.in, coolinginsights.com and bajajfinserv.in alike. So a 1.5-ton AC removes 18,000 BTU an hour (5.28 kW of cooling). AC ratings are tested at 35\u00b0C outdoor ambient temperature under IS 1391 Part 2. Note that kW of cooling is not the electricity the AC draws: a 1.5-ton 5-star inverter typically draws about 1 to 1.5 kW at full load."
      ],
      [
        "Should I buy a 5-star AC?",
        "A higher star rating uses less electricity for the same cooling. Under BEE's star labelling for room air conditioners, a 5-star split AC must have an ISEER of 5.60 or above (January 2026 to December 2027), against 4.30\u20134.99 for 3-star. On BEE's own label figures, a 1.5-ton 5-star (ISEER 5.80) uses 681 units a year against 878 units for a 3-star (ISEER 4.50) \u2014 about 22% fewer units. Do not trust '5-star saves 20\u201325%' as a current BEE fact: that figure traces to a 2016 press report of a BEE claim, and BEE's own star table could not be read directly when this page was checked."
      ],
      [
        "Is this a substitute for an HVAC design?",
        "No. This is a planning estimate from a rule of thumb, not a surveyed measurement. The actual load depends on insulation, windows, climate and how the room is used \u2014 a glass-walled room or a room in a 45\u00b0C city needs more than this calculator says. For a large or unusual room, get a dealer or installer assessment before buying. An oversized AC is not harmless either: it cools in short bursts, leaves the air damp, and wastes electricity."
      ]
    ],
    "inputNote": "Enter the room's length and width \u2014 in feet, or in metres and the calculator converts (1 m = 3.28084 ft). The ceiling height is in feet; 10 ft is the standard Indian ceiling. Pick the sun exposure: normal, sunny, west-facing, or top floor \u2014 a top-floor or west-facing room needs about 20% more cooling. Count the people usually in the room: the first two are already in the baseline, each extra person adds 0.1 ton. Tick cooking heat if the room is a kitchen or a kitchen-diner: cooking adds 0.5 ton. The sample \u2014 a 12 x 12 ft bedroom \u2014 is filled in.",
    "key": "atc",
    "kind": "atc",
    "lead": "Enter the room's length and width, the ceiling height, how much sun it gets, how many people usually sit in it, and whether cooking happens there \u2014 and get the cooling load in tons, BTU per hour and kW, plus the AC size to buy. On the sample \u2014 a 12 x 12 ft bedroom, 10 ft ceiling, normal sun, two people, no kitchen \u2014 the room is 144 sq ft, the load is 1.2 ton (14,400 BTU an hour, 4.22 kW), so the answer is a 1.5-ton AC. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "meta": "AC tonnage calculator \u2014 work out the right air conditioner size for any room in India from its length, width, ceiling height, sun exposure, people and cooking heat. Sample: a 12 x 12 ft bedroom needs 1.2 ton of cooling, so a 1.5-ton AC. Free, in your browser.",
    "methodology": "The rules and examples on this page were checked against calcvind.com, desiutility.com, optimist.in, coolinginsights.com, bajajfinserv.in and vsctimes.com on 14 November 2026, and every worked example on the page reproduces its source exactly except where a method difference is stated \u2014 the optimist.in baseline, the coolinginsights.com flat additions, and the bajajfinserv.in divided-by-600 formula, which is not used. The star-rating bands come from the BEE gazette notifications as reported by benchmarksutra.in; BEE's own star table could not be read directly, and the page says so. The tonnage method is a planning rule, not a surveyed measurement. For related tools, the EMI Calculator works out the monthly cost of buying the AC on EMI, the GST Calculator works out the tax on the purchase, the Percentage Calculator works out any percentage the same way, the SIP Calculator plans the monthly saving for the purchase, and the FD Calculator and PPF Calculator plan the saving itself. The Home Loan EMI Calculator and the Income Tax Calculator cover the bigger money questions, and the CGPA Calculator is the same family of tools. Last updated 14 November 2026.",
