    "mistakesTitle": "Common AC sizing mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The method is the one the Indian calculators agree on, with the disagreements stated. Baseline: 1 ton per 120 sq ft at a 10 ft ceiling (calcvind.com, desiutility.com, and bajajfinserv.in's own summary \u2014 its printed formula, area divided by 600, is wrong and is not used here; 180 divided by 600 is 0.3, not 1.5). Ceiling: multiply by height divided by 10. Sun: normal x1.00, sunny x1.10, west-facing x1.20, top floor x1.20. People: add 0.1 ton for each person beyond two. Cooking: add 0.5 ton. Then round UP to the nearest standard size \u2014 0.8, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 or 3 ton \u2014 never down. Optimist.in uses a different baseline (120 BTU per sq ft, i.e. area divided by 100), so its raw loads run higher: 180 sq ft is 1.8 ton there and 1.5 ton here, and it recommends a 2-ton AC where this calculator recommends 1.5 ton. Coolinginsights.com adds flat tons on a divided-by-100 base: its top-floor example is 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.25 = 2.25 ton, so a 2.5-ton unit, where this calculator gives 1.5 ton for the same room. Both differences are stated on this page with the arithmetic shown, so the reader can judge.",
