    "inputNote": "Enter the built-up area in sq ft \u2014 the total across all floors, not the plot area and not the carpet area. If you only know the plot, switch to plot mode: the calculator multiplies your plot area by the number of floors. Pick the quality tier: basic, standard, premium or luxury \u2014 the rate table on this page shows what each tier assumes. Pick the city type: a metro costs about 15% more than a tier-2 city, a smaller town about 10% less. Set the number of floors: a single storey costs about 6% more per sq ft (the foundation and roof are spread over less area) and a G+3 house about 3% more. If you already have a contractor's Rs/sq ft quote, enter it and the calculator prices your house at that rate instead \u2014 the tier band is still shown, so you can see whether the quote sits inside it. The sample \u2014 a 1,000 sq ft standard house in a tier-2 city \u2014 is filled in.",
    "key": "hcc",
    "kind": "hcc",
    "lead": "Enter the built-up area in sq ft \u2014 all floors added together \u2014 pick the quality tier and the city type, and get the cost as a range: low, typical and high, with the cost per sq ft, the split between structure, finishing and services, and the cement, steel, brick, sand and aggregate you will need. On the sample \u2014 a 1,000 sq ft standard house in a tier-2 city, two floors \u2014 the answer is Rs 20\u201324 lakh, typically Rs 22 lakh. Everything is worked out in your browser; nothing is sent anywhere.",
    "meta": "House construction cost calculator \u2014 work out what it costs to build a house in India from the built-up area, quality tier and city type. Sample: a 1,000 sq ft standard house in a tier-2 city costs Rs 20\u201324 lakh, typically Rs 22 lakh. Free, in your browser.",
    "methodology": "The rates and examples on this page were checked against arcpem.com, build.nineclip.com, vastucalc.web.app, speakarch.com and smarvil.com on 14 November 2026, and the CPWD Plinth Area Rates 2025 figures were verified from the official CPWD document itself. Every worked example on the page reproduces its source exactly except where a band or method difference is stated \u2014 nineclip.com's wider standard and premium bands, speakarch.com's Mumbai and Delhi city bands, vastucalc.web.app's Nashik rate, and arcpem.com's plus-or-minus-8% sample range. The construction-cost method is a planning rule, not a surveyed market price. For related tools, the EMI Calculator works out the monthly cost of a home loan, the Home Loan EMI Calculator covers the loan itself, the GST Calculator works out the tax on materials and services, the Percentage Calculator works out any percentage the same way, the SIP Calculator plans the monthly saving for the build, and the FD Calculator and PPF Calculator plan the saving itself. The Income Tax Calculator covers the bigger money questions, and the CGPA Calculator is the same family of tools. Last updated 14 November 2026.",
    "mistakes": [
      [
        "PLOT",
        "Pricing the plot area instead of the built-up area",
        "A 1,200 sq ft plot with two floors is 2,400 sq ft of built-up area, not 1,200. Price the plot and the estimate comes out at half the real cost. Count every floor, and check whether a quote is on built-up area or carpet area \u2014 carpet runs 15\u201320% less, so a carpet-area rate looks cheaper than it is."
      ],
      [
        "SCOPE",
        "Comparing quotes with different scopes",
        "A labour-only rate and a turnkey rate are not comparable numbers, and the gap between them is larger than any quality difference (vastucalc.web.app). One quote may include flooring, paint, doors, windows, plumbing and electrical while another covers structure only (speakarch.com). Ask each contractor for a per-sq-ft rate with a written list of what it includes before comparing."
      ],
      [
        "EXTRAS",
        "Forgetting what the per-sq-ft rate leaves out",
        "Land, approvals, architect fees (typically 2\u20135%), interiors and furniture, the compound wall and a 5\u201310% contingency all sit outside the rate. Owners commonly budget another 15\u201325% over the civil cost for these (arcpem.com). On the sample, architect fees are Rs 40,000\u20131,20,000 and contingency is Rs 1\u20132.4 lakh on top of Rs 20\u201324 lakh \u2014 the money to actually arrange is Rs 21.4\u201327.6 lakh."
      ],
      [
        "NUMBER",
        "Trusting one precise number",
        "A single number would be wrong: material prices move quarter to quarter, finish choices swing the total by 30% on their own, and the five calculators India uses disagree by lakhs on the same house. A dated range you can defend beats a precise number you cannot (nineclip.com). If a quote sits far outside the band, ask why \u2014 that conversation is what this tool is for."
      ],
      [
        "ORDER",
        "Ordering materials from a thumb rule",
        "The thumb rules are planning allowances, not an order list: cement 0.35\u20130.5 bags and steel 3\u20134 kg per sq ft already include typical wastage, so do not add wastage again (nineclip.com). The exact quantities depend on the structural design, the soil, the spans and the drawings \u2014 a G+2 building needs proportionally more steel per sq ft than a single-storey house (arcpem.com)."
      ]
    ],
    "mistakesTitle": "Common house construction cost mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The method is the one the Indian calculators agree on, with the disagreements stated. Base rates are arcpem.com's national 2026 bands: basic Rs 1,700\u20131,900, standard Rs 2,000\u20132,400, premium Rs 2,500\u20133,000, luxury Rs 3,000\u20134,000 per sq ft. City multipliers are metro x1.15, tier-2 city x1.00, smaller town x0.90, derived from the midpoints of arcpem.com's own city tables \u2014 so the model reproduces arcpem's city bands approximately, not exactly: metro standard is Rs 2,300\u20132,760 here against arcpem's Rs 2,450\u20132,600, and Mumbai standard is Rs 23\u201327.6 lakh here against speakarch.com's Rs 22\u201328 lakh. Floors multiply by 1.06 for a single storey, 1.00 for two or three floors, 1.03 for four floors (vastucalc.web.app). The split is structure 57%, finishing 31%, services 12% (nineclip.com; published sources put it at 55\u201360 / 30\u201335 / 10\u201315). Materials follow nineclip.com's thumb rules: cement 0.35\u20130.5 bags, steel 3\u20134 kg, bricks 8\u201310, sand 1\u20131.4 cft, aggregate 1.1\u20131.5 cft per sq ft. Nineclip.com's own standard band is wider and lower \u2014 Rs 1,500\u20132,500 per sq ft, so Rs 15\u201325 lakh for 1,000 sq ft where this calculator gives Rs 20\u201324 lakh \u2014 and its premium band runs to Rs 4,500 per sq ft, so its 1,500 sq ft premium example is Rs 37.5\u201367.5 lakh where this calculator gives Rs 37.5\u201345 lakh. The low agrees exactly in both cases; the highs differ because the bands differ, and the page states that rather than hiding it.",
    "notice": "A planning-stage estimate, not a quotation \u2014 most builds land within \u00b18\u201315% of the mid-point. The actual cost varies with the design, the soil, the materials chosen and local labour \u2014 get 2\u20133 local contractor quotes and a detailed BOQ before building.",
    "overview": "It multiplies your built-up area by a rate per sq ft, and shows the answer as a range \u2014 low, typical and high \u2014 instead of a single number, because at the planning stage a range is honest and a single number is not. The rate comes from the quality tier (basic, standard, premium or luxury) and the city type (metro, tier-2 city, or smaller town), and the calculator also splits the total into structure, finishing and services, works out the cement, steel, bricks, sand and aggregate you will need, and checks the result against the government's own CPWD Plinth Area Rates 2025. The five calculators India actually uses disagree with each other by a wide margin \u2014 nineclip.com's standard band is Rs 1,500\u20132,500 per sq ft while arcpem.com's is Rs 2,000\u20132,400 \u2014 so this page states every disagreement with the arithmetic shown, instead of smoothing them into one false-precise figure.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a house construction cost calculator do?",
