    "mistakesTitle": "Common paint calculation mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The method is the standard Indian estimate, checked against the five calculators India actually uses on 14 November 2026: net wall area is 2 × (length + width) × height, less 21 sq ft per door and 13 sq ft per window, plus the ceiling (length × width) if you tick it. Finish paint is net area × coats ÷ coverage, where coverage is the midpoint of the paint type's band (economy 115, premium 135, luxury 155, exterior 115, texture 80 sq ft/L). Then 10% is added for wastage and touch-ups and the total is rounded up to the nearest 0.5 L, and the tins are the fewest litres that cover it from 20, 10, 4 and 1 litre packs. Primer is net area ÷ 175 × coats × 1.05 — 150–200 sq ft/L at a 175 midpoint, with a 5% allowance — 1 coat for a repaint, 2 for fresh plaster. Putty is net area × 1.2 kg/m² in total for 2 coats (bttotek), fresh plaster only. Cost is buy litres × your price per litre, and putty kg × your price per kg — nothing is priced as fact.",
    "notice": "Coverage is a planning estimate — real use depends on the wall, the colour change and the tin's own rate. No paint price is printed here as fact: the cost comes only from the per-litre prices you enter.",
    "overview": "It works out how much paint a room needs from the wall area — not the floor area. Measure the room in feet, deduct the doors and windows, divide by the paint's coverage per litre and multiply by the number of coats, then add 10% for wastage and round up to the tins you can actually buy. It also works out the primer for one coat (two on fresh plaster) and the wall putty for fresh plaster, and the cost — but only from the per-litre prices you type in, because paint prices change by brand, city and month and no printed price stays true.",
