    "factorTitle": "Every line on the sample, and how it is worked out",
    "faq": [
      [
        "How much term insurance cover do I need?",
        "The standard guideline is 10–15 times your annual income — on ₹12 lakh a year that is ₹1.2–1.8 crore. The more precise figure is needs-based: 15× income, plus outstanding loans, minus the term cover you already hold. With a ₹50 lakh home loan and ₹25 lakh of existing cover, a 35-year-old on ₹12 lakh needs ₹2.05 crore. This calculator shows both figures, so you can see whether the cover you picked is under, within or above the range."
      ],
      [
        "How much does term insurance cost for a 30-year-old?",
        "For ₹1 crore of cover over 30 years, a 30-year-old male non-smoker pays an estimated ₹9,025 a year — ₹790 a month, ₹2,70,750 over the term, ₹90 per ₹1 lakh of cover. Across the five insurers on the published table the same profile costs ₹8,123–₹10,108 a year (Tata AIA cheapest, LIC dearest). A 30-year-old woman pays ₹7,400 a year for the same cover, and a smoker pays ₹13,086."
      ],
      [
        "Why do smokers pay higher premiums?",
        "Insurers price on mortality data, and smokers die younger at every age — driven by cardiovascular disease, stroke and several cancers. The loading here is 1.45 times the non-smoker rate: ₹13,086 a year against ₹9,025 for the same 30-year-old on ₹1 crore for 30 years, a difference of ₹4,061 a year. PolicyBachat puts the loading at 30–50%, so 45% sits inside its range. To qualify as a non-smoker you must be tobacco-free for at least 12 months, and insurers test cotinine levels at the medical exam."
      ],
      [
        "Is term insurance premium tax-deductible?",
        "Yes, under the old tax regime: premiums qualify for deduction under Section 80C of the Income-tax Act up to a combined ₹1.5 lakh a year, shared with PPF, EPF, ELSS and the rest. On the sample premium of ₹9,025, an old-regime taxpayer at the 20% slab saves ₹1,805 a year (at 30% it is ₹2,708). Under the new tax regime there is no 80C deduction at all. The death benefit paid to the nominee is fully tax-free under Section 10(10D) under either regime."
      ],
      [
        "Is GST charged on term insurance premiums?",
        "No — not since 22 September 2025, when the 56th GST Council meeting's decision took effect. Individual life insurance premiums, including term plans, are exempt from GST; before that they attracted 18%. On the sample premium of ₹9,025 a year the exemption saves ₹1,625 a year — with the old 18% the premium would have cost ₹10,650. Group term policies, such as the cover an employer provides, still attract 18% GST."
      ],
      [
        "What happens if I survive the policy term?",
        "On a plain term plan, nothing comes back — that is by design, and it is why term cover is the cheapest life insurance per rupee of cover. The premiums bought protection for the years you needed it, the same way a car insurance premium is not refunded if you do not claim. Some insurers offer a Return of Premium variant that refunds the premiums at maturity, but it costs 2.3 times the plain-term premium — ₹20,758 a year instead of ₹9,025 on the sample, ₹6,22,740 over 30 years against ₹2,70,750 — which is a poor savings choice."
      ],
      [
        "Is it cheaper to pay monthly or annually?",
        "Annually. Monthly premiums carry a 5% modal loading: on the sample, ₹790 a month works out to ₹9,480 over the year, ₹455 more than the ₹9,025 annual premium. The monthly figure is still useful for budgeting — ₹790 a month for ₹1 crore of cover — but if the annual payment fits your cash flow, pay annually."
      ],
      [
        "What do the critical illness and accidental death riders cost?",
        "Critical illness cover costs ₹120 per ₹1 lakh of CI cover a year at 30, rising by ₹12 per year of age above 30 — so ₹10 lakh of CI cover is ₹1,200 a year at 30 and ₹2,400 at 40, and a smoker pays 1.45 times that. Accidental death cover costs ₹80 per ₹1 lakh a year at any age — ₹800 a year for ₹10 lakh. On the sample, adding ₹10 lakh of each takes the premium from ₹9,025 to ₹11,025 a year. Waiver of premium is 5% of the base premium — ₹451 a year on the sample."
      ],
      [
        "What is the free-look period for term insurance?",
        "30 days from the day you receive the policy document. Under IRDAI's 2024 policyholder-protection rules — Regulation 20 of the IRDAI (Protection of Policyholders' Interests, Operations and Allied Matters of Insurers) Regulations, 2024, and the Master Circular on Protection of Policyholders' Interests of September 2024 — a new life policy with a term of one year or more carries a 30-day free-look period however it was bought; the old 15-day offline window is gone. Cancel inside it and the insurer must refund the eligible premium, less permitted deductions, within 7 days."
      ],
      [
        "Is this premium a quote I can buy at?",
        "No — it is an estimate from a published planning model, not an insurer quote. The actual premium depends on underwriting: medical test results, BMI, occupation, family medical history and the insurer's own mortality tables. The five-insurer range on the sample is ₹8,123–₹10,108 a year for the same profile, and your quote can sit outside it. Use this calculator to budget and to compare like-for-like quotes, then read the policy wording and use the 30-day free-look period to check the documents."
      ]
    ],
    "inputNote": "The sample is a 30-year-old man, non-smoker, earning ₹12 lakh a year, with ₹1 crore of cover for 30 years, paying annually, no riders, on the new tax regime.",
