    "modelNote": "The method is the published calculatorkosh model, reproduced with attribution and checked against the five calculators India actually uses on 14 November 2026. The annual premium is the age rate × gender factor × smoker factor × term factor × (cover ÷ ₹1 crore) × bulk discount. The age rate is the published table per ₹1 crore — ₹5,500 at 18, ₹6,000 at 20, ₹7,200 at 25, ₹9,500 at 30, ₹14,000 at 35, ₹21,500 at 40, ₹34,000 at 45, ₹53,000 at 50, ₹82,000 at 55 and ₹1,30,000 at 60 — with linear interpolation between the points and extrapolation above 60 at ₹9,600 per year of age. The term factor is 0.78 at 15 years or less, rising to 1.00 at 40 years. Gender is male 1.00, female 0.82; smoking is non-smoker 1.00, smoker 1.45. Cover scales with the sum assured, with a bulk discount of ×0.95 at ₹2 crore and ×0.90 at ₹5 crore. Return of Premium replaces the base with 2.3 times the plain-term figure. The critical illness rider is (CI cover ÷ ₹1 lakh) × (₹120 + ₹12 per year of age above 30) × the smoker factor; the accidental death rider is (ADB cover ÷ ₹1 lakh) × ₹80; waiver of premium is 5% of the base. Monthly is the annual premium × 1.05 ÷ 12. The insurer estimates are the annual premium × (1 + adjustment): LIC +12%, HDFC Life −5%, ICICI Pru −8%, Tata AIA −10%, Max Life +2%. Recommended cover is 10–15× annual income; the needs-based figure is 15× income + outstanding loans − existing cover.",
    "notice": "An estimate, not a quote. The actual premium depends on underwriting — medical tests, BMI, occupation and health history — so compare real quotes before buying. No insurer endorses this page, and it is not financial advice.",
    "overview": "It estimates the annual premium for pure term cover — the cheapest life insurance there is, because it pays only if you die during the term and returns nothing if you survive it. It works from a published age-rate table: the rate rises with age, women pay less than men, smokers pay more, longer terms cost a little more per year, and the premium scales with the cover. It also checks the cover against the 10–15× income rule, prices the two common riders, and shows the tax treatment — no GST since 22 September 2025, Section 80C under the old regime only, and a tax-free death benefit under Section 10(10D).",
