    "rateTitle": "Term insurance rates by age — what a ₹1 crore cover costs",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The published calculatorkosh model: the annual premium is the age rate from the table, times the gender factor (male 1.00, female 0.82), times the smoker factor (non-smoker 1.00, smoker 1.45), times the term factor, times cover ÷ ₹1 crore, times the bulk discount (×0.95 at ₹2 crore, ×0.90 at ₹5 crore). On the sample that is ₹9,500 × 1 × 1 × 0.95 × 1 × 1 = ₹9,025 a year.",
        "rule": "Premium = age rate × gender × smoker × term factor × cover"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The table prints ten points — ₹5,500 at 18 through ₹1,30,000 at 60 — and the calculator interpolates linearly between them: at 32 the rate is ₹9,500 + (2/5) × ₹4,500 = ₹11,300. Above 60 it extrapolates at the 55→60 slope of ₹9,600 per year of age, so 65 is ₹1,78,000. Below 18 there is no rate — 18 is the youngest entry age, and 65 is the oldest the model covers.",
        "rule": "The age rate is a published table, interpolated"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The term factor is 0.78 at 15 years or less, 0.85 at 20, 0.90 at 25, 0.95 at 30 and 1.00 from 40 years — a longer term covers older, riskier years, so the insurer charges more per year for it. A 30-year-old pays ₹9,025 a year for 30 years but only ₹7,410 a year for a 15-year term on the same ₹1 crore — the shorter term is cheaper per year, but the cover ends at 45.",
        "rule": "Longer terms cost a little more per year"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The female factor is 0.82 — a 30-year-old woman pays ₹7,400 a year for the sample cover against ₹9,025 for a man, because female life expectancy is longer. The smoker factor is 1.45 — the same man as a smoker pays ₹13,086. PolicyBachat puts the smoker loading at 30–50%, so 45% sits inside its range; calcusy puts it at 1.5–2×, which is higher than this model uses.",
        "rule": "Women pay less, smokers pay more"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Critical illness cover costs (CI cover ÷ ₹1 lakh) × (₹120 + ₹12 per year of age above 30) × the smoker factor — ₹1,200 a year for ₹10 lakh at 30, ₹2,400 at 40. Accidental death cover costs (ADB cover ÷ ₹1 lakh) × ₹80 — ₹800 a year for ₹10 lakh at any age. Waiver of premium is 5% of the base premium (₹451 on the sample). Return of Premium is not a rider: it replaces the base with 2.3 times the plain-term figure — ₹20,758 a year on the sample.",
        "rule": "Riders are priced on the same age rate"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Paying monthly costs the annual premium × 1.05 ÷ 12 — ₹790 a month on the sample, ₹9,480 over the year, ₹455 more than paying ₹9,025 annually. GST is ₹0: individual term insurance has been GST-exempt since 22 September 2025, when the 56th GST Council meeting's decision took effect — the earlier 18% would have made the sample ₹10,650. Group term policies still attract 18% GST.",
        "rule": "Monthly costs 5% more, and there is no GST"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Term premiums qualify for Section 80C up to ₹1.5 lakh a year, shared with PPF, EPF, ELSS and the rest — but only under the old tax regime. On the sample, an old-regime taxpayer at the 20% slab saves ₹1,805 a year; on the new regime the saving is ₹0. The death benefit your nominee receives is tax-free under Section 10(10D) under either regime. The free-look period is 30 days from receiving the policy document, under IRDAI's 2024 rules — cancel inside it and the premium comes back, less permitted deductions.",
        "rule": "Tax: 80C under the old regime only, 10(10D) always"
      }
    ],
