    "lead": "Enter the age of the eldest member, the sum insured, your city tier and who is covered — and get the estimated annual premium, the monthly equivalent, the premium per ₹1 lakh of cover, the Section 80D deduction and the net cost after the tax saving.",
    "meta": "Health insurance premium calculator — estimate the annual premium for health cover in India from your age, sum insured, city tier and family, with the Section 80D tax saving and the net cost.",
    "methodology": "The rates on this page were checked against the calculators India actually uses, on 14–15 November 2026. Four were fetched in full: calkulator.in (working calculator with the published age-band rates, loadings, member factors and floater discount, plus a worked family-floater example), CalcWise (working calculator with an indicative premium table by age band and city tier), Tata AIG (worked example: a 30-year-old on ₹10 lakh in a tier-1 city at about ₹8,855 a year), and DealPlexus (working calculator whose FAQ discloses its own model: a ₹7,000 base for a 35-year-old on ₹5 lakh, plus ₹2,000 per extra member, times a metro 1.2× or tier-3 0.85× multiplier). A fifth, Hanitech, could not be fetched — its page returned an empty reply to curl and the browser fetch failed — so no Hanitech figure is used on this page. The premium model on this page is calkulator.in's published model, reproduced with attribution: the age-band rates, city loadings, member factors, floater discount, pre-existing and smoker loadings, GST treatment and 80D treatment (including the 4% cess) are theirs, and the DealPlexus default reproduces exactly under it — ₹13,200 a year for a 35-year-old in a metro on ₹5 lakh with three members. The other sources do not reproduce under this model, and the differences are stated, not hidden: calkulator.in's own Mehta family example prints about ₹18,500 a year, but its stated model gives ₹25,245 — 36.5% above — and its own base figure of about ₹12,000 is the 1.2% band rate, not the 1.5% rate for a 35-year-old, so the example is internally inconsistent with the model it publishes; the ₹5,772 tax saving on ₹18,500 does reproduce arithmetically (₹18,500 × 30% × 1.04). CalcWise's indicative table puts a 30-year-old on ₹10 lakh at ₹6,000–₹8,000 a year in a tier-2 city and ₹7,000–₹9,500 in a metro, against this model's ₹15,000 and ₹16,500 — 87.5% and 73.7% above the top of each range — so this model runs high against CalcWise's ranges. Tata AIG's worked example puts the 30-year-old on ₹10 lakh in a tier-1 city at about ₹8,855 a year, against this model's ₹15,750 — 77.9% above. DealPlexus's 35-year-old on ₹5 lakh is ₹8,400 a year in a metro and ₹5,950 in a tier-3 town, against this model's ₹8,250 (1.8% below) and ₹7,500 (26.1% above) — close in a metro, far apart in a tier-3 town, because DealPlexus scales tier-3 down by 0.85× while this model applies no tier-3 loading. The tax rules were verified from official sources: Section 80D (₹25,000 for self, spouse and children, ₹50,000 for senior citizens, a separate limit for parents, old regime only) was read directly on incometax.gov.in, in the Income Tax Department's own guidance for salaried individuals for AY 2026-27, which also confirms the 4% health and education cess; the GST exemption (individual health insurance, 18% to nil from 22 September 2025) is the 56th GST Council meeting's decision of 3 September 2025 given effect by Notification No. 16/2025-Central Tax (Rate) dated 17 September 2025, confirmed in the Ministry of Finance's answer to Parliament (the CBIC notification page itself was not read directly); the 36-month cap on pre-existing-disease waiting periods is Schedule III of the IRDAI (Insurance Products) Regulations, 2024, in force 1 April 2024, carried through the IRDAI Master Circular on Health Insurance Business of 29 May 2024 — verified through secondary reporting that cites the regulation, as the regulation text itself was not read directly, although the regulation is confirmed listed on IRDAI's own consolidated regulations page. The 65-band boundary (5.0% at 65, 7.0% from 66) is this page's stated choice where the published bands overlap, and the parents-policy formula is this page's stated extension where calkulator.in gives no formula. This is a planning estimate, not a quotation: the actual premium depends on underwriting, the loadings are one source's published model, and no insurer endorses this page. For related tools, the EMI Calculator works out the monthly cost of a loan, the Home Loan EMI Calculator covers a home loan, the SIP Calculator plans a monthly saving, the PPF Calculator and the FD Calculator plan the saving itself, the Gratuity Calculator works out a retirement payout, and the Income Tax Calculator covers the bigger money questions. Last updated 15 November 2026.",
