    "mistakesTitle": "Common health insurance mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The method is the published calkulator.in model, reproduced with attribution and checked against the four calculators India actually uses on 14–15 November 2026. The annual premium is the base premium × city factor × members factor × floater discount × pre-existing loading × smoker loading. The base premium is the sum insured × the age-band rate — 1.2% up to age 25, 1.5% at 26–35, 2.2% at 36–45, 3.5% at 46–55, 5.0% at 56–65 and 7.0% from 66. The published bands overlap at 65 (56–65: 5.0%, 65+: 7.0%), so this page applies 5.0% at 65 and 7.0% from 66 — the lower band at the boundary — and says so. The city factor is metro 1.10, tier-1 1.05, tier-2 1.00, tier-3 1.00. The members factor is self 1.0, self + spouse 1.4, self + spouse + kids 1.6, family floater 1.7, with a floater discount of 0.90 on the family-floater option only. The pre-existing loading is 1.30 and the smoker loading is 1.20. A parents policy is priced separately at the same sum insured and city tier, using the oldest parent's age band and no member, pre-existing or smoker loadings — calkulator.in gives no parents formula, so that is this page's stated extension. The monthly premium is the annual premium ÷ 12, with no monthly loading in this model. GST is ₹0. The Section 80D deduction is the premium up to the limit — ₹25,000 for self and family (₹50,000 if the eldest member is a senior citizen), plus a separate ₹25,000 for parents (₹50,000 if a parent is a senior citizen) — and the tax saving is the deduction × the slab × 1.04, including the 4% health and education cess, under the old regime only.",
    "notice": "An estimate, not a quote. The actual premium depends on underwriting — medical tests, BMI, occupation and health history — so compare real quotes before buying. No insurer endorses this page, and it is not financial advice.",
    "overview": "It estimates the annual premium for a health insurance policy — the amount you pay each year to keep hospital cover active. It works from a published age-band model: the premium is a percentage of the sum insured that rises in bands with the age of the eldest member, then loadings for the city tier, the number of members, a pre-existing condition and smoking, with a discount for a family floater. It also works out the tax side — no GST since 22 September 2025, and the Section 80D deduction and saving under the old regime, including the 4% cess — and checks the sum insured against the recommended range for your city.",
