    "rateTitle": "How many tiles does 100 sq ft need? Every common size",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The published convention, stated on Rupeka's page: base tiles = ceil(project area ÷ tile coverage), total tiles = ceil(base tiles × (1 + wastage % ÷ 100)), boxes = ceil(total tiles ÷ tiles per box). The base is rounded to a whole tile first. Techidea states the other order — wastage on the unrounded ratio, then one rounding — and the two orders agree on every anchor on this page: the 12×10 ft room gives 35 tiles either way, 21 sq m gives 93 either way, and 100 sq ft at 8% gives 27 either way. On the sample that is 120 ÷ 3.875 = 31 tiles, × 1.10 = 35 to buy, ÷ 4 = 9 boxes.",
        "rule": "Tiles to buy = ceil(area ÷ tile area), then wastage, then whole boxes"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Rooms get measured in feet while tiles are sold in millimetres, so tile dimensions are converted to feet first: 600 mm is 1.9685 ft, so a 600 × 600 mm tile covers 3.875 sq ft — not 4. Ekzapp calls 600 × 600 mm a 2 × 2 ft tile in its worked examples, and its counts follow from the rounded 4 sq ft: its 10 × 10 ft bedroom prints 25 tiles before wastage and 27 with 8%, where the exact conversion gives 26 and 29. This page uses the exact conversion and says so. Mixing feet with millimetres without converting is the single commonest source of a wildly wrong tile count.",
        "rule": "Convert the units before you divide"
      },
      {
        "detail": "RupeesCalc, Ekzapp and Helperzy all print the same bands: a simple rectangular room in a straight grid may need 5%, 10% is the practical general allowance, and diagonal, herringbone or highly irregular layouts may need 12% to 20% or more. On the sample room, 0% wastage would give 31 tiles, 10% gives 35 and 15% gives 36 — the allowance is small in tiles but it is the difference between finishing the job and a second trip for a different shade. Never use 0%: a single cracked tile in transit leaves you short.",
        "rule": "Wastage: 5–10% straight, 12–20% diagonal"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Tiles are sold in complete boxes, so the order rounds up: 35 tiles at 4 a box is 9 boxes, which supplies 36 tiles and leaves 1 spare. That spare comes from box rounding, not from the wastage buffer, and it is shown separately — it is the tile you keep for a future repair, not a tile you can spend on cutting. If 93 tiles are needed and a box holds 8, 11 boxes provide only 88, so the order becomes 12 boxes and 96 tiles. Buying the calculated tile count instead of complete boxes is one of the commonest mistakes.",
        "rule": "Boxes round up — and the spares are not wastage"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Box pricing is boxes × price per box; tile pricing is tiles to buy × price per tile. Use box pricing when the supplier sells only complete boxes — it prices the full rounded box quantity, spares included. Use per-tile pricing only when a genuine per-tile rate applies. Never mix them: 132 tiles at ₹65 a tile is ₹8,580, but 132 tiles bought as boxes at ₹65 a tile would be a different number, and this calculator refuses to compute it. No price on this page is a market fact — prices are figures you enter, or clearly-labelled worked examples from the cited calculators.",
        "rule": "Price per box and price per tile are different bills"
      },
      {
        "detail": "For a wall, multiply wall length by wall height for the gross area, then subtract the combined opening area. A 10 ft by 9 ft wall is 90 sq ft gross; a 7 ft by 3 ft door takes 21 sq ft off, leaving 69 sq ft to tile — 48 tiles before wastage, 53 with 10%. The opening must be smaller than the wall: an opening that equals or exceeds the wall area is rejected. Small openings are often left in the estimate, because the cuts around them use up the tiles they save. For floor tiling, door openings are usually small enough to ignore.",
        "rule": "Wall mode: subtract the doors and windows — but only the large ones"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Ekzapp's guide: a standard 20 kg bag of tile adhesive, applied with a 4–6 mm notch trowel, covers approximately 40–50 sq ft on a reasonably flat surface — about 50 sq ft with a 4 mm trowel on small tiles, about 40 sq ft with a 6 mm trowel on medium tiles, and 30–35 sq ft with an 8–10 mm trowel on large-format tiles. This page uses the midpoint, 45 sq ft a bag, rounded up, and prints the range: the 120 sq ft sample needs 3 bags (3 at 50 sq ft a bag, 3 at 40). Uneven substrates and back-buttering large tiles use more — round up by a bag on an older, uneven floor. It is a rough guide, not a quote.",
        "rule": "Adhesive: a 20 kg bag covers about 40–50 sq ft"
      }
    ],
