    "rateTitle": "Third-party premium rates (IRDAI-fixed)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The formula every one of the five fetched calculators states, in the same words. Zurich Kotak prints it as 'Premium = Own Damage Premium − (No claim bonus + discounts) + Liability Premium as Fixed by the IRDAI + Cost of Add-ons', and PolicyBazaar prints the same formula. On the sample that is ₹16,000 − ₹3,200 + ₹3,416 + ₹0 + ₹750 = ₹16,966, and 18% GST on that is ₹3,054, so the year costs ₹20,020. The GST arithmetic reproduces exactly on both published examples: 18% of Zurich Kotak's ₹6,505 is ₹1,171, and 18% of PolicyBazaar's ₹12,901 is ₹2,322.",
        "rule": "Premium = own damage − no-claim bonus + third-party + add-ons + PA cover, then 18% GST"
      },
      {
        "detail": "New (under 6 months) 5%, 6 months–1 year 15%, 1–2 years 20%, 2–3 years 30%, 3–4 years 40%, 4–5 years 50% — CalcFinex and CalcWise print the same schedule. Two published figures reproduce exactly: PolicyBazaar's new Tata Nexon, ₹7,99,990 ex-showroom, has an IDV of ₹7,59,991 (5% off), and Zurich Kotak's 1–2-year-old car, ₹4,16,000 ex-showroom, has an IDV of ₹3,32,800 (20% off). Over 5 years the schedule says the IDV is by mutual agreement, so this page holds depreciation at 50% and tells you to enter the IDV directly for an older car.",
        "rule": "IDV follows the IRDAI depreciation schedule"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Up to 1000cc ₹2,094, 1000–1500cc ₹3,416, above 1500cc ₹7,897 a year, under the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance Base Premium and Liability) Rules, 2022, in force 1 June 2022. PolicyBazaar, Zurich Kotak, CalcWise and Tata AIG all print the same three figures. CalcFinex prints ₹2,072 / ₹3,221 / ₹7,890 — its mid-band figure is 6.1% low — and this page does not use them. The 3-year bundled rates are ₹6,521 / ₹10,640 / ₹24,596, which is why PolicyBazaar's Nexon example shows a third-party figure of ₹10,640. Electric cars get a 15% discount on these rates (₹2,094 × 0.85 = ₹1,780, as PolicyBazaar prints), but their premium is set by motor power in kW, so this calculator covers petrol, diesel and CNG cars only.",
        "rule": "The third-party premium is fixed — and CalcFinex's figures are wrong"
      },
      {
        "detail": "20% after 1 claim-free year, 25% after 2, 35% after 3, 45% after 4, 50% after 5 — CalcFinex and CalcWise agree, and the bonus transfers to a new car or a new insurer. On the sample, 20% of the ₹16,000 own-damage premium is ₹3,200, and the year costs ₹20,020 instead of ₹23,796 without the bonus — a saving of ₹3,776, not a fifth of the premium. A 50% bonus halves the own-damage premium and nothing else: on the ₹6 lakh, 4–5-year-old car in the presets it takes ₹3,750 off a ₹7,500 own-damage premium and saves ₹4,425 a year. Renew within 90 days of expiry to keep the bonus; a longer gap loses it entirely.",
        "rule": "The no-claim bonus discounts the own-damage premium only"
      },
      {
        "detail": "No source publishes an own-damage rate table by car age, and the two published worked examples disagree: PolicyBazaar's new Nexon implies 3.29% of the IDV (₹24,966 on ₹7,59,991) and Zurich Kotak's example implies 1.97% (₹6,556 on ₹3,32,800). CalcWise says only that it is typically 3–4% in the first year. So this page leaves the rate as an input, default 2.5% — the middle of the published 2–3% range — and anchors both examples as presets, so you can see what each rate does. At 3.29% the Nexon own-damage premium reproduces as ₹25,004 against the published ₹24,966 (+0.2%). Change the rate to your insurer's figure before trusting the total.",
        "rule": "The own-damage rate is the insurer's price — so it is an input, not a fixed table"
      },
      {
        "detail": "CalcWise prices every add-on on the own-damage base: zero depreciation 15%, engine protection 10%, return to invoice 12%, roadside assistance 5% (plus NCB protection 6%, key replacement 3% and others this page does not price). On the sample's ₹16,000 own-damage premium, all four add-ons together cost ₹6,720 and take the year from ₹20,020 to ₹27,949. CalcFinex prices the same covers differently — zero depreciation as 15–20% extra premium, engine protection as 0.5–1% of the IDV — which is why this page follows CalcWise's stated basis and says so. Add-ons apply to comprehensive policies only: a third-party-only policy has no own-damage premium for them to attach to, so ticking them changes nothing.",
        "rule": "Add-ons are a percentage of the gross own-damage premium"
      },
      {
        "detail": "If repairs after an accident cost more than 75% of the IDV, the insurer declares a total loss and pays the IDV instead of repairing the car (CalcWise). On the sample that threshold is ₹4,80,000. The same rule decides when comprehensive cover stops being worth it: once the premium climbs above roughly 8–10% of the IDV, paying to insure a low-value car's own damage may no longer make sense (CalcWise). This page applies that rule as its verdict — the ₹10,000-IDV boundary case, where the premium is over 30% of the IDV, is flagged 'consider third-party-only', while the sample at 3.13% is 'good value'. The same car on third-party-only cover costs ₹4,916 a year on the sample.",
        "rule": "Total loss at 75% of the IDV — and the 8–10% rule for old cars"
      }
    ],
