    "rateTitle": "Third-party premium rates (IRDAI-fixed)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The formula every one of the four fetched calculators states, in the same words. SMC Insurance prints it as 'Total premium = (own-damage premium − NCB discount + add-ons) + third-party premium + owner-driver personal accident cover, plus 18% GST', and PolicyBachat prints the same formula as 'Total Premium = (OD Premium + TP Premium + Add-ons) − Discounts (NCB, Anti-theft, etc.)'. On the sample that is ₹1,440 − ₹288 + ₹1,366 + ₹0 + ₹750 = ₹3,268, and 18% GST on that is ₹588, so the year costs ₹3,856. The GST arithmetic reproduces exactly on SMC's published example: 18% of ₹1,644 is ₹296.",
        "rule": "Premium = own damage − no-claim bonus + third-party + add-ons + PA cover, then 18% GST"
      },
      {
        "detail": "New (under 6 months) 5%, 6 months–1 year 15%, 1–2 years 20%, 2–3 years 30%, 3–4 years 40%, 4–5 years 50% — the same schedule the car calculator uses, and the one PolicyBazaar states as 'IDV = Ex-showroom Price − Depreciation (based on bike's age)'. SMC's worked example reproduces exactly: a 110cc scooter bought for ₹80,000, two and a half years old, has 30% depreciation and an IDV of ₹56,000. Over 5 years the schedule says the IDV is by mutual agreement, so this page holds depreciation at 50% and tells you to enter the IDV directly for an older bike.",
        "rule": "IDV follows the IRDAI depreciation schedule"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Up to 75cc ₹538, 75–150cc ₹714, 150–350cc ₹1,366, above 350cc ₹2,804 a year, under the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance Base Premium and Liability) Rules, 2022, in force 1 June 2022. SMC Insurance, BikeLeague and PolicyBazaar all print the same four figures. The five-year rates for a new two-wheeler are ₹2,901 / ₹3,851 / ₹7,365 / ₹15,117 — BikeLeague states plainly that the five-year premium is not just five times the annual rate, and the numbers confirm it: 5 × ₹538 is ₹2,690, but the published five-year rate is ₹2,901 (+7.8%). A new bike must take five years of third-party cover, following the Supreme Court order of 20 July 2018, which is why this calculator lets you pick five years: the own-damage premium and the PA cover stay one-year, and only the third-party part switches to the five-year rate.",
        "rule": "The third-party premium is fixed — and the five-year rate is not five times the annual rate"
      },
      {
        "detail": "20% after 1 claim-free year, 25% after 2, 35% after 3, 45% after 4, 50% after 5 — SMC Insurance and PolicyBachat agree, and the bonus transfers to a new bike or a new insurer. On the sample, 20% of the ₹1,440 own-damage premium is ₹288, and the year costs ₹3,856 instead of ₹4,196 without the bonus — a saving of ₹340, not a fifth of the premium. A 50% bonus halves the own-damage premium and nothing else: on the ₹1 lakh, 4–5-year-old bike in the presets it takes ₹375 off a ₹750 own-damage premium and saves ₹443 a year. Renew within 90 days of expiry to keep the bonus; a longer gap loses it entirely. One caution from the sources: PolicyBachat's Pulsar example prints a 40% NCB, which is not a standard slab — the slabs are 20/25/35/45/50 — so that example is not used as an anchor.",
        "rule": "The no-claim bonus discounts the own-damage premium only"
      },
      {
        "detail": "No source publishes an own-damage rate table by bike age, and the only published worked example anchors it at 1.5%: SMC Insurance's 110cc scooter, IDV ₹56,000, has an own-damage premium of ₹840, which is 1.5% of the IDV. So this page leaves the rate as an input, default 1.5%, range 0.5–5%. Change the rate to your insurer's figure before trusting the total — on a cheap bike the fixed third-party premium and PA cover dominate the price, so a one-point change in the OD rate moves the total far less than on a car.",
        "rule": "The own-damage rate is the insurer's price — so it is an input, not a fixed table"
      },
      {
        "detail": "CalcWise's motor basis, as used for the car calculator: zero depreciation 15%, engine protection 10%, return to invoice 12%, roadside assistance 5% of the gross own-damage premium. On the sample's ₹1,440 own-damage premium, all four add-ons together cost ₹605 and take the year from ₹3,856 to ₹4,570. SMC Insurance's worked example prices add-ons differently — a flat ₹300 — which is why its subtotal of ₹1,644 does not reproduce on this page (₹1,344 without add-ons, −18.2%); the page follows the percentage basis and says so. Add-ons apply to comprehensive policies only: a third-party-only policy has no own-damage premium for them to attach to, so ticking them changes nothing. Bikes older than 5 years usually cannot take zero depreciation at all.",
        "rule": "Add-ons are a percentage of the gross own-damage premium"
      },
      {
        "detail": "If repairs after an accident cost more than 75% of the IDV, the insurer declares a total loss and pays the IDV instead of repairing the bike. On the sample that threshold is ₹72,000. The same rule decides when comprehensive cover stops being worth it — and for bikes the bar is higher than for cars, because the fixed third-party premium and PA cover are a larger share of a cheap bike's value: this page uses 10–15% of the IDV as its rule of thumb (the car page's 8–10% would flag ordinary scooters). The ₹15,000-IDV boundary case, where the premium is 13.29% of the IDV, is flagged 'borderline', and the old low-value bike at 19.88% is flagged 'consider third-party-only', while the sample at 4.02% is 'good value'. The same bike on third-party-only cover costs ₹2,497 a year on the sample.",
        "rule": "Total loss at 75% of the IDV — and the 10–15% rule for old bikes"
      }
    ],
