    "rateTitle": "What counts as a normal cycle",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The rule every one of the five fetched calculators states, in the same words. Calculator Park prints it as 'Add your cycle length to the first day of your last period', Toolsped as 'The next period is the first day of your last period plus your cycle length', and Mr. Calco as 'Next period ≈ last period start + cycle length'. On the sample that is 1 November 2026 + 28 days = 29 November 2026. The second and third predicted periods are simply the first one plus one and two more cycle lengths — 27 December 2026 and 24 January 2027 on the sample — and this page shows six, where Mr. Calco stops at three because 'beyond that, small timing errors compound'. A 30-day cycle from the same start date gives 1 December 2026 instead (Toolsped), and a 32-day cycle gives 2 April 2026 from a 1 March start (ProbaBaby).",
        "rule": "Next period = first day of last period + cycle length"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The period length only sets the end date shown for each predicted period; it does not change the start dates, the ovulation day or the fertile window. On the sample, a 5-day period starting 29 November runs to 3 December 2026 (Calculator Website: 'runs to 4 December' for a period starting 30 November; Toolsped: 'a 5-day period starting November 29 runs through December 3'). Period length is usually 2 to 7 days (NHS); FIGO counts bleeding for more than 8 days as prolonged, and this page flags a period over 8 days and suggests seeing a doctor.",
        "rule": "Period end = period start + period length − 1 day"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The 14-day figure is the average length of the luteal phase (ovulation to next period), which is usually 12 to 16 days — so the ovulation date can sit a day or two either side of the estimate. Calculator Website, Calculator Park, Toolsped and Mr. Calco all state it the same way, and their worked examples reproduce exactly: 30 November − 14 = 16 November 2026 (Calculator Website), 8 October − 14 = 24 September (Calculator Park, 28-day), 12 October − 14 = 28 September (Calculator Park, 32-day), 29 November − 14 = 15 November 2026 (Toolsped). ProbaBaby's worked example is the one exception: it prints ovulation on 14 March 2026 for a last period of 1 March and a 28-day cycle — counting ovulation as cycle day 14 (last period + 13 days) — while this page gives 15 March, 14 days before the next period on 29 March. The one-day difference is stated on the page, and the convention used here is the one ACOG states ('ovulation occurs about 14 days before the next period').",
        "rule": "Ovulation = next period − 14 days — not 14 days after the last period"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Sperm can survive up to five days inside the body, so the five days before ovulation can lead to pregnancy, and the egg itself survives for about a day after ovulation — which is why the window ends on ovulation day itself. Calculator Park, Toolsped and ProbaBaby (citing Wilcox 1995) all use this 6-day window, and their worked examples reproduce exactly: 10–15 November 2026 for ovulation on 15 November (Toolsped), 19–24 September for ovulation on 24 September (Calculator Park). Calculator Website is the one exception: it prints a 7-day window, five days before ovulation through one day after it — 11–17 November 2026 for ovulation on 16 November — while this page gives 11–16 November. The difference is stated on the page.",
        "rule": "The fertile window is six days: the five days before ovulation plus ovulation day"
      },
      {
        "detail": "ProbaBaby's table, which cites FIGO 2018 and the NHS, and Calculator Website's sources (ACOG, Cleveland Clinic, Mayo Clinic) agree on the shape of the ranges: an average cycle of about 28 days, most adult cycles between 21 and 35 days, and up to 7 days of difference between your shortest and longest cycle (up to 9 days at ages 18–25 and 42–45) still counting as regular. This page accepts cycle lengths from 20 to 45 days — the range ProbaBaby tests its own calculator against — and flags anything outside FIGO's 24–38 days as a rough estimate, and anything under 21 or over 35 days as irregular by the NHS definition, with a suggestion to talk to a clinician.",
        "rule": "FIGO counts 24–38 days as typical; the NHS calls under 21 or over 35 days irregular"
      },
      {
        "detail": "That is a calendar fact only: it does not say why a period is late. If the predicted start date has passed, this page flags the prediction stale and asks for the first day of the most recent period, exactly as Calculator Park does ('If the date you enter is more than a cycle ago, the calculator tells you, because the predictions will already be out of date'). On the late preset — a last period of 1 October 2026 counted from 15 November — the next period was due on 29 October, 17 days ago. Stress, illness, travel, weight change, breastfeeding and some contraception can all delay a period; pregnancy is one possible reason, and a test can tell you.",
        "rule": "A period counts as late once the expected first day has passed"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Most pregnancy tests work from the first day of a missed period (NHS, via ProbaBaby), so the test day this page shows is the next period start date — 29 November 2026 on the sample. And the calendar is not contraception: ovulation moves from cycle to cycle, sperm can survive for up to 7 days (NHS, via ProbaBaby), and a calendar estimate is not reliable enough to identify safe days. Calculator Website, Calculator Park, Toolsped, Mr. Calco and ProbaBaby all say the same thing, in the same words: 'Do not use this calculator as contraception.' If you want to avoid pregnancy, use a reliable method; if you are trying to conceive and it has not happened after a year (or six months if you are over 35), talk to a doctor.",
        "rule": "A pregnancy test works from the first day of a missed period — and this is not contraception"
      }
    ],
