    "modelNote": "The method is weight × a goal rate, checked on 15 November 2026 against the five protein calculators India actually uses. Daily protein = body weight (kg) × the rate for the goal: general health (sedentary) 0.83–1.0 g/kg, active / regular exercise 1.2–1.4, muscle building 1.6–2.2, fat loss (keep muscle) 1.6–2.2, older adult (50+) 1.0–1.2, endurance athlete 1.2–1.6. The ICMR RDA figure is weight × 0.83 — for 70 kg that is 58 g. Per-meal protein is the daily figure divided by the meals per day (3, 4 or 5). Calories from protein are the daily grams × 4 kcal/g. Every gram figure is rounded to the nearest whole gram (half up — 50 × 0.83 = 41.5 g rounds to 42 g, the figure FitTrackPro prints). This page uses total body weight: ProteinIntakeCalculator's worked example uses lean body mass instead (75 kg at 20% body fat is 60 kg of lean mass, giving 96–132 g for muscle building where this page gives 120–165 g), and the difference is stated on the page. Six published figures do NOT reproduce on this page and are shown with the reason: CalcVind prints 77 g for 70 kg active while this page gives 84 g (its lower bound is 1.1 g/kg, not 1.2); FitLives prints 84 g for 'general health' while this page gives 58–70 g (its general health includes light activity at 1.2 g/kg, which is this page's active range); CalcVind and ProteinIntakeCalculator print 0.8 g/kg as the general-health lower bound (70 kg → 56 g) while this page uses the ICMR RDA 0.83 (70 kg → 58 g); MuscleBlaze caps muscle at 2.0 g/kg (70 kg → 140 g) while this page runs to 2.2 g/kg per Morton et al. 2018 (70 kg → 154 g); and FitLives' worked-example ranges are ±0.2 g/kg around a point (Priya 96–120 g, Rahul 135–165 g) while this page uses the full goal ranges (96–132 g, 120–165 g).",
    "notice": "Estimates only — not medical advice. This calculator gives a general protein target from your weight and goal. If you have kidney disease, ask your doctor first; if you are pregnant or breastfeeding, follow your doctor's advice. It is not a substitute for individual dietary advice.",
    "overview": "It turns your body weight and your goal into a daily protein target in grams. You give it your weight in kg, pick one of six goals — general health, active, muscle building, fat loss, older adult or endurance athlete — and say how many meals a day you eat, and it works out the range in grams per day, the grams per meal, the ICMR Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for your weight, and the calories that protein carries. The arithmetic is one multiplication: weight × a rate in grams per kg, where the rate depends on the goal. The baseline is the ICMR–National Institute of Nutrition figure for Indian adults, 0.83 g per kg per day; the higher goals follow the International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand. It is an estimate for planning meals, not a medical prescription — and where the published calculators disagree, this page prints the difference with the reason instead of silently following one of them.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a protein calculator do?",
