    "rateTitle": "Protein in common Indian foods",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "The six rates on this page are the ones the five fetched calculators converge on: general health (sedentary) 0.83–1.0 g/kg, active / regular exercise 1.2–1.4, muscle building 1.6–2.2, fat loss (keep muscle) 1.6–2.2, older adult (50+) 1.0–1.2, endurance athlete 1.2–1.6. FitTrackPro prints the same table for 50–100 kg (50 kg → 42 / 60–70 / 80–110 g; 70 kg → 58 / 84–98 / 112–154 g; 100 kg → 83 / 120–140 / 160–220 g) and every row reproduces exactly. ProteinIntakeCalculator prints the same six goal ranges, except its fat-loss range is 1.2–1.4 g/kg — this page uses 1.6–2.2 for fat loss, following FitTrackPro, FitLives and MuscleBlaze, because in a calorie deficit the higher range is what protects muscle. On the sample, 70 kg × 1.2–1.4 = 84–98 g a day.",
        "rule": "Daily protein = body weight × a goal rate"
      },
      {
        "detail": "ICMR–National Institute of Nutrition, Nutrient Requirements for Indians (2020): the Recommended Dietary Allowance is 0.83 g per kg per day and the Estimated Average Requirement is 0.66 g/kg. The RDA covers 97.5% of healthy adults and is designed to maintain nitrogen balance and prevent deficiency in everyday sedentary life. It works out to 54 g for the 65 kg reference man and 46 g for the 55 kg reference woman — both reproduce exactly on this page. CalcVind and ProteinIntakeCalculator print 0.8 g/kg as the general-health lower bound (70 kg → 56 g); this page uses the ICMR RDA 0.83 (70 kg → 58 g). The RDA is a minimum, not a training target.",
        "rule": "The ICMR baseline is 0.83 g/kg — the EAR is 0.66 g/kg"
      },
      {
        "detail": "ICMR–NIN 2020 carries a footnote MuscleBlaze quotes: for people eating cereal-based diets with lower-quality protein, the requirement rises to around 1 g/kg per day, because cereals like rice and roti are lower in digestibility and amino-acid completeness than animal protein. ICMR–NIN also recommends that at least 66% of daily protein come from quality sources — pulses, dairy, nuts and eggs — not just cereals. NSO Report No. 594 (2025) puts it bluntly: Indian diets derive close to 60% of their protein from cereals. That is why the general-health range on this page runs 0.83–1.0, and why a vegetarian eating mostly roti, rice and a small bowl of dal should aim at the top of it.",
        "rule": "Cereal-based Indian diets need at least 1 g/kg — and 66% from quality sources"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Morton et al., British Journal of Sports Medicine, 2018 — a systematic review of 49 resistance-training studies, cited by FitTrackPro and FitLives — found the benefits of protein on muscle growth plateaued around 1.62 g/kg/day, with the upper confidence range extending to about 2.2 g/kg/day. That is why this page prints a range, not one number, for muscle building (1.6–2.2 g/kg) and fat loss. MuscleBlaze caps muscle at 2.0 g/kg (70 kg → 140 g); this page runs to 2.2 g/kg (70 kg → 154 g) on the Morton evidence. Total calories matter too: without enough calories, the body burns dietary protein for energy instead of building muscle.",
        "rule": "Muscle gains plateau around 1.62 g/kg — the range runs to 2.2"
      },
      {
        "detail": "CalcVind: your muscles use protein best in servings of about 20–40 g, and three or four protein-rich meals beat one large one. FitLives: spreading protein across the day supports muscle protein synthesis better than concentrating it in one meal, at roughly 0.4 g/kg a meal. Total daily intake matters more than exact timing — but the per-meal figure is what makes the target practical: the sample's 84–98 g a day is 28–33 g across 3 meals. FitLives' Priya (60 kg, fat loss) is 108 g a day, about 36 g a meal; Rahul (75 kg, muscle) is 150 g a day, about 38 g across 4 meals — both points sit inside this page's ranges.",
        "rule": "Split it across 3–5 meals, about 20–40 g each"
      },
      {
        "detail": "ProteinIntakeCalculator's worked example uses lean body mass: a 75 kg person with 20% body fat has 60 kg of lean mass, and at 1.6–2.2 g/kg that gives 96–132 g a day, 24–33 g across 4 meals. This page uses total body weight and gives 120–165 g a day, 30–41 g a meal for the same person — 25% higher, because 75 kg of total weight is not 60 kg of lean mass. FitTrackPro and MuscleBlaze give the same advice in words: if you carry significant extra weight, base the calculation on your target healthy weight. If you know your body fat percentage, the lean-mass figure is the more accurate one; this calculator does not ask for it, so use your target weight instead.",
        "rule": "This page uses total body weight — lean mass is a different number"
      },
      {
        "detail": "CalcVind's active lower bound is 1.1 g/kg, not 1.2 (70 kg → 77 g, where this page gives 84 g) and its fat-loss ceiling is 'up to 2.4 g/kg' (this page uses 2.2). FitLives labels 1.2 g/kg as 'general health, light activity' (70 kg → 84 g); this page keeps general health at the ICMR 0.83–1.0 (70 kg → 58–70 g) and puts 84 g at the bottom of its active range. ProteinIntakeCalculator's fat-loss range is 1.2–1.4 g/kg (this page uses 1.6–2.2). MuscleBlaze caps muscle at 2.0 g/kg. And FitLives' worked-example ranges are ±0.2 g/kg around a point (Priya 96–120 g, Rahul 135–165 g) while this page uses the full goal ranges (96–132 g, 120–165 g). None of these is an error in either calculator — they are different choices at the edges, and the page states each one rather than hiding it.",
        "rule": "The ranges disagree at the edges — and this page prints where"
      }
    ],
