    "rateTitle": "Brick sizes, mortar and the constants behind the answer",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Wall volume ÷ the volume of one brick plus its 10 mm mortar joint on three faces. CivilSite, RupeesCalc and NineClip all use this volume method, and their worked examples reproduce on this page: CivilSite's 30 m² wall gives 2,995 bricks (3,145 to order); RupeesCalc's 10 × 3 m wall gives 2,990 (3,140 to order); NineClip's 10 × 10 ft wall gives 833 (875 to order). A per-sq-ft shortcut hides the brick size — the same wall needs 833 traditional bricks or 1,062 modular ones.",
        "rule": "The count comes from the wall volume, not a per-sq-ft shortcut"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Each brick occupies its own size plus a 10 mm mortar joint on three faces: traditional 230 × 115 × 75 mm becomes a 240 × 125 × 85 mm module (0.00255 m³, 392 bricks per m³); modular 190 × 90 × 90 mm becomes 200 × 100 × 100 mm (0.002 m³, 500 per m³); traditional 230 × 110 × 70 mm becomes 240 × 120 × 80 mm (0.002304 m³, 434 per m³); jumbo 300 × 150 × 100 mm becomes 310 × 160 × 110 mm (0.005456 m³, 183 per m³). Dividing by the solid brick volume alone would overstate the count — the joint space has to be reserved first.",
        "rule": "One brick plus its 10 mm joint is the module"
      },
      {
        "detail": "In feet mode the thickness is the exact inch value: 9 in = 228.6 mm, so a 10 × 10 ft wall is exactly 75 cft = 2.12 m³ — NineClip's figure. In metres mode the thickness is the nominal value: 230 mm, so a 10 × 3 m wall is 6.90 m³ — CivilSite's figure. The two conventions differ by 0.6% on the thickness, which is why NineClip's partition example (362 bricks) reproduces only when the exact-inch thickness is used.",
        "rule": "Feet use exact inches; metres use nominal millimetres"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The wastage % you enter is added to the brick count and rounded up — 833 × 1.05 = 875 on the sample. It is NOT added to the mortar: RupeesCalc's worked example prints 7.55 bags of cement and 1.57 m³ of sand with 5% brick wastage, and both reproduce exactly only when the mortar carries no extra allowance. CivilSite adds a separate 5% mortar allowance (9.18 bags, 10 to buy, 1.91 m³ of sand) and NineClip adds 3% on cement and 10% on sand (2.7 bags, 20.9 cft) — their after-allowance figures are printed on this page with the reason, not silently followed.",
        "rule": "Wastage applies to bricks only"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Loose cement and sand occupy more volume before mixing than the mortar does in the wall. CivilSite, RupeesCalc and NineClip all multiply the wet mortar volume by 1.33 to get the dry volume, then split it by the mix ratio. On the sample: 0.47 m³ wet × 1.33 = 0.63 m³ dry, of which one part in seven is cement (2.58 bags) and six parts are sand (0.54 m³, 19.0 cft). The 1.33 factor is an estimating convention, not a mix-design clause — CivilSite says so itself.",
        "rule": "Dry mortar is wet mortar × 1.33"
      },
      {
        "detail": "NineClip's convention, and standard Indian practice: 1:6 (one part cement to six parts sand) for 9-inch load-bearing and external walls; 4.5-inch partitions use a richer 1:4 because the thin wall relies more on mortar strength. A richer mix than needed wastes cement and can increase shrinkage cracking, so more cement is not automatically better. OnsiteTeams prints the reverse (1:6 internal, 1:4 external) — this page follows NineClip and the standard practice, and says so.",
        "rule": "1:6 for 9-inch walls, 1:4 for 4.5-inch partitions"
      },
      {
        "detail": "OnsiteTeams counts bricks per face area: one brick per 200 × 100 mm face module per leaf — 50 per m² for a 4.5-inch wall, 100 for 9-inch, 150 for 13.5-inch. This page uses the volume method, which adds the 10 mm joint on all three faces, so for modular bricks it gives 57.5, 115 and 172.5 per m² — exactly 15% more. Neither is wrong: the face method is exact for a single leaf of stretcher bond, the volume method is what CivilSite, RupeesCalc and NineClip publish and what their worked examples reproduce. This page uses the volume method and prints the face-method figures with the reason.",
        "rule": "The face method counts fewer bricks — and this page prints where"
      }
    ],
