    "mistakesTitle": "Common steel weight calculator mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The method is the D² ÷ 162 rule, checked on 15 November 2026 against the six steel calculators India actually uses. A TMT bar's weight per metre is its diameter in millimetres squared, divided by 162 — the shortcut for (π ÷ 4) × diameter² × 7,850 kg/m³, the density of steel. This page uses the site convention D² ÷ 162. The exact-density divisor is 162.2: a 12 mm × 12 m bar gives 10.65 kg instead of 10.67 kg, and TheCivilStudies prints 10.66 kg because it multiplies the rounded 0.888 kg/m by 12. An MS plate weighs length (m) × width (m) × thickness (mm) × 7.85 kg — 7.85 kg per square metre for every millimetre of thickness. An MS pipe weighs π × (outside diameter − wall thickness) × wall thickness × 0.00785 kg per metre — the same as (OD − t) × t × 0.02466, the form ERP Group prints; the two forms agree to 0.001 kg/m on the worked pipe example. Pieces per tonne are 1,000 ÷ kg per piece, rounded DOWN — Vystra rounds instead, so it prints 94 bars for 12 mm where this page gives 93. Cost is total kg × the rate you enter; the page prints no fixed steel price, because rates vary by brand, diameter and region. Grade (Fe415, Fe500, Fe550) affects strength, not weight. Published figures that do NOT reproduce on this page are shown with the reason: TheCivilStudies' chart figures (0.888 kg/m and 10.66 kg per 12 m bar for 12 mm; 7.40 kg for a 10 mm 12 m bar; 90 bars = 959 kg) use the rounded kg/m or the 162.2 divisor; Ekzapp's chart kg/m figures for 12 mm (0.888) and 16 mm (1.578) use the 162.2 divisor, its per-12-m figures for 6/10/12/16 mm come from the rounded kg/m, and its 20/25/32/36/40 mm rows match this page; and Vystra's bars-per-tonne figures (94 for 12 mm, 211 for 8 mm, 53 for 16 mm, 34 for 20 mm, 22 for 25 mm) round instead of rounding down.",
    "notice": "Theoretical (nominal) weights for estimation, not a bill of quantities. Actual bars vary within the rolling tolerances in IS 1786 — weigh a delivery before billing by weight. No fixed steel price is printed on this page — rates vary by brand, diameter and region.",
    "overview": "It turns a bar, plate or pipe size into a weight you can order and pay for. Steel is priced and delivered by weight, not by length or piece count, so every reinforcement order comes down to one conversion: diameter and length into kilograms. You pick the shape, enter the size and how many pieces you need, and it works out the weight per metre, the weight of one piece, the total in kg and tonnes, and how many pieces of that size make one tonne — with the cost if you enter your own ₹ per kg rate. The arithmetic is the D² ÷ 162 rule the Indian site calculators use for bars, length × width × thickness × 7.85 for plates, and π × (OD − t) × t × 0.00785 for pipes. It is an estimate for ordering steel, not a structural design — and where the published calculators disagree, this page prints the difference with the reason instead of silently following one of them.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a steel weight calculator do?",
