    "rateTitle": "TMT bar weight chart: 6 mm to 40 mm (D² ÷ 162)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Weight per metre in kg = diameter in mm squared, divided by 162. It is the shortcut for (π ÷ 4) × diameter² × 7,850 kg/m³, the density of steel: (π ÷ 4) × 7,850 ÷ 1,000,000 = 1 ÷ 162.2, and 162 is the rounded divisor the site calculators use. ERP Group, Ekzapp, CivilSolution, Vystra and Plotr all print D² ÷ 162; TheCivilStudies prints the same formula in its headline and then a 162.2 chart. This page uses 162, so its 12 mm bar is 0.889 kg/m and 10.67 kg per 12 m bar — ERP Group's figures, reproduced exactly. The 162.2 figures (0.888 kg/m, 10.65 kg per bar) are 0.1% lower and are stated on the page.",
        "rule": "Bars use D² ÷ 162 on this page"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Fe415, Fe500 and Fe550 describe the bar's yield strength — 415, 500 and 550 N/mm². The weight depends only on the diameter and the density of steel, so a 12 mm Fe500 bar weighs exactly what a 12 mm Fe415 bar weighs: 0.889 kg/m. Ekzapp says the same: the formula is universal across grades because grade affects yield strength, not the physical volume-to-weight relationship. Do not order by grade expecting a different weight.",
        "rule": "Grade affects strength, not weight"
      },
      {
        "detail": "IS 1786 is the Bureau of Indian Standards specification for high-strength deformed steel bars for concrete reinforcement, and its Table 1 lists a nominal mass per metre for each size, taking steel density as 7,850 kg/m³. TheCivilStudies states that the nominal mass per metre in IS 1786 matches the D² ÷ 162 formula, and ERP Group cites IS 1786 as its source. The standard allows tolerances on bar mass, so a weighed delivery can differ a little from the theoretical weight — which is why the page says to weigh a delivery before billing by weight.",
        "rule": "The chart matches the IS 1786 nominal mass"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Weight in kg = length (m) × width (m) × thickness (mm) × 7.85. A 10 mm plate weighs 78.50 kg/m², so a standard 1.25 × 2.5 m sheet (3.125 m²) weighs 245.31 kg — CivilSolution's worked example, reproduced exactly (it prints 245.31 kg). ERP Group's chart gives the same: 122.7 kg for a 5 mm sheet and 245.3 kg for a 10 mm sheet, both reproducing at one decimal place. The rule of thumb is the same thing: an MS plate weighs 7.85 kg per square metre for every millimetre of thickness.",
        "rule": "Plates weigh 7.85 kg per square metre per millimetre"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Weight per metre in kg = π × (outside diameter − wall thickness) × wall thickness × 0.00785, with the diameters in millimetres. CivilSolution prints this form; ERP Group prints the equivalent (OD − t) × t × 0.02466, because π × 0.00785 = 0.02466. On the worked pipe (100 mm OD, 5 mm wall) the two forms give 11.714 and 11.714 kg/m — they agree to 0.001 kg/m. The wall thickness must be less than half the outside diameter, or there is no bore left — the calculator rejects it.",
        "rule": "Pipes use π × (OD − t) × t × 0.00785"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Bars per tonne = 1,000 ÷ kg per piece, rounded down — you cannot sell a fraction of a bar, and the count is the number of whole pieces that fit in a tonne. TheCivilStudies states this convention in its chart ('Bars per tonne are rounded down') and ERP Group follows it: 93 bars of 12 mm × 12 m, 375 of 6 mm, 210 of 8 mm — every row reproduces. Vystra rounds instead, so it prints 94 for 12 mm, 211 for 8 mm, 53 for 16 mm, 34 for 20 mm and 22 for 25 mm — each one more than this page, and each stated on the page with the reason.",
        "rule": "Pieces per tonne are rounded down"
      },
      {
        "detail": "These are nominal weights from the nominal diameter. Rolling tolerances in IS 1786 mean a real bar can be a little heavier or lighter, and TheCivilStudies' delivery check shows why it matters: 90 bars of 12 mm × 12 m weigh 960 kg by this page's unrounded figure, against a billed tonne — about 40 kg short either way. Weigh a few sample bars before paying by weight. And add 3–5% for laps and cutting waste (Ekzapp's allowance: 2–3% for straight slab bars, 4–5% for columns, 5–8% for stirrups) — the calculator gives the theoretical weight; the order needs the allowance on top.",
        "rule": "Theoretical weight is not the billed weight"
      }
    ],
