    "rateTitle": "Cement bags per cubic metre of concrete (M5–M25)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Wet volume × 1.54 gives the dry volume — wet concrete shrinks as it sets and the voids between the sand and aggregate have to be filled. TheCivilStudies, NineClip, DevCalc, Whiz.tools and Construction Estimator India all use 1.54 (textbooks give 1.52–1.57). CalculatorLib's default formula leaves it out: its 2 m³ 1:2:4 example gives 8.23 bags where this page gives 12.68 — about 35% less — and it admits the omission on its own page.",
        "rule": "Concrete uses the dry-volume method (× 1.54)"
      },
      {
        "detail": "IS 456:2000 Table 9 lists the nominal mixes: M5 1:5:10, M7.5 1:4:8, M10 1:3:6, M15 1:2:4 and M20 1:1.5:3. M5 is 1:5:10 — TheCivilStudies' table gives 2.8 bags per m³ for M5, which only 1:5:10 reproduces (1:4:8 would give 3.41). CalculatorLib prints the same table. M25 has no nominal mix in Table 9.",
        "rule": "Nominal mixes come from IS 456:2000 Table 9 — M5 is 1:5:10"
      },
      {
        "detail": "IS 456:2000 cl. 9.1.1 and Table 9 allow nominal mixes only up to M20; M25 and above need a design mix to IS 10262:2019, based on actual materials and trials. A design mix usually needs only about 340–400 kg of cement per m³ (7–8 bags), against the 554 kg that 1:1:2 gives — and 1:1:2 is above the 450 kg/m³ ceiling in IS 456 cl. 8.2.4.2. Construction Estimator India prints 8.6 bags/m³ for M25, which is inconsistent with its own 1:1:2 ratio (11.09) — that figure is not used. This page shows the M25 nominal figures for reference and carries the warning on every M25 result.",
        "rule": "IS 456 allows nominal mixes only up to M20"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Cement bulk density is 1,440 kg/m³ (IS 875 Part 1), so one 50 kg bag is 50 ÷ 1,440 = 0.0347 m³ (1.23 cft). Bags = cement volume ÷ 0.0347. The weight path (cement kg ÷ 50) gives a fraction less — 8.06 bags/m³ for M20 against 8.07 by volume — because 0.0347 × 1,440 = 49.97 kg, not quite 50. TheCivilStudies prints 8.06 in its worked example and 8.1 in its table; NineClip prints 8.07. Whiz.tools counts 40 kg bags, so its bag counts are not directly comparable to Indian 50 kg figures.",
        "rule": "One bag is 50 kg — 0.0347 m³ at 1,440 kg/m³"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Wet mortar is the wall area × thickness; dry mortar is wet × 1.33 — the voids and shrinkage factor every source uses for mortar (NineClip, and the site's Brick Calculator). NineClip's figure for 100 sq ft at 12 mm in 1:6 is 0.61 bags, 0.63 with its 3% wastage allowance — this page gives 0.61 and 0.63 exactly. Charts quoting 3–4 bags per 100 sq ft are about five times the dry-volume figure (0.61–0.85 bags), and no fetched source states them.",
        "rule": "Plaster uses the dry-mortar factor 1.33"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Mortar is not a flat allowance per cubic metre of wall — it is the wall volume less the solid brick volume. With modular 190 × 90 × 90 mm bricks and a 10 mm joint, a cubic metre of brickwork holds 0.2305 m³ of mortar. That gives 0.25 bags per 100 bricks at 1:6 (2.52 bags per 1,000). The figure sometimes quoted online, 1.25 bags per 100 bricks at 1:6, would need about 1.15 m³ of mortar per m³ of brickwork — more mortar than the wall can hold. The site's Brick Calculator gives the same mortar by the same volume method.",
        "rule": "Brickwork mortar is worked out by volume, not by a flat allowance"
      },
      {
        "detail": "This page gives 8.07 bags/m³ for M20 by the volumetric method (1.54 ÷ 5.5 ÷ 0.0347). CPWD rate analysis gives 8.00 bags (4.00 quintal/m³) and IS 10067:1982 gives 7.33 bags — a spread of about 10%, stated explicitly by NineClip. The three figures come from different conventions: the volumetric method, a rate-analysis allowance and a standard consumption figure. This page uses the volumetric method and prints all three, so an order checked against any of them is within 10%.",
        "rule": "M20 has three published answers — 8.07, 8.00 and 7.33"
      }
    ],
