    "mistakesTitle": "Common water tank calculator mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The arithmetic on this page was checked against the seven water-tank calculators India actually uses. For sizing, the daily demand is people × litres per person per day (135 is the IS 1172:1993 / NBC norm for a house with flushing toilets); storage is daily demand × days of storage; the overhead tank holds at least half a day (NBC cl. 4.4.10, IS 2065 cl. 11.11); the sump holds the rest; and each tank is rounded up to the next ready-made size — 500, 750, 1,000, 1,500, 2,000, 3,000, 5,000 or 10,000 litres. Above 10,000 litres the buy is rounded up to the next 5,000-litre multiple. For capacity, the geometric volume is length × width × height (rectangular) or π × (diameter/2)² × height (cylindrical), in metres — feet convert at 0.3048 m/ft — and litres are m³ × 1,000 (1 cft = 28.32 litres). The usable depth is the height less the freeboard (default 0.15 m) and the water below the outlet (default 0.05 m); usable litres are plan area × usable depth × 1,000. Water weighs about 1 kg a litre. Cost is litres to buy × the rate you enter; the page prints no fixed tank price. Published figures that do NOT reproduce on this page are shown with the reason: NirmanShastra's piped table uses a third of a day overhead (draft NBC) where this page uses the half-day minimum, so for 8 or more people this page's overhead tank is one ready-made size larger (750 vs 500 litres for 8 people) and its one-day sump is smaller (500 vs 1,000 litres for 6 people); InteriorCalculator sizes the overhead tank at a full day (750 litres for 5 people) against this page's 500 litres; Sintex's single 750 litre tank for 4 people on daily supply against this page's two tanks totalling 1,000 litres; Bansal's two 750 litre tanks (1,500 litres) for 5 people × 2 days against this page's 500 + 1,500 = 2,000 litres, because each tank rounds up separately; and CivilSite's storage target adds a 10% reserve (1,485 litres for 5 people × 2 days) where this page has no reserve input and the rounding-up to ready-made sizes provides the spare. The brief's parenthetical 1,020.5 litres for the CivilSite cylinder came from π ≈ 3.14 — this page uses full π and gives 1,021.02 litres, matching CivilSite's printed 1,021.",
    "notice": "Domestic storage only — fire-fighting water is separate. No fixed tank price is printed on this page — rates vary by brand and city.",
    "overview": "It turns the people in a house into the two tanks to buy — or a measured tank into the litres you can actually use. A house uses about 135 litres of water a person a day (IS 1172:1993), so a family of five needs 675 litres a day. The calculator works out the storage for the days you choose, puts at least half a day on the roof (NBC cl. 4.4.10) and the rest in the sump, and rounds each tank up to the next ready-made size — 500, 750, 1,000, 1,500, 2,000, 3,000, 5,000 or 10,000 litres. In the second mode it works out a tank's capacity from its inside dimensions: the geometric volume, then the usable volume after the freeboard and the water below the outlet are taken off. It is an estimate for ordering and planning, not a plumbing design — and where the published calculators disagree, this page prints the difference with the reason instead of silently following one of them.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a water tank calculator do?",
