    "rateTitle": "Ready-made tank sizes — what each size covers (at 135 litres a person)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "All seven fetched sources use 135 litres per person per day for a house with flushing toilets, attributed to IS 1172:1993 and/or NBC/CPHEEO and the MoHUA 2024 Manual Part A, which describes 135 as a minimum. It is a norm, not a measurement: NineClip suggests 180 for a big-city house with a flush toilet and a shower in every bathroom, InteriorCalculator gives 70–100 litres where there is no sewerage system, and NirmanShastra uses 150 for a house on its own borewell — flagged as its own choice, not the standard's. The 135-litre breakdown differs between sources: Bansal gives drinking, cooking and bathing 70 / flushing 30 / clothes and house washing 35, while NirmanShastra gives flushing 45 and 90 for everything else (5 drinking, 5 cooking, 30 bathing, 25 laundry, 25 utensils and floors). This page prints Bansal's table and notes NirmanShastra's split.",
        "rule": "135 litres a person a day is the IS 1172:1993 / NBC norm"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Where supply is intermittent, NBC cl. 4.4.10 asks for at least half a day's supply in the overhead tank, and IS 2065 cl. 11.11 says the same. Half of 675 litres is 337.5, so a 500 litre tank. NirmanShastra's piped table uses a third of a day from the NBC Part 9 draft (Nov 2024) — for 8 or more people this page's overhead tank is one ready-made size larger (750 vs 500 litres for 8 people). Sintex and InteriorCalculator use a full day overhead (750 litres for 5 people) — a valid, more generous choice. CivilSite refuses a fixed split and sizes each tank from the actual pumping and supply strategy.",
        "rule": "The overhead tank holds at least half a day (NBC cl. 4.4.10)"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Storage less the overhead share goes in the sump: for 2 days, a day and a half. For one day's storage the NBC note to cl. 4.4.10 puts 66.6% underground and 33.4% overhead (450 and 225 litres for a family of five). NineClip's rules: keep the sump at least as big as one day's demand, and never build the sump smaller than the roof tank. The NBC note sizes an underground tank on its own at 50 to 150% of a day's demand.",
        "rule": "The sump holds the rest of the storage"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Each tank is rounded up to the next size that is sold, never down. The ladder is 500, 750, 1,000, 1,500, 2,000, 3,000, 5,000 and 10,000 litres. NirmanShastra lists 500/1,000/1,500/2,000/3,000/5,000 and states plainly that no standard lists them, so check what your supplier stocks. The 750 litre size is attested by NineClip ('500, 750, 1,000 litres and up'), InteriorCalculator (675 L rounds up to 750 L) and Bansal (standard capacities 500, 750, 1,000). Above 10,000 litres the calculator rounds up to the next 5,000-litre multiple; above 5,000 litres underground the tank is usually built, and NBC cl. 4.4.5 splits storage over 5,000 litres so one tank can be cleaned while the other supplies the house.",
        "rule": "Tanks are bought at the next ready-made size up, never down"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Usable depth = internal height − freeboard − unusable depth below the draw-off outlet. CivilSite's worked-example defaults are 0.15 m freeboard and 0.05 m below the outlet: a 2 × 1 × 1 m tank is 2,000 litres geometric and 1,600 litres usable (0.80 m usable depth), and a 1 m diameter × 1.5 m cylinder is 1,021 usable litres — both reproduce exactly on this page. Engineering Nepal puts freeboard usually at 150 to 300 mm; NineClip puts the outlet 50 to 75 mm above the tank floor (NBC cl. 4.4.6), and the water below it stays where it is. InteriorCalculator uses 0.3 m freeboard on its sump.",
        "rule": "Usable capacity is less than geometric capacity"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Litres = volume in m³ × 1,000 (1 m³ = 1,000 litres), and 1 cft = 28.32 litres (Engineering Nepal's rounded figure; the exact figure is 28.3168). Engineering Nepal's worked examples reproduce: an 8 × 6 ft tank with 5 ft of water is 240 cft = 6,796 litres, and a 5 × 5 × 5 ft tank is 125 cft × 28.32 = 3,540 litres. Rectangular volume = length × width × water depth; round volume = π/4 × diameter² × water depth.",
        "rule": "One cubic metre is 1,000 litres; one cubic foot is 28.32 litres"
      },
      {
        "detail": "One usable litre of water has a mass of approximately one kilogram, so a 1,000 L usable volume means about 1,000 kg of water, before tank self-weight (CivilSite). Its roof-load table runs 500 L = 500 kg (half a tonne), 1,000 L = 1 tonne, 2,000 L = 2 tonnes, 5,000 L = 5 tonnes — needs a designed support. NineClip: a full 1,000 litre tank weighs about a tonne with its water. NBC cl. 4.4.8 asks for 2 m of head from the tank outlet down to the highest tap or shower. Roof placement and sump structure need a licensed engineer.",
        "rule": "Water weighs about 1 kg a litre — a full tank is heavy"
      }
    ],
