    "rateTitle": "Steps for common floor heights (175 mm riser, 250 mm tread)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Risers = ceiling of height ÷ target riser. TheCivilStudies states it plainly — 'round UP' — and NineClip, InteriorCalculator and Calcvind all round up too: 3,000 ÷ 175 = 17.14 → 18 risers; 3,048 ÷ 190 = 16.04 → 17 risers; 3,048 ÷ 170 = 17.93 → 18 risers. Only Fingtaj rounds to the nearest whole number (3,000 ÷ 165 = 18.18 → 18 risers of 166.7 mm, where this page gives 19 of 157.9 mm). Rounding up never makes a step steeper than the target you entered.",
        "rule": "Risers are rounded up, never down"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Actual riser = height ÷ risers, so every step is exactly the same height — 3,000 ÷ 18 = 166.7 mm. Fingtaj's rule: all risers in a flight must be exactly equal, because small differences are a trip hazard. TheCivilStudies' mistake list says the same: unequal risers happen when the floor finish thickness is ignored, so measure finished floor to finished floor.",
        "rule": "Every riser is exactly the same height"
      },
      {
        "detail": "2R + T should be roughly 600 to 650 mm for a natural walking stride — the Blondel formula. TheCivilStudies, Infralens and Fingtaj's FAQ all use 600–650 mm, with a table of combinations: 150 × 300 → 600, 165 × 280 → 610, 175 × 275 → 625, 190 × 250 → 630. InteriorCalculator uses a wider 550–700 mm band, so a 583 mm stair reads comfortable there and a little tight here. For children and older family members, 150 mm rise with 300 mm tread is the gold standard.",
        "rule": "2R + T of 600–650 mm is comfortable"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The Model Building Bye-Laws 2016, Town and Country Planning Organisation, Ministry of Urban Development — read in the bye-laws' own text: cl. 4.17.2 least width 1.0 m for a dwelling (0.75 m for two-storey row housing); cl. 4.17.3 least tread without nosing 250 mm for residential buildings, 300 mm for other buildings; cl. 4.17.4.1 largest riser 190 mm for residential buildings, 150 mm for other buildings, limited to 12 per flight; cl. 4.17.4.2 minimum headroom 2.2 m in a passage under a landing and in any staircase; cl. 5.4.3 for high-rise buildings: tread 0.25 m, riser 0.19 m, 15 risers per flight, handrail 0.9 m, headroom 2.2 m. NBC 2016 Part 3 asks for 2.1 m of headroom (cl. 17.5.5) and 900 mm for a single-family internal stair — both are looser than the bye-laws, so this page flags the gap rather than hiding it.",
        "rule": "The bye-law limits: riser 190, tread 250, 12 a flight, width 1.0 m, headroom 2.2 m"
      },
      {
        "detail": "TheCivilStudies cites the NBC limit as 15 risers per flight; Infralens cites NBC 2016 Part 3 as 12. The Model Bye-Laws say 12 generally (cl. 4.17.4.1) and 15 in high-rise buildings (cl. 5.4.3). This page follows the bye-laws: 12 or fewer passes, 13–15 is flagged 'check — over the 12-per-flight bye-law limit, allowed only in high-rise (15)', and anything over 15 fails both limits. Your state's notified bye-laws can differ — check them.",
        "rule": "NBC and the bye-laws do not quite agree on risers per flight"
      },
      {
        "detail": "A landing is at least as deep as the stair is wide — Infralens's rule, and NBC's. This page takes the landing depth as the stair width, so a 1.0 m stair gets a 1.0 m landing and a 2.0 m going plus a 1.0 m landing makes a 3.0 m room. Infralens's worked example uses a 1.2 m landing on a 1.1 m stair, so its room is 3.4 m long where this page gives 3.3 m — a deeper landing is always allowed; a shallower one is not. The gap between the two flights of a dog-legged stair is 0.1 m (TheCivilStudies).",
        "rule": "A landing is at least as deep as the stair is wide"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Concrete = waist slab + steps + landing. Inclined length = √(going² + flight rise²) — TheCivilStudies' formula: √(2.0² + 1.5²) = 2.5 m a flight for the sample stair. Waist = inclined length × width × waist thickness; steps = treads × ½ × R × T × width; landing = landing depth × landing span × waist thickness. Materials follow the same dry-volume method as this site's Concrete Calculator: dry volume = wet × 1.54, mixes M15 1:2:4, M20 1:1.5:3, M25 1:1:2, one 50 kg bag = 0.0347 m³. Steel is the 1% thumb rule — 110 kg for the sample stair's 1.40 m³ — not a bar-bending schedule; Infralens designs the steel properly at 550 mm²/m for its 3 m stair.",
        "rule": "Concrete is waist + steps + landing, by the dry-volume method"
      }
    ],
