    "faq": [
      [
        "How many rolls of wallpaper do I need for a 10×10 wall?",
        "2 rolls of a standard 21 in × 33 ft roll at 50 sq ft usable coverage — 100 sq ft of wall, 6 strips, 3 strips per roll. Buy 3 with the spare roll. At Magicdecor's ~38 sq ft coverage the same wall needs 3 rolls on the area method."
      ],
      [
        "How much does wallpaper cost per sq ft in India?",
        "₹50–₹600+ a sq ft for the paper alone in 2026 (Asian Paints: vinyl ₹50–200, non-woven ₹75–250, fabric ₹100–300, 3D ₹200–500, designer ₹250–600+), plus ₹8–₹30 a sq ft for professional fitting. The sample room works out at ₹197 a sq ft typically, all in."
      ],
      [
        "What is the standard wallpaper roll size in India?",
        "21 in wide × 33 ft long — 57.75 sq ft gross. The usable coverage is less, about 50 sq ft after trimming and pattern matching (Asian Paints: one roll covers about 50 sq ft; HouseYog: 50–60 sq ft). Always confirm the labelled coverage before ordering, and enter it in the calculator."
      ],
      [
        "What is pattern repeat, and why does it need more wallpaper?",
        "The repeat is the vertical distance between two identical motifs — 53 cm and 64 cm in NRTechStudio's worked examples. Every strip must be cut so the motif lines up at the seam, so the cut length rounds up to whole repeats and up to one full repeat per strip is wasted. A large repeat adds 15% waste on this page; a half-drop pattern needs roughly 15–25% more rolls than the same repeat on a straight match."
      ],
      [
        "Why does this calculator give two roll counts?",
        "The area method divides the paper area by the coverage per roll; the strip method counts physical strips and how many fit on a roll. They disagree on narrow walls — a 10×10 ft bedroom is 6 rolls by area and 8 by strips — because the strip method sees the paper wasted at the roll ends. This page orders the larger figure, so you do not run short."
      ],
      [
        "How much does wallpaper installation cost in India?",
        "The sources disagree: HouseYog says ₹8–₹15 a sq ft, Asian Paints says installation starts at around ₹30 a sq ft. This page uses ₹8–₹30, typical ₹19 (the midpoint), and shows the fitting as its own line so you can see it. Fitting it yourself costs ₹0 here — the paper still costs the same."
      ],
      [
        "Should I subtract doors and windows?",
        "Subtract their area from the total — this page does. But be careful with strips: you still need full-height strips on both sides of a window, plus short pieces above and below it, so a window rarely saves a whole strip. Only openings that run nearly full height (at least 90% of the wall, like sliding doors) save whole strips — enter those separately as full-height width."
      ],
      [
        "How much extra wallpaper should I order?",
        "One spare roll, from the same dye lot as the rest — colours drift slightly between print runs, so a later reorder can show a visible shade difference. The pattern waste is already in the roll count (10% for a small repeat, 15% for a large one), so do not add another percentage on top."
      ],
      [
        "Is wallpaper cheaper than paint?",
        "No — paint is cheaper upfront. HouseYog puts emulsion paint at ₹20–₹40 a sq ft including labour against wallpaper at ₹60–₹500 a sq ft including fitting. Wallpaper wins on finish and longevity (a good paper lasts up to 15 years, Asian Paints; 5–7 years for a standard paper, HouseYog) — which is why most people paper one or two feature walls and paint the rest."
      ],
      [
        "Can I use this calculator for a sloped or staircase wall?",
        "As a starting estimate only. Measure the maximum height and maximum width, as if drawing a box around the wall, and use those figures — Magicdecor and NRTechStudio both recommend the bounding-box method for sloped walls. For a staircase, use the longest drop from the highest ceiling point to the lowest stair as the wall height."
      ]
    ],
