    "rateTitle": "Wallpaper rates per sq ft in India (2026)",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources disagree, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Asian Paints' 2026 material table is the anchor: vinyl ₹50–200, non-woven ₹75–250, fabric ₹100–300, 3D ₹200–500, designer ₹250–600+ per sq ft, with the typical at the midpoint. The material sets the rate before any pattern is chosen — designer paper costs about 3.4× vinyl for the same wall (₹425 against ₹125 a sq ft typically). HouseYog's bands agree in shape (basic ₹50–100, premium up to ₹300–500), and the page prints both sources so the spread is visible.",
        "rule": "The material sets the rate"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The standard Indian roll is 21 in wide × 33 ft long — 57.75 sq ft gross. The usable coverage is less, about 50 sq ft after trimming and pattern matching (Asian Paints: one roll covers about 50 sq ft; HouseYog: 50–60 sq ft). Magicdecor's own rolls cover ~38 sq ft, so the coverage is a user input on this page (10–100 sq ft), default 50 — never a fixed number the page invented.",
        "rule": "A roll is 57.75 sq ft gross, about 50 usable"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Two methods, order the larger. The area method divides the adjusted area by the coverage per roll; the strip method counts physical strips (perimeter ÷ roll width, rounded up), cut length (height + 4 in trim, rounded up to whole repeats) and strips per roll (roll length ÷ cut length, rounded down). A 10×10 ft bedroom is 6 rolls by area and 8 by strips — the strip method sees the paper wasted at the roll ends. Calclabhub's 12×10×8 ft room is 10 rolls by area and 9 by strips; this page orders 10.",
        "rule": "Two roll counts — order the larger"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Pattern waste is 0% for plain paper, 10% for a small repeat (21 in) and 15% for a large repeat (25 in) on the area method — Magicdecor's 10–15% for patterns, CodingAce's 10% plain and 15–20% bold. On the strip method the same repeat lengthens every cut: the cut is the wall height plus 4 in trim, rounded up to whole repeats. NRTechStudio's patterned feature wall wastes 37.4% of the paper bought. Half-drop patterns (+ half a repeat per strip, roughly 15–25% more rolls) are not modelled; the large-repeat waste covers the extra paper approximately.",
        "rule": "The pattern decides the waste"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Doors and windows deduct their area from the total — this page subtracts doors × door area + windows × window area from the gross wall area. But a window almost never saves a whole strip: you still need full-height strips on both sides of it, plus short pieces above and below. Only openings that run nearly full height (at least 90% of the wall, like sliding doors) save whole strips — NRTechStudio credits ⌈opening width ÷ roll width⌉ strips for those, and this page does the same from the full-height width you enter.",
        "rule": "Openings save area, rarely strips"
      },
      {
        "detail": "HouseYog says installation is ₹8–₹15 a sq ft; Asian Paints says it starts at around ₹30 a sq ft for basic paper. This page uses ₹8–₹30, typical ₹19 — the midpoint of the full range — and shows fitting as its own line, so you can see what the paper costs without it. Fitting it yourself is ₹0 here. No city multiplier is used: no source supports one, so none is invented.",
        "rule": "Installation is ₹8–₹30 — the sources disagree"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The spare is one whole roll, from the same dye lot as the rest — colours drift slightly between print runs, so a later reorder can show a visible shade difference. The spare roll costs about coverage × typical rate (₹6,250 on the sample) and sits outside the total: the total buys the rolls the methods say you need, and the spare is extra. The pattern waste is already in the roll count, so do not add another percentage on top.",
        "rule": "Buy one spare roll, same dye lot"
      }
    ],
    "rulesTitle": "The rules behind the answer",
