    "mistakesTitle": "Common credit card payoff mistakes to avoid",
    "modelNote": "The arithmetic on this page is a month-by-month simulation. Each month: interest = balance × APR ÷ 12 ÷ 100; minimum due = max((balance + interest) × minPct ÷ 100, floor), capped at balance + interest + new spending; payment = the smaller of the mode amount and balance + interest + new spending; new balance = balance + interest + new spending − payment. The simulation runs until the balance reaches zero, capped at 600 months; if the balance never falls the result is “does not clear”, never a number. Target mode finds the smallest payment clearing within exactly the target months by bisection to the paisa. The minimum uses 5% of the balance including that month's interest — the HDFC, CredoCalc and Rupeka convention — not 5% of the opening balance as the original brief said; CreditBharosa's minimum-only totals therefore do not reproduce here (₹50,000: they show 203 months and ₹1,10,333, this page 185 months and ₹99,095), while their fixed-payment figures do. The core model is interest only: no GST, no fees. Real cards usually charge interest daily, which costs very slightly more than the monthly interest used here. Validation: balance above zero; APR 0–60%; a fixed payment must exceed the first month's interest and be at least the first month's minimum due; target months at least 1; minimum % 0.5–20%; floor ₹0–₹10,000; new spending ₹0–₹1,00,000.",
    "notice": "Interest only — no GST or fees. The figures here are the floor of the real cost: 18% GST on the interest, plus any fees, sit on top. This is a planning estimate, not your statement.",
    "overview": "It works out how long a credit card balance takes to clear, and what it costs, for three ways of paying: a fixed amount every month, only the minimum amount due, or a date you pick and the calculator finds the payment. On the sample — ₹1,00,000 at 42% a year, ₹10,000 a month from December 2026 — the card clears in 13 payments, debt-free December 2027, with ₹25,265 of interest and ₹1,25,265 paid in total. The same card on the minimum alone takes 226 months and ₹2,03,573 of interest. It is an estimate for planning, not your statement — and where the published calculators disagree, this page prints the difference with the reason instead of silently following one of them.",
    "overviewTitle": "What does a credit card payoff calculator do?",
