    "rateTitle": "Rent receipt figures at a glance",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where a rule could not be verified from the official source, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "If the annual rent exceeds ₹1,00,000, the employee must report the landlord's PAN to the employer — CBDT Circular No. 08/2013, F.No. 275/192/2013-IT(B), dated 10 October 2013. At ₹8,334 a month the year is ₹1,00,008 and the PAN is required; at ₹8,333 it is ₹99,996 and it is not. If the landlord has no PAN, a declaration with the landlord's name and address is filed instead. The circular text could not be fetched from an official site — the wording is quoted verbatim from Hindustan Times and Business Standard (2013).",
        "rule": "Landlord PAN over ₹1,00,000 a year"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Indian Stamp Act 1899, Schedule I, Article 53: a receipt for any money the amount of which exceeds five thousand rupees carries a duty of one rupee. This generator shows the stamp box on cash receipts over ₹5,000 only — exactly ₹5,000 needs none — and the landlord signs across the stamp. Honest note: the duty attaches to the receipt as an instrument, not only to cash; the cash-only rule is employer and competitor practice, because a bank transfer is its own proof.",
        "rule": "₹1 revenue stamp over ₹5,000"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Under Sec 10(13A) + Rule 2A, the exempt amount is the least of: the HRA actually received; 50% of basic plus DA in Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata and Chennai (40% elsewhere); and the rent paid minus 10% of basic plus DA. On the worked example (basic plus DA ₹40,000 a month, HRA ₹20,000, rent ₹18,000, metro Delhi) the least is ₹1,68,000 a year. Old regime only — the new regime gives no HRA exemption (incometax.gov.in FAQ, 24 September 2025).",
        "rule": "HRA exemption is the least of three"
      },
      {
        "detail": "A receipt for April 2026 is dated 30 April 2026; March 2027 is dated 31 March 2027. February is dated the 28th — or the 29th in a leap year, as the February 2028 preset shows. Never date every receipt the same day.",
        "rule": "Each receipt is dated at its month-end"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Every receipt shows the rent twice: ₹12,500 and \"Twelve Thousand Five Hundred Rupees Only\". The words use the Indian system — lakh and crore, never million — with compound tens hyphenated (\"Thirty-Four\") and no \"and\". ₹1,00,000 is \"One Lakh Rupees Only\"; ₹0 is \"Zero Rupees Only\".",
        "rule": "Amount in figures AND in words"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Under Sec 194-IB, rent over ₹50,000 a month carries TDS at 2% (reduced from 5% with effect from 1 October 2024), filed on Form 26QC: ₹60,000 a month means ₹1,200 a month, ₹14,400 a year. This is a related rule, not a receipt rule — the generator does not deduct TDS.",
        "rule": "TDS on rent over ₹50,000 a month"
      },
      {
        "detail": "An employee who receives no HRA can claim up to ₹5,000 a month (₹60,000 a year) under Sec 80GG, subject to its conditions. The receipts still prove the rent was paid.",
        "rule": "80GG where there is no HRA"
      }
    ],
