  "mistakesTitle": "Common balance transfer mistakes to avoid",
  "modelNote": "The arithmetic on this page is exact reducing-balance EMI with round-half-up (1e-9 nudge), as in the 368/369/370/371 pure files. EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), r = annual rate ÷ 1200, n = tenure in months. Computed once at the current rate over the remaining tenure, once at the new rate over the new tenure, on the same outstanding principal. Monthly saving = current EMI − new EMI; monthly saving % = monthly saving ÷ current EMI × 100. Total interest (each loan) = EMI × n − P; interest saved = current total interest − new total interest; net saving = interest saved − transfer costs. Break-even months = ceil(transfer costs ÷ monthly saving) when monthly saving > 0 (whole months, rounded up); 0 when transfer costs are 0; null (“never”) when monthly saving ≤ 0 — this is the simple break-even, and the page explains how CredoCalc's balance-based break-even differs. Verdict: “does-not-save” when monthly saving ≤ 0 or net saving ≤ 0; “marginal” when net saving > 0 but break-even months > remaining months ÷ 2; “saves” otherwise. Required new rate: the new rate (2dp, found by bisection between 0.10% and the current rate) at which net saving is exactly zero — the highest new rate at which the transfer still pays; null when no rate in that range makes net saving positive. Total paid (each loan) = EMI × n. Rates are illustrative inputs, not facts: they vary by lender and credit profile, so confirm the actual offer from the lender. The model assumes both rates stay fixed for the whole remaining tenure and ignores tax benefits, part-payments and top-ups. A top-up is new borrowing, not a saving, and is not modelled. Validation: principal ₹1,00,000–2,00,00,000; current rate 1–20% p.a.; remaining months 1–360 (integer); new rate 1–20% p.a.; new months 1–360 (integer); transfer costs ₹0–50,00,000. Invalid inputs return null or an error string, never a number.",
  "notice": "A planning estimate, not a buying decision and not financial advice. Rates are illustrative inputs — confirm the actual offer from the lender. The model assumes both rates stay fixed for the whole remaining tenure and ignores tax benefits, part-payments and top-ups.",
  "overview": "It compares your existing home loan with a new lender's offer, on the same outstanding principal: both EMIs, the monthly saving, the total interest on each loan, the interest saved, the net saving after transfer costs, the break-even month, the verdict on whether the transfer pays, and the highest new rate at which it still pays. On the sample — ₹40,00,000 at 9.5% with 15 years left, moving to 8.5% with ₹33,600 of costs — the EMI falls from ₹41,769 to ₹39,390, saving ₹2,379 a month and ₹3,94,693 after costs, with break-even in 15 months. This is a different job from the Home Loan EMI Calculator, which works out one loan's EMI: this tool compares two loans and decides the transfer. A lower new EMI alone does not mean the transfer saves money — the tenure-reset preset proves it, saving ₹6,098 a month while costing ₹3,18,116 more interest overall.",
