  "rateTitle": "How big a rate cut do you need?",
  "rulesNote": "Eight rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where the sources define a figure differently, the page says so.",
  "rulesRows": [
    {
      "detail": "EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), r = annual rate ÷ 1200, n = tenure in months. Worked out twice on the same outstanding principal: on the sample, ₹40,00,000 at 9.5% for 180 months gives ₹41,769 a month, and at 8.5% for 180 months gives ₹39,390. The exact model figures are ₹41,768.99 and ₹39,389.58 — under ₹1 from the published figures, which are rounded to the rupee.",
      "rule": "Each EMI uses the reducing-balance formula"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Monthly saving is the current EMI minus the new EMI: ₹41,769 − ₹39,390 = ₹2,379 a month on the sample, about 5.7% of the current EMI. Total interest on each loan is EMI × n − principal: ₹35,18,418 if you stay, ₹30,90,125 on the new loan. Interest saved is the difference: ₹4,28,293.",
      "rule": "The saving is the difference between two EMIs"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Net saving is the interest saved minus the transfer costs: ₹4,28,293 − ₹33,600 = ₹3,94,693 on the sample. The costs are one figure covering the processing fee (0.25–1% of the loan) plus 18% GST, legal and valuation charges (₹3,000–₹8,000) and MODT stamp duty (0.10–0.50% by state). If the costs are bigger than the interest saved, the net saving is negative and the transfer does not pay — as on the ₹5,00,000-costs preset, where it is minus ₹71,707.",
      "rule": "Net saving is interest saved minus all the costs"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Break-even months is the transfer costs divided by the monthly saving, rounded up to whole months: ₹33,600 ÷ ₹2,379 = 15 months (1 year 3 months) on the sample. It is 0 when the costs are 0, and never when the monthly saving is 0 or negative. This is the simple break-even; CredoCalc's headline 10 months on the ₹50 lakh case is balance-based and counts how much less you owe on the new loan as well — the simple figure there is 14 months, which CredoCalc itself quotes.",
      "rule": "Break-even is the costs divided by the monthly saving"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The verdict is “does-not-save” when the monthly saving is 0 or negative, or the net saving is 0 or negative; “marginal” when the net saving is positive but the break-even is more than half the remaining tenure; “saves” otherwise. The tenure-reset preset has a big monthly saving (₹6,098) but a negative net saving, so it is “does-not-save” — a lower EMI alone does not mean the transfer saves money.",
      "rule": "The verdict follows the net saving, not the EMI"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The required new rate is the new rate at which the net saving is exactly zero, found by bisection between 0.10% and the current rate and given to 2dp. On the sample it is 9.42%: below 9.42% the switch pays, at 9.42% it breaks even (net saving +₹1,118), and at 9.43% it does not pay (net saving −₹3,218). It is null when no rate in that range makes the net saving positive — a tiny balance, a short tenure or huge costs.",
      "rule": "The required rate is where the net saving is zero"
    },
    {
      "detail": "A longer tenure at the new lender cuts the EMI but stretches the interest: on the ₹50 lakh case, 204 months at 8.5% gives a new EMI of ₹46,415 and total interest of ₹44,68,579, while 240 months at 8.5% gives ₹43,391 a month but total interest of ₹54,13,879 — ₹3,18,116 more than staying. Compare like with like: start with the new tenure equal to the remaining tenure, and only lengthen it if you keep paying the old EMI.",
      "rule": "Compare like with like on tenure"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The old lender cannot charge a foreclosure fee on a floating-rate loan taken by an individual for a non-business purpose: the RBI's Pre-payment Charges on Loans Directions, 2025 (RBI/2025-26/64, 2 July 2025, paragraph 5(i)) bar it for loans sanctioned or renewed from 1 January 2026, and floating-rate home loans were already exempt. A fixed-rate loan may carry a charge under paragraph 6, so add it to the costs if yours does. The rule was verified against the RBI's own circular page on 15 November 2026.",
      "rule": "No foreclosure fee on floating-rate loans to individuals"
    }
  ],
