  "rateTitle": "How long does the battery last? Six setups, one formula",
  "rulesNote": "Eight rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess \u2014 and where the sources define a figure differently, the page says so.",
  "rulesRows": [
    {
      "detail": "Ceiling fan 75 W, LED bulb 12 W, tube light 20 W, LED TV 100 W, Wi-Fi router 15 W, laptop 65 W, refrigerator 180 W, air cooler 200 W, water pump 0.5 HP 750 W, plus any other load in watts. On the sample \u2014 2 fans, 4 LEDs, TV, router, laptop, fridge \u2014 the running load is 150 + 48 + 100 + 15 + 65 + 180 = 558 W. Check the label if you want to be exact.",
      "rule": "The running load is the sum of the appliance watts"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Inverter VA = running load \u00f7 0.8 (the power factor), rounded up to the next standard slab: 600, 850, 1,100, 1,600, 2,000, 2,500 or 3,000 VA. On the sample, 558 \u00f7 0.8 = 698 VA, so an 850 VA inverter. DesiUtility's 600 W example gives exactly 750 VA by the same division. Above 3,000 VA the page gives the exact VA and says the load is above the standard home range.",
      "rule": "Inverter VA is the load divided by the power factor"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Usable energy = battery Ah \u00d7 system volts \u00d7 derate, where the derate is 0.60 for flat plate, 0.65 for tubular and 0.75 for lithium (toq.in). On the sample, 150 \u00d7 12 \u00d7 0.65 = 1,170 Wh usable from 1,800 Wh stored. The derate covers inverter conversion losses and a safe depth of discharge.",
      "rule": "Only part of the stored energy is usable"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Backup hours = usable energy \u00f7 running load. On the sample, 1,170 \u00f7 558 = 2.10 hours. On a 250 W load the same battery gives 4.68 hours; on 200 W it gives 5.85 hours. Lentoindia's 5.76 hours on 250 W uses a flat 0.80 efficiency with no depth-of-discharge split \u2014 the page states both.",
      "rule": "Backup hours are usable energy divided by load"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Battery Ah needed = running load \u00d7 wanted hours \u00f7 (volts \u00d7 derate), rounded up to a standard size: 80, 100, 120, 150, 180, 200 or 220 Ah. On the sample, 558 \u00d7 4 \u00f7 (12 \u00d7 0.65) = 286 Ah \u2014 above the largest standard size, so a bigger bank is needed. On a 250 W load, 4 hours needs 139 Ah, so a 150 Ah battery.",
      "rule": "The Ah you need follows from the wanted backup"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Load energy in the month = running load \u00d7 outage hours \u00f7 1,000 (units). Grid energy = load energy \u00f7 charging efficiency \u2014 0.75 for lead-acid, 0.88 for lithium. Charging cost = grid energy \u00d7 tariff. On the sample, 558 \u00d7 60 \u00f7 1,000 = 33.48 units, \u00f7 0.75 = 44.64 units from the grid, \u00d7 \u20b96.5 = \u20b9290.16 a month.",
      "rule": "Charging cost is the recharged energy times the tariff"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Battery cost a month = (\u20b9/Ah \u00d7 Ah) \u00f7 (life years \u00d7 12). Flat plate \u20b965 an Ah for 3.5 years, tubular \u20b985 for 5 years, lithium \u20b9180 for 9 years (toq.in, verified September 2026). On the sample, \u20b985 \u00d7 150 \u00f7 60 = \u20b9212.50 a month. Maintenance is \u20b950 a month for lead-acid (distilled water, about \u20b9600 a year) and \u20b90 for lithium.",
      "rule": "The battery is a monthly cost, not just a price"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Available watts = inverter VA \u00d7 0.8. The inverter fits when it covers the surge load, fits the running load only when it covers the running load but not the surge, and is undersized otherwise. On the sample, an 850 VA inverter gives 680 W: enough for the 558 W running load, not for the 1,068 W surge.",
      "rule": "An existing inverter is checked against the surge"
    }
  ],
