  "factorTitle": "Every figure on the sample, and how it is worked out",
  "faq": [
    [
      "What is a 7th Pay Commission salary made of?",
      "Four parts make the gross: basic pay from the pay matrix, dearness allowance (DA, 63% of basic from 1 July 2026), house rent allowance (HRA, by city class) and transport allowance (by level and city, plus DA on it). On the sample \u2014 Level 7, \u20b944,900 basic, X city \u2014 that is \u20b944,900 + \u20b928,287 + \u20b913,470 + \u20b95,868 = \u20b992,525 a month. Four deductions come off: NPS (10% of basic plus DA), the CGHS contribution (\u20b9650 for Level 7), professional tax (your state's figure, \u20b90 on the sample) and income tax \u2014 leaving net \u20b984,556 a month."
    ],
    [
      "What DA rate does the calculator use?",
      "63% of basic pay, from 1 July 2026 \u2014 the rate announced by the government on 28 October 2026 (PIB Release ID 2329484), up from 60%. DA is revised twice a year, in January and July, so the rate on the page is current as of 16 November 2026 \u2014 and it is an editable input, so you can change it when the next revision comes. HRA and transport allowance both depend on the DA rate: HRA moves to the 30%/20%/10% tier once DA crosses 50%, and DA is paid on the transport allowance too."
    ],
    [
      "How is house rent allowance (HRA) worked out?",
      "HRA is a percentage of basic pay by city class \u2014 X, Y or Z \u2014 with a floor. The rates are 24%/16%/8% when DA is below 25%, 27%/18%/9% when DA is between 25% and 50%, and 30%/20%/10% when DA is above 50% (Department of Expenditure OM No. 2/5/2017-E.II(B), 7 July 2017). The floors are \u20b95,400 (X), \u20b93,600 (Y) and \u20b91,800 (Z) a month. At the current 63% DA the 30%/20%/10% tier applies, so a Level 7 employee on \u20b944,900 gets \u20b913,470 in an X city, \u20b98,980 in a Y city and \u20b94,490 in a Z city. No HRA is paid in government quarters \u2014 choose \u201cNo HRA\u201d on the form."
    ],
    [
      "How is transport allowance worked out?",
      "Transport allowance is a fixed monthly slab by pay level and city: Levels 9 and above get \u20b97,200 in higher-TPTA cities and \u20b93,600 elsewhere; Levels 3\u20138 get \u20b93,600 and \u20b91,800; Levels 1\u20132 get \u20b91,350 and \u20b9900 (Department of Expenditure OM No. 21/5/2017-E.II(B), 7 July 2017). DA is paid on the transport allowance as well, so at 63% DA the Level 7 slab of \u20b93,600 becomes \u20b95,868. One narrow exception is not modelled: Level 1\u20132 employees drawing \u20b924,200 or more get the Level 3\u20138 rate. Level 14+ officers entitled to an official car may opt for a different transport allowance instead \u2014 that option is not modelled either."
    ],
    [
      "NPS or GPF \u2014 which should I pick?",
      "NPS is compulsory for central government employees who joined on or after 1 January 2004, so most employees do not get to choose. Under NPS, 10% of basic pay plus DA is deducted every month and the government adds 14% \u2014 on the sample that is \u20b97,319 from the employee and \u20b910,246 from the government. GPF applies only to employees who joined before 2004; it deducts nothing on this page, so the in-hand pay is higher (\u20b91,12,012.58 against \u20b91,02,868.58 for the same Level 10 pay slip), but no pension corpus builds up. Choose the scheme you are actually on \u2014 the page does not advise which is better."
    ],
    [
      "How is income tax worked out?",
      "On the new regime for FY 2026-27, opened directly on incometax.gov.in: nil to \u20b94 lakh, 5% to \u20b98 lakh, 10% to \u20b912 lakh, 15% to \u20b916 lakh, 20% to \u20b920 lakh, 25% to \u20b924 lakh, 30% above \u20b924 lakh, plus 4% health and education cess. The \u20b975,000 standard deduction comes off the gross first, and the Section 87A rebate (up to \u20b960,000) wipes out the tax when taxable income is \u20b912 lakh or less \u2014 so the sample's taxable income of \u20b910,35,300 pays no tax, and a salary up to \u20b912.75 lakh a year is tax-free under the new regime. This is an estimate: for the exact figure, use the Income Tax Calculator, which works out tax on any salary."
    ],
    [
      "When is my next increment, and what does promotion pay?",
      "The annual increment is 3% of basic pay, taken as the next cell down in the same level of the pay matrix \u2014 \u20b944,900 becomes \u20b946,200 (44,900 \u00d7 1.03 = 46,247, rounded to the nearest \u20b9100). There is one increment a year, on 1 January or 1 July (Rule 10, CCS (Revised Pay) Rules, 2016). Promotion moves you to the next level's first cell: Level 7 to Level 8 starts at \u20b947,600. Take your basic pay from your pay slip, not from the level's first cell \u2014 the first cell is the starting basic, and the increment is worked out from the basic you actually draw."
    ],
    [
      "What about the 8th Pay Commission?",
      "The 8th Pay Commission is not in force. It was constituted on 3 November 2025, chaired by Justice Ranjana Prakash Desai, with 18 months to submit its report (around May 2027), and the Minister of State for Finance told the Lok Sabha on 10 August 2026 that no recommendations had been submitted. The fitment factor has not been decided: figures in circulation \u2014 1.92\u00d7, 2.28\u00d7, 2.86\u00d7, 3.83\u00d7, a \u20b969,000 minimum \u2014 are union demands and illustrations, not official figures. This calculator works out the 7th Pay Commission salary you are actually paid today."
    ],
    [
      "Is this the same as the Income Tax Calculator?",
      "No. The Income Tax Calculator works out tax on any salary you type in. This tool works out the whole 7th Pay Commission pay slip \u2014 basic pay, DA, HRA, transport allowance, NPS, CGHS and the tax \u2014 and then the in-hand pay. If you only want the tax on a salary you already know, use the Income Tax Calculator; if you want the pay slip for a level and basic pay, use this one."
    ],
    [
      "Why is CGEGIS not deducted?",
      "CGEGIS \u2014 the Central Government Employees Group Insurance Scheme \u2014 deducts \u20b9120, \u20b960, \u20b930 or \u20b915 a month depending on the employee's group (A, B, C or D). The subscription amounts are verified, but the pay-level to group mapping is not verified from an official source, so this page does not guess which group a level belongs to. That is the \u20b960 difference against Palz, which deducts \u20b960 for a Level 7 employee. If you know your CGEGIS deduction, add it to the professional tax field \u2014 or subtract it from the net yourself."
    ]
  ],
  "inputNote": "The sample is a Level 7 central government employee on \u20b944,900 basic pay \u2014 the first cell of Level 7 \u2014 posted in an X city with the higher transport allowance, at the current 63% DA, on NPS, with no professional tax. Results: DA \u20b928,287; HRA \u20b913,470 (30%); transport allowance \u20b95,868 (\u20b93,600 base plus \u20b92,268 DA); gross \u20b992,525 a month (\u20b911,10,300 a year); NPS \u20b97,319; government NPS share \u20b910,246; CGHS \u20b9650; taxable income \u20b910,35,300; income tax \u20b90; deductions \u20b97,969 a month; net \u20b984,556 a month (\u20b910,14,672 a year); next increment \u20b946,200 (+\u20b91,300); promotion to Level 8 starts at \u20b947,600.",
  "key": "cpc7Calculator",
