  "factorTitle": "Every figure on the sample, and how it is worked out",
  "faq": [
    [
      "What is a personal loan eligibility calculator?",
      "It estimates how much personal loan your monthly take-home pay can support, using the FOIR method Indian banks use: the most your EMIs may add up to is a share of your income, minus the EMIs you already pay, converted into a loan amount at the rate and tenure you choose. On the sample \u2014 \u20b975,000 take-home, \u20b910,000 of existing EMIs, 50% FOIR, 14% over 48 months \u2014 that is \u20b910,06,350. It is an estimate of repayment capacity, not approval."
    ],
    [
      "What is FOIR?",
      "FOIR is the Fixed Obligation to Income Ratio: the share of your take-home pay already committed to fixed repayments, plus the new EMI. If you earn \u20b975,000 a month and pay \u20b910,000 of EMIs, your FOIR is 13.3% today; adding a \u20b927,500 EMI takes it to 50.0%. Most banks cap FOIR at 40\u201350% \u2014 L&T Finance says 50\u201355% \u2014 and decline above their cap. The cap is a planning assumption on this page, defaulting to 50%: each lender sets its own, and the page lets you change it."
    ],
    [
      "How is the eligible loan worked out?",
      "In three steps. First, the FOIR cap: take-home pay \u00d7 FOIR% \u2014 \u20b975,000 \u00d7 50% = \u20b937,500 on the sample. Second, the headroom: cap minus existing EMIs \u2014 \u20b937,500 \u2212 \u20b910,000 = \u20b927,500. Third, the present value of that headroom at the rate and tenure: \u20b927,500 a month over 48 months at 14% services \u20b910,06,350. The EMI on that loan is \u20b927,500.00 \u2014 the check that the maths closes."
    ],
    [
      "Why is my eligible loan less than my income \u00d7 18?",
      "Because the multiplier is a shortcut, not the rule. L&T Finance says lenders typically multiply net monthly salary by 10\u201318 times, but the FOIR calculation governs: the rate and tenure decide how much loan an EMI can service. At 24% over 5 years, a \u20b960,000 income supports \u20b910,42,827 \u2014 17.4 times the income \u2014 while at 12% the same income supports \u20b913,48,651, 22.5 times. A high rate or a short tenure pulls the eligible loan below the multiplier; a low rate and a long tenure push it above."
    ],
    [
      "What if my existing EMIs use up the whole FOIR cap?",
      "Then there is no headroom and the eligible loan is \u20b90 \u2014 as in the worked example of \u20b950,000 take-home with \u20b930,000 of existing EMIs at 50% FOIR, where the cap is \u20b925,000 and the FOIR is already 60.0%. The way out is to close or part-pay an existing loan, or to raise the income. Raising the FOIR alone does not help much: at 60% the cap is \u20b930,000, exactly the existing EMIs, so the headroom is still \u20b90."
    ],
    [
      "How does the interest rate change the answer?",
      "A higher rate means more of each EMI goes to interest, so the same EMI buys a smaller loan. At \u20b924,000 of headroom over 5 years, 12% supports \u20b910,78,921, 18% supports \u20b99,45,126 and 24% supports \u20b98,34,261 \u2014 Calxo prints approximately \u20b910.8 lakh, \u20b99.3 lakh and \u20b98.0 lakh for the same three cases. The rate you are offered follows your credit score: HDFC Bank calls a CIBIL above 720 good, ICICI Bank prefers 750+."
    ],
    [
      "Does a longer tenure mean I can borrow more?",
      "Yes \u2014 and it costs you. On \u20b950,000 take-home with \u20b95,000 of existing EMIs at 12%, 12 months supports \u20b92,25,102 and 84 months supports \u20b911,32,969, because the same \u20b920,000 of headroom runs for seven times as long. But the total interest rises from \u20b914,898 to \u20b95,47,031. Stretch the tenure to raise eligibility only if you accept the interest bill that comes with it."
    ],
    [
      "Am I eligible for an HDFC or ICICI personal loan?",
      "The page checks the criteria those banks publish. HDFC Bank asks for net monthly income of \u20b925,000 or more, age 21\u201360, and salaried employment (private limited companies and PSUs), with 2 years' work experience including 1 year with the current organisation; it lends up to \u20b950 lakh over up to 7 years. ICICI Bank asks for monthly income of \u20b930,000 or more and age 18\u201365 for the salaried or 23\u201365 for the self-employed, lending up to \u20b950 lakh over up to 84 months. The sample profile meets both banks' published criteria. Meeting them is not approval: the lender also checks your credit score, employer, documents and internal policy."
    ],
    [
      "What about SBI?",
      "SBI's official pages state that Real-Time Xpress Credit lends up to \u20b950 lakh to SBI Salary Package account holders via YONO \u2014 but the detailed eligibility criteria load in JavaScript tabs and are not in the published text of the official pages, so this page does not check SBI criteria and does not publish SBI income, age, FOIR or multiple figures. Secondary sources disagree with each other (\u20b915,000 against \u20b920,000/\u20b925,000 minimum income; 24\u00d7 against 30\u00d7 multiple; 50% against 65% FOIR), so none of them is used. Check the current criteria on SBI's own site before applying."
    ],
    [
      "Is this the same as the Personal Loan EMI Calculator?",
      "No. The Personal Loan EMI Calculator works out the monthly EMI on a loan amount you choose \u2014 you type the loan, it gives the EMI. This tool works the other way round: you type your income and existing EMIs, and it gives the biggest loan that EMI can service. Use this one first to find out how much you can borrow, then the EMI calculator to check the EMI on the amount you actually take."
    ]
  ],
  "inputNote": "The sample is a salaried borrower of 30 with a take-home pay of \u20b975,000 a month and existing EMIs of \u20b910,000, at 50% FOIR, 14% a year over 48 months. Results: FOIR cap \u20b937,500; headroom \u20b927,500 a month; eligible loan \u20b910,06,350 (13.4 times the monthly income); EMI \u20b927,500.00 a month; total payable \u20b913,20,000; total interest \u20b93,13,650; FOIR after the loan 50.0%; meets HDFC Bank's and ICICI Bank's published criteria.",
