    "rateTitle": "Sand per cubic metre, by grade and ratio",
    "rulesNote": "Seven rules, each one checkable against the sources. Nothing here is a guess — and where a source states a figure differently, the page says so.",
    "rulesRows": [
      {
        "detail": "Wet concrete, mortar and plaster contain voids, so the dry materials take more volume than the wet job: 1 m³ wet needs 1.54 m³ dry for concrete and 1.33 m³ dry for mortar and plaster (PropertiesOnline; SiteSetu's M20 worked example uses the same 1.54). The factor multiplies the wet volume first — before any ratio is applied.",
        "rule": "Dry volume: × 1.54 concrete, × 1.33 mortar and plaster"
      },
      {
        "detail": "The sand is the sand share of the dry volume: M20 is 1:1.5:3, so the sand is 1.5 of 5.5 parts — 1.54 × 1.5 ÷ 5.5 = 0.42 m³ per cubic metre wet (PropertiesOnline states the formula as wet volume × dry factor × sand share; SiteSetu's per-grade table agrees at every grade). Mortar and plaster ratios work the same way: 1:4 means 4 of 5 parts sand.",
        "rule": "Sand is the sand share of the mix ratio"
      },
      {
        "detail": "One brass is exactly 100 cubic feet, or 2.8317 m³ (Toolique; SiteSetu agrees). So brass is cft ÷ 100 and cft is m³ × 35.3147. At the densities on this page, one brass weighs about 4.53 tonnes of river sand, 4.95 tonnes of M-sand (4.955 t unrounded) and 4.67 tonnes of P-sand.",
        "rule": "1 brass = 100 cft = 2.8317 m³"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Weight is volume × density, using bulk densities for dry, clean sand: river sand 1,600 kg/m³, M-sand 1,750 kg/m³ and P-sand 1,650 kg/m³ (Toolique, IS 383:2016 grading zones; PropertiesOnline agrees on river sand). One cubic foot of river sand is about 45 kg. Damp sand is heavier — SiteSetu's IS 875 table gives wet sand at 1,760–2,000 kg/m³.",
        "rule": "Densities: river 1,600, M-sand 1,750, P-sand 1,650 kg/m³"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Wastage multiplies the sand: 5% is the usual allowance for concrete and mortar, and plaster on uneven walls can need up to 20% (Toolique's plaster factor combines 1.33 dry volume with a 20% allowance for unevenness, raked joints and scaffolding drops — 1.33 × 1.20 = 1.596 ≈ 1.60). The range on this page is 0–20%, default 5%; the with-wastage figure is the order quantity.",
        "rule": "Wastage 0–20%, default 5%"
      },
      {
        "detail": "Toolique's plaster example (100 sq ft, 12 mm, 1:4) states 0.178 m³ (6.29 cft, 0.063 brass, ~293 kg of P-sand). That figure reproduces exactly as wet volume × 1.60 with the 4/5 sand share never applied — the 1:4 ratio is never used for the sand. This page applies the share and gives 0.142 m³, 5.03 cft, 0.050 brass, 235 kg — 25% less. SiteSetu agrees with the share method (0.136 m³ with a 15% allowance). The page states the difference, it does not hide it.",
        "rule": "The Toolique plaster difference, stated plainly"
      },
      {
        "detail": "No sand price is fixed anywhere on this page. Enter your supplier's rate per cft and the cost is the with-wastage cft times that rate; enter only a rate per brass and the cost is the with-wastage brass times that rate. Per-cft and per-brass rates are not the same price — ₹60 a cft and ₹5,800 a brass give ₹1,869 and ₹1,807 for the same 2 m³ of M20 — so check which unit your supplier means.",
        "rule": "Cost uses your rate, never a fixed price"
      }
    ],
    "rulesTitle": "The rules behind the answer",
