    "factorTitle": "Every figure on the sample, and how it is worked out",
    "faq": [
      [
        "How many plywood sheets do I need?",
        "Add up the panel area for each thickness, divide by the sheet area, round up, then add wastage and round up again. On the sample — 144 sq ft of 18 mm on 8×4 sheets (32 sq ft) with 10% wastage — that is 144 ÷ 32 = 4.5, rounded up to 5, then 5 × 1.10 = 5.5, rounded up to 6 sheets of 18 mm. Do the same for 12 mm and 6 mm and add the three counts: 6 + 3 + 2 = 11 sheets."
      ],
      [
        "What is the formula for plywood sheets?",
        "Sheets per thickness = ceil(panel area ÷ sheet area), then ceil(that × (1 + wastage ÷ 100)). The first rounding up is because you cannot buy part of a sheet; the second is because cutting wastes board. With 10% wastage the two roundings are separate: 100 sq ft on 8×4 sheets is 3.125 → 4 base sheets, then 4 × 1.10 = 4.4 → 5 sheets — Greenply's own worked rule."
      ],
      [
        "How much wastage should I add for plywood?",
        "Greenply says 5–10% extra for cuts and wastage; Saburi says 10–15%. This tool defaults to 10% and lets you pick 0–20%. Use 10% for a straightforward wardrobe or kitchen, 15% where panels are small or the layout is awkward, and 0% only to see the bare sheet count before wastage — never to order by."
      ],
      [
        "What size is a plywood sheet in India?",
        "The most common sheet is 8×4 ft, sold as 2440×1220 mm, which gives 32 sq ft of face. Smaller sizes are 7×4 ft (28 sq ft), 7×3 ft (21 sq ft), 6×4 ft (24 sq ft) and 6×3 ft (18 sq ft). IS 303, the Bureau of Indian Standards specification for general-purpose plywood, lists nominal sizes of 2400×1200, 2100×1200, 2100×900, 1800×1200 and 1800×900 mm. Always measure the sheet you are buying — the trade size and the nominal size differ by a few millimetres."
      ],
      [
        "Why does this calculator give fewer sheets than my carpenter?",
        "Because the area method divides total area by 32 and rounds up, while a carpenter lays each panel on real sheets — and panels rarely divide evenly into 8×4. Sri Sri Plywoods' worked 6×7 ft wardrobe needs 6 sheets of 18 mm by layout but only about 4.3 sheets by area (5 after rounding up). The leftover strips are too narrow to use. This tool states that gap on the page: use it to plan, and order from your carpenter's cutting list."
      ],
      [
        "Which thickness of plywood goes where?",
        "18 mm for the carcass and the shutters — the load-bearing panels. 12 mm for shelves and drawer sides. 6 mm for backs. That is the split this tool asks for, and it matches how Sri Sri and Saburi break a job down. A 6 mm back is never a substitute for an 18 mm carcass panel, and counting all three thicknesses as one lump total hides the shortfall on the thick board."
      ],
      [
        "How much does plywood cost per sheet?",
        "This tool does not state a price — plywood rates vary by brand, grade (MR, BWR, BWP), city and dealer, and a printed price would be wrong within weeks. Enter the per-sheet price your dealer quotes for each thickness and the tool works out the cost: on the sample, 6 sheets at ₹4,500, 3 at ₹3,200 and 2 at ₹1,800 come to ₹40,200. Leave the prices blank and you get the sheet count only."
      ],
      [
        "Can I enter the area in square metres?",
        "Yes. Pick m² and the tool converts with 1 m² = 10.7639 sq ft before it divides by the sheet area. 10 m² of 18 mm is 107.64 sq ft, which is 4 base sheets on 8×4 and 5 with 10% wastage. Measure in whichever unit your drawing uses — the conversion is exact, not an approximation."
      ],
      [
        "Does the sheet count include laminate and edge-banding?",
        "No. Laminate, liner and edge-banding are counted separately from the plywood — Sri Sri says so in its guide, and this tool follows it. A shutter needs one plywood sheet and one laminate sheet, and they are different purchases at different prices. The Modular Kitchen Cost Calculator on this site prices the finished kitchen, including finishes; this tool counts the plywood sheets only."
      ],
      [
        "Is the answer the exact number of sheets to order?",
        "No — it is a planning estimate. The area method undercounts against a cutting layout, and the exact count depends on the panel sizes, the grain direction and how the carpenter nests the cuts. NRTech Studio's cut-sheet calculator lays every part out on real sheets and is the right tool for the final count. Use this page to budget and to check a quote, then confirm the order against the cutting list."
      ]
    ],
    "inputNote": "The sample is 144 sq ft of 18 mm, 64 sq ft of 12 mm and 32 sq ft of 6 mm, on 8×4 sheets with 10% wastage, at ₹4,500 / ₹3,200 / ₹1,800 a sheet — 6 + 3 + 2 = 11 sheets, ₹40,200.",
    "key": "plywoodCalculator",
