    "lead": "Add up the panel area for each thickness, pick the sheet size and the wastage, and get the sheet count for 18 mm, 12 mm and 6 mm — plus the cost at the prices you enter. Everything runs in your browser; nothing you type is sent or saved.",
    "meta": "Plywood calculator — work out how many plywood sheets a wardrobe, kitchen or whole home needs, by thickness, with wastage and cost. Free, in your browser.",
    "methodology": "The figures on this page were researched 16 November 2026 from plywood quantity guides and a cut-sheet tool: Sri Sri Plywoods' wardrobe guide (https://www.srisriplywoods.in/blog/how-many-plywood-sheets-do-i-need/), Saburi Ply's buying guide (https://www.saburiply.com/plywood-buying-guide/how-much-plywood), eightbyfour's estimating guide (https://www.eightbyfour.com/guides/estimating-plywood-laminate-quantities), Greenply's quantity guide (https://www.greenply.com/blogs/how-to-calculate-plywood-quantity-and-requirement-for-your-home) and NRTech Studio's cut-sheet calculator (https://www.nrtechstudio.com/tools/cut-sheet-calculator/). Sheet sizes are the trade sizes in Sri Sri's sheet-size table (8×4 ft = 32 sq ft, 7×4 = 28, 7×3 = 21, 6×4 = 24, 6×3 = 18), and the IS 303 nominal sizes (2400×1200, 2100×1200, 2100×900, 1800×1200, 1800×900 mm) are the Bureau of Indian Standards sizes for general-purpose plywood. The formula is eightbyfour's and Greenply's: base sheets = ceil(panel area ÷ sheet area), then sheets = ceil(base × (1 + wastage ÷ 100)). eightbyfour's worked example (180 sq ft, 8×4, 12% wastage) gives 6 base sheets then 7 — exact in this tool. Greenply's rule (area ÷ 32, +5–10% wastage) gives 4 base sheets then 5 at 10% for 100 sq ft — exact in this tool. Sri Sri's worked 6×7 ft wardrobe needs 6 sheets of 18 mm and 2 of 6 mm by layout, against about 4.3 sheets (5 after rounding up) and 2 by area alone — a documented difference, because panels do not divide evenly into sheets and the leftover strips are too narrow to use; with the default 10% wastage this tool also gives 6 sheets of 18 mm, but for a different reason, and the page says so. Saburi's brand ranges are layout-based estimates: modular kitchen 30–45 sheets of 18 mm, 8×8 ft wardrobe 20–30, 6 ft wardrobe 15–20, 3BHK whole-home 200–300. This tool's area-method presets give 38, 21 and 201 for the first three — inside the ranges — but 9 sheets for the 6×7 ft wardrobe against Saburi's 15–20 for a 6 ft wardrobe: a documented difference, because the area method misses the offcuts a layout plan counts. NRTech Studio's cut-sheet calculator plans the actual layout — stock sheet size, saw kerf and a parts list of up to 12 part types — and is the right tool for the final count; this page is the planning estimate that comes before it. No price is fixed anywhere on this page: the ₹/sheet figures are the visitor's own entries (0 means not entered), and the sample prices (₹4,500 / ₹3,200 / ₹1,800) are the sample's inputs, not a rate card. Related tools on this site: EMI Calculator, SIP Calculator, PPF Calculator, FD Calculator, Income Tax Calculator, Percentage Calculator, GST Calculator, CGPA to Percentage Calculator, BMI Calculator and HRA Calculator. Last updated 16 November 2026.",
