  "faq": [
    [
      "How much water should I drink a day?",
      "It depends on your weight, your exercise and your weather — there is no one number. For most healthy adults the base is 30–35 ml of total fluid per kilogram of body weight, plus about 400 ml per half hour of exercise, plus 0–1,200 ml for the weather. On the sample — 70 kg, 30 minutes of exercise, a warm day — that is 2,850–3,200 ml of total fluid a day (2.9–3.2 litres, about 11–13 glasses of 250 ml), of which about 2,280–2,560 ml is water to drink; the rest arrives in food."
    ],
    [
      "What is the formula for daily water intake?",
      "Total fluid (ml) = weight in kg × 30–35 ml, plus 400 ml per 30 minutes of exercise, plus the weather addition (0 ml for cool or air-conditioned, 350 ml for warm, 750 ml for hot and humid, 1,200 ml for peak summer outdoors), plus 300 ml if pregnant or 700 ml if breastfeeding. The water to drink is 80% of that total, because about a fifth of daily fluid arrives in food. So a 70 kg adult with 30 minutes of exercise on a warm day needs 2,100–2,450 + 400 + 350 = 2,850–3,200 ml of fluid, and 2,280–2,560 ml of it as water to drink."
    ],
    [
      "Is eight glasses of water a day correct?",
      "No — it is a rule of thumb with no real evidential basis, and it ignores body weight, activity and climate, all three of which change the answer substantially. Eight glasses of 250 ml is 2 litres, which is close to this tool's answer for a 60 kg adult on a cool, sedentary day (1,800–2,100 ml of fluid) but far below what a 90 kg worker outdoors in May needs (4,350 ml or more). It also counts only water, when roughly a fifth of most people's fluid arrives in food. A weight-based range, checked against urine colour, is considerably more useful."
    ],
    [
      "Do tea, coffee and other drinks count towards my water intake?",
      "Yes. Tea, coffee, buttermilk, milk, dal, rasam, sambar, curd, fruit and vegetables all count towards the total fluid — roughly a fifth of most people's fluid intake arrives in food rather than in a glass. The mild diuretic effect of caffeine at ordinary intakes does not outweigh the fluid the drink supplies. One caveat is not hydration but timing: tea with meals substantially reduces iron absorption, which matters in a country with very high anaemia rates, so it is worth moving it an hour away from iron-rich meals."
    ],
    [
      "How does hot weather change how much water I need?",
      "A lot. This tool adds 0 ml for a cool or air-conditioned day, 350 ml for an ordinary warm day spent mostly indoors, 750 ml for hot and humid weather or time outdoors in the sun, and 1,200 ml for manual or field work through the heat of a peak-summer day. A 70 kg adult with 30 minutes of exercise needs 2,500–2,850 ml on a cool day and 3,700–4,050 ml on a peak-summer day outdoors — the weather alone is worth 1,200 ml. In Indian summers sweat losses can reach one to two litres an hour during outdoor activity."
    ],
    [
      "How much extra water do I need when I exercise?",
      "About 400 ml per half hour of exercise, on top of the base. The published range is 350–500 ml per half hour: the American College of Sports Medicine suggests about 350 ml, DoItSwift uses 500 ml, Calkulator uses 350–500 ml, and Calculator Planet uses 12 ml a minute (360 ml per half hour). This tool uses 400 ml, in the middle of that range. An hour of exercise therefore adds 800 ml, and two hours adds 1,600 ml. In hot weather, add the weather figure as well — exercise and heat stack."
    ],
    [
      "How much water should I drink in pregnancy or while breastfeeding?",
      "Add 300 ml a day in pregnancy and 700 ml a day while breastfeeding, on top of the base plus exercise and weather. Those are the EFSA and Institute of Medicine increments, also cited by Calkulator as the ICMR recommendations. For a 60 kg woman on a warm day with 30 minutes of exercise, pregnancy takes the total to 2,850–3,150 ml; for a 58 kg breastfeeding woman on a warm day with no exercise, the total is 2,790–3,080 ml. Any pregnancy complication, or a doctor's instruction, overrides this figure."
    ],
    [
      "Can I drink too much water?",
      "Yes, though it is uncommon in healthy people. Drinking very large volumes quickly can dilute blood sodium to dangerous levels — hyponatraemia — a risk seen most often in endurance athletes who over-drink during long events. Spreading intake through the day rather than consuming large amounts at once avoids it entirely. More importantly, anyone with heart failure, kidney disease or liver disease may have a medically restricted fluid allowance, and in those conditions drinking to a general target can cause genuine harm — follow the figure your doctor set, not this one."
    ],
    [
      "Why does this calculator give a range, not one number?",
      "Because the underlying evidence supports a range, and any tool implying more precision than that is inventing it. The base alone is 30–35 ml per kilogram — a 70 kg adult's base is 2,100–2,450 ml, a spread of 350 ml before exercise or weather are added. Your sweat rate, your diet, the humidity and how much water is in the food you eat all move the true figure inside that range. Use the range as a target to aim inside, and check it against pale urine through the day."
    ],
    [
      "How do I know if I am drinking enough?",
      "Urine colour through the day is the most practical check: pale straw means adequate, dark yellow means you need more. It is free, immediate and responds to today rather than to an average, and it beats the arithmetic. Thirst is a reasonable secondary signal for most healthy adults, with two exceptions: older adults have a blunted thirst response and can become dehydrated without feeling thirsty, and during hard exercise in heat, thirst lags behind actual losses — which is why athletes are told to drink to a schedule rather than to thirst."
    ]
  ],
