  "lead": "Enter your weight, how long you exercise and the weather you are in, and get your daily fluid target as a range — in millilitres, litres and glasses. Everything runs in your browser; nothing you type is sent or saved.",
  "meta": "Water intake calculator — how much water should you drink a day in India, by body weight, exercise and climate. Free, in your browser.",
  "methodology": "The figures on this page were researched 16 November 2026 from water intake calculators and hydration guides: NNWA's water intake calculator for India (https://nnwa.in/tools/water-intake-calculator/), DoItSwift's guide to how much water to drink per day (https://www.doitswift.com/blog/how-much-water-per-day/), Calkulator's water intake calculator (https://www.calkulator.in/water-intake-calculator), Calculator Planet's water intake calculator (https://www.calculatorplanet.in/calculators/water-intake-calculator), AIM India's water intake calculator (https://aimindia.in/water-intake-calculator/) and AFG Wellness on how much water to drink (https://afgwellness.allfrontierglobal.com/habits/how-much-water-should-you-drink/). The method is NNWA's: a base of 30–35 ml of total fluid per kilogram of body weight, plus about 400 ml per half hour of exercise, plus the weather — 0 ml for cool or air-conditioned, 350 ml for warm, 750 ml for hot and humid, 1,200 ml for peak summer outdoors — plus 300 ml in pregnancy and 700 ml while breastfeeding. NNWA's FAQ-stated case (65 kg, warm day, 30 minutes of exercise -> 2.7–3.0 litres) is exact in this tool (2,700–3,025 ml). CalculatorPlanet's 70 kg + 30 minutes case gives 2,810 ml (70 × 35 + 30 × 12); it sits inside this tool's 2,500–2,850 ml range for the same inputs. Calkulator's marathon-trainee case (70 kg, an hour of running -> 3.0–3.2 litres) sits inside this tool's 2,900–3,250 ml, and its Kiran case (75 kg -> 3,125 ml) sits inside this tool's 3,050–3,425 ml. DoItSwift's Bangalore office-worker case (65 kg × 33 ml = 2.15 litres) sits inside this tool's 1,950–2,275 ml, but DoItSwift then adds 300 ml for the air-conditioned office and prints about 2.5 litres — this tool adds 0 ml for a cool or air-conditioned day, following NNWA, so the printed totals differ; the page states the difference. Calkulator adds only 200 ml for a hot climate where this tool adds 750 ml for hot and humid weather — its hot-climate figure is the smaller one; DoItSwift's hot addition of 500–1,000 ml brackets this tool's 750 ml. The official references: ICMR–National Institute of Nutrition, Nutrient Requirements for Indians, RDA and EAR 2020, gives total fluid of 32–58 ml/kg/day for men and 27–52 ml/kg/day for women; the US National Academies (Institute of Medicine, 2005) set adequate intakes of 3.7 litres a day for men and 2.7 for women, all fluids and food; EFSA (2010) set 2.5 litres for men and 2.0 for women. The 3.7-litre and 2.7-litre figures are the US National Academies' adequate intakes — some Indian pages attribute them to ICMR–NIN, which is a misattribution, and this page attributes them correctly. About a fifth of daily fluid arrives in food (EFSA / National Academies consensus; NNWA: roughly a fifth), so the water to drink is 80% of the total. Related tools on this site: EMI Calculator, SIP Calculator, PPF Calculator, FD Calculator, Income Tax Calculator, Percentage Calculator, GST Calculator, CGPA to Percentage Calculator, BMI Calculator and HRA Calculator. Last updated 16 November 2026.",
