    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The calculation, explained</p>
          <h2>How expenses today become a corpus</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The retirement corpus rule</small>
          Corpus = first year's expenses × (1 − q^n) ÷ (1 − q), q = (1 + inflation) ÷ (1 + post-retirement return) · SIP = shortfall ÷ what ₹1 a month grows to · Expenses drawn at the start of each year
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. Age 30, retiring at 60, ₹50,000 a month today
            <strong>${inr(sample.corpus)} corpus · ${inr(sample.sip)} a month SIP</strong>
            <span>₹50,000 a month today becomes ${inr(sample.monthlyAtRetirement)} a month at 60 (6% inflation for 30 years). The corpus is ${inr(sample.corpus)} — 20.2 times the first year's expenses of ${inr(sample.annualAtRetirement)}. Your ${inr(sample.savings)} grows to ${inr(sample.savingsGrown)}, leaving a shortfall of ${inr(sample.shortfall)}, which a ${inr(sample.sip)} monthly SIP closes.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. Same plan, retiring at 65 instead
            <strong>${inr(ret65.corpus)} corpus · ${inr(ret65.sip)} a month SIP</strong>
            <span>The expenses have five more years to inflate — ${inr(ret65.monthlyAtRetirement)} a month at 65 — so the corpus is larger, yet the SIP nearly halves: five more years of saving and five fewer years of withdrawing. Your savings grow to ${inr(ret65.savingsGrown)} by 65.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. Same plan, with a 10% yearly step-up
            <strong>First SIP ${inr(stepUpSample.sipStepUp)} a month · flat SIP ${inr(stepUpSample.sip)} a month</strong>
            <span>Raising the SIP 10% a year, in line with a rising salary, lets you start at ${inr(stepUpSample.sipStepUp)} a month instead of ${inr(stepUpSample.sip)} — blogbanking's published figure for the same plan. The SIP rises every year with your salary, so the burden stays in step with your income.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>${e(p.calloutTitle)}</strong>
          ${e(p.calloutNote)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

