    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The calculation, explained</p>
          <h2>How a lump sum grows</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The lumpsum rule, annual compounding</small>
          FV = P × (1 + r)^n · Gains = FV − P · Today's money = FV ÷ (1 + i)^n
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. After one year
            <strong>₹1,00,000 × 1.12 = ₹1,12,000</strong>
            <span>One year at 12% adds ₹12,000. The return compounds once a year, so the rate you enter works as a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) — the way fund returns over several years are usually quoted.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. After ten years
            <strong>₹1,00,000 × 1.12^10 = ₹3,10,585</strong>
            <span>1.12^10 is 3.1058, so ₹1,00,000 becomes ₹3,10,585. The gains are ₹3,10,585 − ₹1,00,000 = ₹2,10,585, or 3.11 times your money. After five years it is ₹1,76,234 — the second five years add far more than the first five.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. In today's money, and as a SIP
            <strong>₹3,10,585 ÷ 1.04^10 = ₹2,09,820 · SIP ₹833 a month → ₹1,93,616</strong>
            <span>Dividing by 1.04^10 strips out 4% a year of inflation. The SIP comparison puts the same ₹1 lakh in as ₹833 a month at the start of each month, at the monthly rate 12% ÷ 12 — the same method as the SIP calculator on this site.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹3,10,585 from ₹1,00,000 at 12% over 10 years.</strong>
          The whole amount compounds from day one, which is why a lump sum beats the same money as a SIP on paper. But the return is an assumption, not a forecast: at 9% the same ₹1 lakh reaches only ₹2,36,736, and real markets fall as well as rise. Run a cautious figure alongside the hopeful one.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

