        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Compare the rate, not just the EMI.</strong> On ₹1,00,000 over 3 years, 9% costs ₹14,479 of interest in all; 18% costs ₹30,149 — ₹15,670 more for the same loan.</li>
          <li><strong>Decide the down payment before you walk in.</strong> Paying 30% upfront on a ₹1,50,000 bike saves ₹8,807 of interest; even 10% down saves ₹2,935.</li>
          <li><strong>Check flat against reducing balance.</strong> A flat 8% quote costs the same as about 14.5% reducing — ask the lender which basis the rate uses.</li>
          <li><strong>Pick the shortest tenure whose EMI you can afford.</strong> Stretching from 3 to 5 years cuts the EMI from ₹3,321 to ₹2,224 but adds ₹13,895 of interest.</li>
          <li><strong>Check prepayment terms in the Key Facts Statement.</strong> Floating-rate loans to individuals, sanctioned or renewed on or after 1 January 2026, carry no prepayment charge; most bike loans are fixed-rate, so the lender's own foreclosure charge applies — typically 2% to 5% of the outstanding principal.</li>
        </ul>
