    <section class="calc-section" id="compare">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Which repayment costs least?</p>
          <h2>The same gold, three ways to repay</h2>
          <p>For the same 50g of 22K gold at ₹13,769 a gram and 10% for 12 months, the EMI costs the least interest — ₹26,035 — because the principal falls every month. Paying only the interest costs ₹47,344, and a bullet loan costs ₹45,909, because the full principal stays outstanding for the whole year. The bullet loan also lends less: ₹4,59,091 against ₹4,73,438 for the EMI, because the RBI's LTV is tested on the total repayable, and the interest has to fit inside the cap.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table" aria-describedby="compare-note">
            <caption>Repayment comparison — 50g of 22K gold at ₹13,769 a gram, 10% a year, 12 months.</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Repayment type</th>
                <th scope="col">Loan</th>
                <th scope="col">Monthly</th>
                <th scope="col">Total interest</th>
                <th scope="col">Total payable</th>
                <th scope="col">How it works</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              ${comparisonRows}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="calc-small" id="compare-note">The bullet loan is smaller than the EMI loan because the LTV is tested on the total repayable: ₹4,59,091 + ₹45,909 = ₹5,05,000, within the 80% cap of ₹5,05,000. A bullet loan of the full ₹4,73,438 would repay ₹5,20,782 in all — above the 80% cap of ₹5,05,000 and above the 75% cap of ₹4,73,438 — so it is not allowed.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / EMI</span>
            <h3>Choose EMI when you have a steady income</h3>
            <p>You want the lowest total interest. The monthly outgo is the highest of the three — ₹41,623 a month on the example — but the loan shrinks every month, so the interest keeps falling.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / MONTHLY INTEREST</span>
            <h3>Choose monthly interest when you can repay in a lump sum</h3>
            <p>You want the lowest monthly outgo and can repay the principal in a lump sum at the end — for example, from a bonus or a sale. The interest is ₹3,945 a month on the example, but the total interest is ₹47,344, almost twice the EMI's ₹26,035.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / BULLET</span>
            <h3>Choose bullet when you expect a lump sum within a year</h3>
            <p>You want nothing to pay until then. The tenure is capped at 12 months, and the loan is smaller — ₹4,59,091 on the example — because the LTV is tested on the total repayable.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

