    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The EMI formula</p>
          <h2>How the EMI, the GST and the total are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The credit card EMI rule</small>
          EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1) · Monthly outgo = EMI + 18% GST on that month's interest
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The EMI
            <strong>₹50,000 × 0.011667 × (1 + 0.011667)^12 ÷ ((1 + 0.011667)^12 − 1) = ₹4,489</strong>
            <span>The purchase of ₹50,000 at a monthly rate of 14% ÷ 12, over 12 monthly instalments. The total EMI repayment comes from the unrounded EMI, so the rounded EMI times 12 can differ from the total by a few rupees: ₹4,489 × 12 = ₹53,868, but the total is ₹53,872 and the interest is ₹3,872.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The GST
            <strong>18% × ₹3,872 of interest = ₹697 · 18% × ₹199 fee = ₹36</strong>
            <span>GST at 18% applies to the interest and to the processing fee, never to the principal. The GST on the interest is charged on top of the EMI each month — ₹105 in the first month, when the interest is ₹583 — so the first month's outgo is ₹4,594. The fee of ₹199 plus ₹36 GST is charged upfront.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The total
            <strong>₹53,872 + ₹697 + ₹235 = ₹54,804 · ₹4,804 over the cash price</strong>
            <span>The total payable is the EMI total plus the GST on the interest plus the fee with its GST. The extra cost is ₹4,804 — 9.6% over the cash price. A calculator that shows only the EMI would report ₹53,872 and hide ₹932 of GST and fees.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹4,489 a month on a ₹50,000 purchase at 14% over 12 months — but ₹54,804 in all.</strong>
          The interest is ₹3,872, the GST on it is ₹697, and the fee with its GST is ₹235. The first month's outgo is ₹4,594, and the extra cost over the cash price is ₹4,804 (9.6%).
        </div>

        <p>The RBI's Master Direction – Credit Card and Debit Card – Issuance and Conduct Directions, 2022 (RBI/2022-23/92, 21 April 2022, effective 1 July 2022, updated 7 March 2024) requires complete transparency in EMI conversion: issuers must clearly indicate the principal, interest and upfront discount before the conversion, show the same split on the statement, and must not camouflage an EMI with an interest component as zero-interest or no-cost. The same directions require issuers to quote annualised percentage rates with the method of calculation, to show the methodology for finance charges with illustrative examples, and to carry the warning that paying only the minimum amount due stretches repayment over months or years with compounded interest. The Most Important Terms and Conditions (MITC) must be highlighted and published separately to customers — the MITC is where your issuer's EMI rate, fee and foreclosure terms live.</p>

        <p><strong>Issuers may compute interest on the average daily balance with exact day counts</strong>, so the amounts on your statement can differ by a few rupees from this calculator, which uses monthly rests with a fixed EMI. Treat the figures here as a close estimate and check the first statement. Two conventions differ across the published calculators and are documented, not bent to: calcfinex.com works from the rounded EMI, so its ₹1,00,000 example at 36% for 12 months shows ₹20,552 of interest (₹10,046 × 12 − ₹1,00,000), while this tool works from the unrounded EMI and shows ₹20,555 of interest, ₹3,700 of GST and ₹1,24,255 in all; and pocketly.in's tenure table is internally inconsistent — its 6-month EMI of ₹17,407 does not reproduce (the correct figure is ₹17,403) and its 18-month interest of ₹12,290 disagrees with its own EMI by ₹6.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

