    <section class="calc-section" id="overview">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Understand the result</p>
          <h2>${e(p.overviewTitle)}</h2>
          <p>${e(p.overview)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / THE SAME AMOUNT EVERY YEAR</span>
            <h3>₹7,000 a year, every year</h3>
            <p>Simple interest is always worked out on the original ₹1,00,000, so the sample earns ₹7,000 in year one, ₹7,000 in year two and ₹7,000 in year three — ₹21,000 in all. The balance grows in a straight line, never faster.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / A MONTHLY RATE IS 12 TIMES THE YEARLY ONE</span>
            <h3>₹2 per hundred a month is 24% a year</h3>
            <p>Indian moneylenders quote rates as rupees per hundred per month. Multiply by 12 for the yearly simple rate: ₹2 per hundred is 2% a month, 24% a year — ₹2,000 a month on ₹1,00,000. Entering 2% as a yearly rate instead would give ₹2,000 a year, twelve times too little.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / COMPOUNDING PULLS AHEAD OVER TIME</span>
            <h3>₹2,143.93 more over 3 years</h3>
            <p>Compounded quarterly, the sample earns ₹23,143.93 instead of ₹21,000. The gap is small at first — ₹186 after one year — and widens with time: over 10 years compounding earns ₹30,159.73 more at the same 7%. For short periods the two barely differ.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p>Simple interest is the oldest way of charging for the use of money: the interest is a fixed share of the original amount, for a fixed time. It is still the right tool for short-term loans between people, for loans quoted at a flat rate, for moneylender loans, and for deposits that pay the interest out instead of adding it — the Post Office Monthly Income Scheme pays its interest out every month, and the Senior Citizens Savings Scheme every quarter, so what they pay is simple interest in effect. Bank fixed deposits, on the other hand, usually compound quarterly, and most bank loans charge interest on the reducing balance in monthly EMIs — so check which basis applies before comparing two numbers.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

