    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How simple interest is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The simple interest rule</small>
          SI = P × R × T ÷ 100 · Total = P + SI
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The interest
            <strong>1,00,000 × 7 × 3 ÷ 100 = ₹21,000</strong>
            <span>Principal ₹1,00,000, annual rate 7%, time 3 years. The interest is the same every year — ₹7,000 a year — because it is always worked out on the original principal.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The total, per year, per month, per day
            <strong>₹1,00,000 + ₹21,000 = ₹1,21,000 · ₹7,000 a year</strong>
            <span>The total amount is the principal plus the interest. Per month the interest is ₹7,000 ÷ 12 = ₹583 (₹583.33 to two decimals); per day it is ₹7,000 ÷ 365 = ₹19 (₹19.18 to two decimals).</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The quarterly comparison
            <strong>₹23,143.93 compounded quarterly — ₹2,143.93 more</strong>
            <span>At the same 7%, quarterly compounding gives 1,00,000 × (1 + 0.07 ÷ 4)¹² − 1,00,000 = ₹23,143.93. Simple interest takes 14.3 years to double the money; quarterly compounding takes 10.0 years.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹21,000 of interest on ₹1,00,000 at 7% for 3 years — but ₹23,143.93 if it compounded quarterly.</strong>
          The interest is the same ₹7,000 every year, and the total is ₹1,21,000. The compounding comparison is what tells you whether the rate you were quoted is the whole story.
        </div>

        <p>Time in months is converted to years by dividing by 12, and time in days by dividing by 365 — a 365-day year, so a lender that counts days differently gets a slightly different figure for a period given in days: 90 days at 10% on ₹1,00,000 is ₹2,466 on a 365-day year and ₹2,500 on a 360-day year. A rate quoted per month is annualised by multiplying by 12 — the Indian moneylender convention, where the rate is quoted as rupees per hundred per month. The quarterly comparison adds interest only at the end of each quarter, as a bank fixed deposit does, so a part quarter earns simple interest on the principal for the remaining days; over 90 days the quarterly figure is exactly the simple figure. The Reserve Bank of India requires banks to charge interest on advances at monthly rests, so a bank loan compounds monthly rather than charging simple interest — simple interest on a bank loan appears mainly during a repayment holiday or moratorium, where the interest is debited on a simple basis.</p>

        <p>Three conventions differ across the published calculators and are documented, not bent to: credocalc.com shows the per-month and per-day figures to two decimals (₹583.33 and ₹19.18 on the sample) while this tool rounds to the nearest rupee (₹583 and ₹19); nivesh.org.in compares against annual compounding (₹46,933 on ₹1,00,000 at 8% for 5 years) while this tool's comparison is quarterly (₹48,595 on the same figures) — both are shown on this page; and the growth table below rounds to the nearest rupee, so its third-year extra is ₹2,144 where the exact figure is ₹2,143.93.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

