    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How CAGR is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The CAGR rule</small>
          CAGR = (End ÷ Start) to the power of 1 ÷ years − 1 · End = Start × (1 + CAGR) to the years
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The CAGR
            <strong>(12,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000) to the power of 1 ÷ 7, minus 1 = 13.32%</strong>
            <span>End ÷ start is 2.4. Raised to the power of 1 ÷ 7, that is 1.1332, and subtracting 1 gives a CAGR of 13.32% a year.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The absolute return and the gain
            <strong>140% absolute return · ₹7,00,000 gain · 2.40 times</strong>
            <span>The absolute return is (12,00,000 − 5,00,000) ÷ 5,00,000 = 140%. The gain is ₹7,00,000 and the money multiplied 2.40 times. Dividing 140% by 7 gives 20% a year, which sounds much better than 13.32% — but it is not what happened.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The monthly rate and the doubling time
            <strong>1.05% a month · doubles in 5.5 years</strong>
            <span>The steady monthly rate behind a 13.32% CAGR is 1.05% a month, not 13.32 ÷ 12. The money doubles in 5.5 years at that rate; the rule of 72 estimates 5.4 years.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>13.32% a year on ₹5,00,000 for 7 years — but only ₹8,02,891 at a 7% fixed deposit.</strong>
          The gain is ₹7,00,000 at that CAGR, against ₹3,02,891 at 7% a year. The fixed-deposit comparison is what tells you whether the risk was rewarded.
        </div>

        <p>For part years, each month counts as one twelfth of a year: 6 years and 6 months is 6.5 years, and 18 years and 6 months is 18.5 years. The growth table below is worked out at the exact CAGR, not the rounded figure — the sample's exact CAGR is 13.3224%, so the first-year value is ₹5,66,612 and the fifth-year value is ₹9,34,436, the figures credocalc.com publishes. At the rounded 13.32% the two would be ₹5,66,600 and ₹9,34,335. Two of themunim.com's worked examples are a touch out and are not followed: ₹3,00,000 growing to ₹5,85,000 in 5 years is 14.29% a year, not the 14.31% the page states, and ₹620 growing to ₹1,540 in 5 years is 19.96%, not 19.94%.</p>

        <p>Tax is not in the CAGR. For equity mutual funds in 2026–27, units held for more than 12 months are taxed at 12.5% on long-term gains above ₹1.25 lakh a year, and shorter holdings at 20% — the rates that have applied since 23 July 2024 under the Finance (No. 2) Act 2024. Debt funds are taxed at your slab rate. A CAGR quoted before tax and costs overstates what you actually received, so use the figure after tax when you compare two investments.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

