    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How BMR is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The Mifflin-St Jeor rule</small>
          BMR = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age) + 5 (men) or − 161 (women) · TDEE = BMR × activity
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The BMR
            <strong>(10 × 65) + (6.25 × 165) − (5 × 30) − 161 = 1,370 kcal a day</strong>
            <span>Weight adds 650, height adds 1,031.25, age takes away 150, and being a woman takes away 161. The four parts add to 1,370.25, shown as 1,370 kcal a day.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The TDEE and the targets
            <strong>1,370.25 × 1.375 = 1,884 kcal a day</strong>
            <span>Lightly active multiplies the BMR by 1.375. The loss target is 1,884 − 500 = 1,384 kcal a day, the gain target is 1,884 + 300 = 2,184. At sedentary the same BMR gives only 1,644 a day — 240 less.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The BMI and Harris-Benedict
            <strong>BMI 23.9 · Harris-Benedict 1,430 kcal a day</strong>
            <span>Priya's BMI is 65 ÷ (1.65 × 1.65) = 23.9, healthy, with a healthy weight range of 50.4–67.8 kg at 165 cm. Harris-Benedict gives 1,430 kcal a day for the same figures — 60 kcal, or 4.4%, higher than Mifflin-St Jeor.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>1,370 kcal a day at rest — but 1,884 a day lightly active.</strong>
          The BMR is the floor. What you eat is decided by the TDEE: 1,384 to lose, 1,884 to maintain, 2,184 to gain.
        </div>

        <p>For feet and inches, 1 inch is 2.54 cm exactly, so 5 ft 5 in is 165.1 cm — a tenth of a centimetre above the 165 cm the sample uses, which moves the BMR by less than 1 kcal a day. The calculator accepts ages 18–80 only: the formula is validated for healthy adults, and it is not for under-18s, pregnant or breastfeeding women, or people with a thyroid disorder, whose energy needs follow separate guidance. Two of the fetched pages state figures a touch out and are not followed: aimindia.in states 1,632 kcal a day for a 30-year-old man of 70 kg and 170 cm, where the formula gives 1,617.5, and calcpioneer.com states 1,680 for a 30-year-old man of 70 kg and 175 cm, where the formula gives 1,648.75. The exact figures are used here, and both discrepancies are logged in the pack's sources.</p>

        <p>The ICMR and National Institute of Nutrition publish recommended daily intakes for Indian adults — 2,110 kcal a day for a sedentary man and 1,660 for a sedentary woman, 2,710 and 2,130 at moderate activity, 3,470 and 2,720 at heavy activity. Those are population averages for a reference adult; your own TDEE from the calculator above is the figure to plan from, because it uses your actual weight, height, age and sex.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

