    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How XIRR is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The XIRR equation</small>
          Σ Ci ÷ (1 + r)^((di − d0) ÷ 365) = 0 · Ci is each cash flow, di its date, d0 the first date
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The equation
            <strong>Σ Ci ÷ (1 + r)^((di − d0) ÷ 365) = 0</strong>
            <span>Each flow is discounted back to 5 April 2022, the first date, at the rate r. Investments are negative, receipts are positive, and the rate that makes them add up to zero is the XIRR.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. Solving it
            <strong>At 10% the flows add up to ₹710; at 10.16% they add up to ₹0</strong>
            <span>There is no formula for r, so the calculator solves it with Newton–Raphson from a 0.1 guess, falling back to bisection inside the bracket −0.999999 to 10. It converges in a handful of steps.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The FD comparison
            <strong>Every flow at 7% to 19 October 2026: ₹1,89,548</strong>
            <span>₹50,000 for 4.54 years, ₹25,000 for 4.02 years, ₹40,000 for 3.33 years, ₹30,000 for 2.21 years and ₹25,000 for 1.61 years, less the ₹20,000 withdrawal grown for 2.76 years — ₹1,89,548 in all, so the portfolio is ahead by ₹20,452.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>10.16% a year — 35.29% absolute, ₹60,000 of gain, ₹20,452 ahead of an FD at 7%.</strong>
          The annualised figure is the one that compares: the absolute return says nothing about the 4.54 years it took.
        </div>

        <p>For a single lumpsum, XIRR equals CAGR only when the holding period is a whole number of 365-day years. CAGR counts whole calendar years, so a span containing 29 February gives an XIRR a fraction lower than the CAGR: ₹1,00,000 growing to ₹1,61,051 from 1 January 2020 to 1 January 2025 is 10.00% by CAGR and 9.99% by XIRR, because the span is 1,827 days rather than 1,825. The reference table below shows the same gap on every row that carries leap days.</p>

        <p>XIRR has honest limits, and the page states them: it annualises short periods aggressively, so a 5% gain in one month reads as about 80% a year and a three-month-old SIP can show 50% or more from a small market move. It is most meaningful over a year or more. It ignores tax, charges and exit load — enter the value net of those if you want them reflected — and past performance is not a guarantee of future returns. And where no rate balances the flows, the calculator says there is no XIRR instead of inventing one.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

