    <section class="calc-section" id="overview">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Understand the result</p>
          <h2>${e(p.overviewTitle)}</h2>
          <p>${e(p.overview)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / IT IS NET WORTH, NOT EMI</span>
            <h3>₹52,069 EMI, ₹20,000 rent — renting still wins</h3>
            <p>The EMI looks dearer than the rent, but part of it is principal that comes back as equity, and the renter invests ₹38,319 a month on top of the ₹20,25,000 upfront sum. After 10 years the renter's portfolio is ₹1,07,60,964 against the buyer's ₹77,72,746.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / THE RENT DECIDES IT</span>
            <h3>₹40,000 rent flips the same home to buying</h3>
            <p>At ₹30,000 rent the breakeven comes in year 20; at ₹40,000 it comes in year 5 and buying ends ₹14,05,340 ahead. The rent is the single biggest lever — check it before anything else.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / THE UPFRONT MONEY WORKS</span>
            <h3>₹20,25,000 invested beats ₹20,25,000 spent</h3>
            <p>The down payment plus stamp duty is ₹20,25,000. The buyer spends it on day one; the renter invests it, and at 8% a year it alone grows to ₹43,71,823 in 10 years. Stamp duty is the cost buying can never recover.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p>Renting suits a short stay, an uncertain job or a city where rents are low against prices. Buying suits a long stay, a stable income and a rent that is already close to the EMI plus maintenance. For the loan itself, use the <a href="/home-loan-emi-calculator/">home loan EMI calculator</a>; for what the salary supports, the <a href="/salary-calculator/">salary calculator</a>; and for the tax on the interest, the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">income tax calculator</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

