          <p>
            Two households start with the same money. The buyer pays the
            down payment plus stamp duty and registration on day one, then
            the EMI plus maintenance and property tax every month. The
            renter invests the whole upfront outlay on day one, pays rent
            that rises once a year, and invests the monthly difference
            between owning and renting. Investments grow at the effective
            monthly factor (1 + return)^(1/12) − 1, with contributions at
            month end; the home grows at the effective monthly factor of
            the appreciation; maintenance is 1% of the home's value a year
            by default, charged monthly on the value at the start of the
            month; the loan follows the standard reducing-balance EMI.
            Buying leaves the home value minus the loan left minus selling
            costs (plus any surplus the buyer invested once owning became
            cheaper than renting); renting leaves the investment portfolio.
            The breakeven is the first year buying moves ahead and stays
            ahead through year 40. Money rounds to the nearest rupee; the
            lead is the rounded difference of the exact figures, so it can
            sit ₹1 away from subtracting the two rounded columns.
          </p>
