    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the due date is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>Naegele's rule</small>
          Due date = last period + 280 days, moved by cycle length − 28 · From conception + 266 days · After IVF, transfer + 263 days (day 3) or + 261 days (day 5)
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The last period
            <strong>1 August 2026 + 280 days</strong>
            <span>Naegele's rule counts 40 weeks from the first day of the last period. For a 28-day cycle that lands on Saturday 8 May 2027.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The cycle adjustment
            <strong>+0 days for 28 days; +7 for 35</strong>
            <span>A 35-day cycle ovulates a week later, so the due date moves a week later — 15 May 2027 for the same last period. A 21-day cycle moves it a week earlier.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The check
            <strong>Three published examples reproduce exactly</strong>
            <span>fertilitynetwork: 1 June 2025 → 8 March 2026. sdev: 8 June 2026 → 15 March 2027. calculator.com.in: 1 January 2027 → 8 October 2027. All three are LMP + 280 days.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Saturday 8 May 2027 — the estimated due date on the sample.</strong>
          Only about 4–5% of babies arrive on the date itself; most arrive within about two weeks either side of it.
        </div>

        <p>Two honest limits, and the page states them: the date assumes a regular cycle and ovulation about 14 days after the last period — irregular cycles and late ovulation move it, which is why an early scan is the surest check. And the date is an estimate for planning, not medical advice: confirm it with your doctor, and ask about anything that worries you.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

