    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the ovulation date is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The calendar method</small>
          Ovulation = last period + (cycle length − luteal phase) · Fertile window = 5 days before ovulation + ovulation day · Next period = last period + cycle length · Due date if you conceive = ovulation + 266 days
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The ovulation day
            <strong>1 November 2026 + 14 days</strong>
            <span>The calendar method counts back 14 days from the next period. For a 28-day cycle that lands on Sunday 15 November 2026, day 14 of the cycle.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The fertile window
            <strong>10 to 15 November — six days</strong>
            <span>Sperm can survive up to 5 days, so the five days before ovulation are fertile; the egg lives only 12–24 hours, so the day after ovulation is not. The window ends on ovulation day.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The check
            <strong>Five published examples reproduce exactly</strong>
            <span>CalculatorKing: 1 January 2026 → 15 January 2026. acalculator.org: 1 September 2026 → 15 September 2026, and 10 February 2026 (32 days) → 28 February 2026. AgeCalculatorLab: 1 October 2026 → 15 October 2026.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Sunday 15 November 2026 — the ovulation date on the sample.</strong>
          The fertile window is 10 to 15 November 2026, six days. The two days before ovulation — 13 and 14 November — give the highest chance per cycle.
        </div>

        <p>Two honest limits, and the page states them: the date assumes a regular cycle and a stable luteal phase — irregular cycles, PCOS, thyroid conditions and recent contraceptive use move it, which is why an ovulation test is the best check. And the date is an estimate for planning a pregnancy, not medical advice and not contraception: confirm ovulation with a test or your doctor.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

