// Stamp duty calculator (India).
// Stamp duty + registration fee + total cost for a property purchase,
// per state, per buyer category (male / female / joint), on the higher
// of the agreement value and the circle rate / ready reckoner value
// (the user enters the higher value; the page explains the rule).
// - Stamp duty = value x the state's base duty rate for the buyer.
//   Karnataka's base rate is a slab on the value (2% to Rs 20 lakh,
//   3% to Rs 45 lakh, 5% above); every other state has a flat rate
//   per buyer category.
// - Surcharge: some states add a percentage ON the duty (Gujarat 40%,
//   Karnataka 12% = 10% cess + 2% surcharge), others add a percentage
//   OF the value (Maharashtra metro cess 1%, + 1% LBT in Pune/Thane/
//   Nagpur; Telangana transfer duty 1.5%). Both are shown separately.
// - Registration fee = value x the state's registration rate, subject
//   to the state's cap where one applies (Maharashtra 1% capped at
//   Rs 30,000); Delhi adds a Rs 100 pasting fee. Gujarat waives the
//   registration fee for a sole female buyer. Tamil Nadu charges women
//   3% registration instead of 4% on property up to Rs 10 lakh.
//   Uttar Pradesh's 1% women's rebate applies only up to Rs 1 crore.
// - Total = stamp duty (base + surcharge) + registration fee.
// - Money is rounded to paise (2 decimals, half up); percentages to
//   1 decimal for the total and 2 decimals for the effective duty rate.
//   The Python model rounds the same way, so the JS and the model
//   agree exactly.
// - Rates verified 13 November 2026 — see sources.md for the
//   per-state source. Delhi's male/female rates were read on the
//   Revenue Department, GNCT of Delhi, official page; Telangana's
//   5.5% + 1.5% + 0.5% from G.O.Ms.No.59 (20 July 2021) as quoted
//   verbatim. Maharashtra's 5% base is Article 25(b) of Schedule I
//   to the Maharashtra Stamp Act, 1958 (as modified to 1 June 2022),
//   read verbatim, with the 1% women's reduction from Government
//   Order R & FD No Mudrank-2021/UOR/UOR12/CR.107/M-1 (31 March
//   2021); the 1% metro cess and 1% LBT and the Rs 30,000
//   registration cap are as published in the 2026 IGR-based guides
//   and cross-checked across independent sources. Karnataka's slabs,
//   10% cess + 2% surcharge and 2% registration fee (from
//   31 August 2025, Karnataka Registration (Amendment) Act, 2025)
//   are cross-checked across independent 2026 guides. Tamil Nadu,
//   Uttar Pradesh, Gujarat, Punjab and Madhya Pradesh rates are
//   cross-checked across independent 2026 guides; the IGRS portals'
//   calculators are JavaScript-gated and did not return rate text
//   to our reader, so the page tells the reader to verify on the
//   portal before paying. Rajasthan, West Bengal and Haryana are
//   LEFT OUT: Rajasthan's surcharge is disputed (20% vs 33% of the
//   duty), West Bengal's structure and women's rebate are disputed,
//   and Haryana's registration-fee slab middle rows are not
//   verifiable — see sources.md.
// - Anchors, checked 13 November 2026 against the independent
//   Python model: Calcusy — Rs 80 lakh Mumbai flat, man: duty
//   Rs 4,80,000 (6%) + registration Rs 30,000 = Rs 5,10,000;
//   TAXAJ — Rs 1 crore Mumbai flat, man: duty Rs 6,00,000 +
//   registration Rs 30,000 = Rs 6,30,000; TAXAJ — Rs 50 lakh Delhi
//   flat, man: duty Rs 3,00,000 + registration Rs 50,000 =
//   Rs 3,50,000, woman Rs 2,00,000 + Rs 50,000 = Rs 2,50,000
//   (Delhi registration here is the 1% fee without the Rs 100
//   pasting fee); Calcusy — Rs 80 lakh Pune flat, man: duty
//   Rs 5,60,000 (7%) + registration Rs 30,000 = Rs 5,90,000;
//   Calcusy — Rs 80 lakh Chennai flat: duty Rs 5,60,000 (7%) +
//   registration Rs 3,20,000 (4%) = Rs 8,80,000.
// - NOT reproduced, stated honestly: LegalXIndia writes "Stamp
//   Duty at 7%: Rs 3,50,000" for a Rs 50 lakh Mumbai flat, man.
//   That 7% adds the 1% metro cess to a 6% base; the statutory
//   base under Article 25(b) is 5%, so the duty is 6% (5% + 1%
//   metro cess) = Rs 3,00,000, and the total is Rs 3,30,000 with
//   the capped registration fee. The brief's own example quoted
//   the same 7% figure; it is wrong for the same reason.
// - Differentiation from the live Home Loan Eligibility Calculator
//   (entry 309, /home-loan-eligibility-calculator/): that page
//   shows stamp duty and registration only as a range (about
//   Rs 3,67,000–Rs 4,89,000 on its sample price). This tool
//   computes the exact duty + registration fee per state and
//   buyer category. The page says so and links to it.
// - This is an educational estimate, not legal or tax advice.
//   Rates are set by state notification and change; verify with
//   your Sub-Registrar / IGRS portal before paying. Nothing is
//   sent anywhere: every calculation runs in the browser.
// Conventions (stated on the page):
// - The client sends exactly the 3 STAMP_KEYS; the state is one of
//   STAMP_STATE_IDS and the buyer is "male", "female" or "joint".
// - Property value: a finite number above Rs 0 up to Rs 1,000 crore
//   (10,000,000,000). Enter the higher of the agreement value and
//   the circle rate / ready reckoner value for the property.
