    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How inflation is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The inflation formulas</small>
          Future cost = amount × (1 + rate)^years · Value today = amount ÷ (1 + rate)^years · CII value = amount × CII(to) ÷ CII(from) · Doubling time = ln 2 ÷ ln(1 + rate) · Rule of 70 = 70 ÷ rate
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The future cost
            <strong>₹1,00,000 × 1.04^10 = ₹1,48,024.43</strong>
            <span>The growth factor (1 + 4%)^10 is 1.4802. Multiplying today's cost by it gives the cost in 10 years — ₹48,024.43 more.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The value today
            <strong>₹1,00,000 ÷ 1.06^10 = ₹55,839.48</strong>
            <span>Dividing instead of multiplying runs the same arithmetic backwards: a sum in 10 years is worth that much in today's rupees, and 44.2% of its purchasing power is lost.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The check
            <strong>Thirteen published examples reproduce exactly</strong>
            <span>CredoCalc: ₹1,48,024 at 4% and ₹1,79,085 at 6% over 10 years, doubling in 17.7 years at 4%. BlogBanking: ₹55,839 and 44.2% lost, and its whole future-cost table. ap7am: ₹3,84,000 by CII. Palz: ₹1,772.16 by CPI.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹1,48,024.43 — the future cost on the sample.</strong>
          ₹48,024.43 more than today, a factor of 1.4802. At 4%, prices double in about 17.7 years.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the rate is held steady for the whole period, so the result is an estimate at that rate, not a forecast — real inflation moves every year. The category presets (general 6%, education 10%, healthcare 12%) are planning assumptions, not official figures. And the CII tracks only about three-quarters of consumer-price rises, so it understates what everyday prices did over the same years. One rounding note is stated honestly too: Palz writes ₹1,58,295 at "4.71%" — that figure matches 4.7% exactly, not 4.71%.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

