    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How stamp duty is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The stamp duty formulas</small>
          Stamp duty = value × the state's duty rate for the buyer · Surcharge = duty × the cess on duty, or value × the cess on value · Registration fee = value × the registration rate, capped where the state caps it · Total = stamp duty + registration fee
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The stamp duty
            <strong>₹50,00,000 × 5% + 1% metro cess = ₹3,00,000</strong>
            <span>The base duty is 5% — ₹2,50,000 — under Article 25(b). The metro cess adds 1% of the value — ₹50,000. Together the duty is 6% of the price.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The registration fee
            <strong>₹50,00,000 × 1% = ₹50,000, capped at ₹30,000</strong>
            <span>The fee is 1% of the value, but Maharashtra caps it at ₹30,000 — so only ₹30,000 is charged. Delhi adds a ₹100 pasting fee on top of its 1%.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The check
            <strong>Six published examples reproduce exactly</strong>
            <span>Calcusy: ₹5,10,000 on an ₹80 lakh Mumbai flat, ₹5,90,000 in Pune, ₹8,80,000 in Chennai. TAXAJ: ₹6,30,000 on a ₹1 crore Mumbai flat, and ₹3,00,000 / ₹2,00,000 duty in Delhi — the ₹100 pasting fee is the only difference.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹3,30,000 — the total cost on the sample.</strong>
          ₹3,00,000 of stamp duty and a ₹30,000 registration fee, capped. That is 6.6% of the price.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the rates are set by state notification and change — Karnataka's registration fee doubled to 2% on 31 August 2025, and Telangana's 5.5% rate dates from 2021, so older pages quoting 4% are stale. The duty is charged on the higher of the agreement value and the circle rate, so enter the higher value. And one published example is wrong, stated honestly: LegalXIndia writes "Stamp Duty at 7%: ₹3,50,000" for a ₹50 lakh Mumbai flat — that 7% adds the 1% metro cess to a 6% base, but the statutory base is 5%, so the duty is 6%, ₹3,00,000.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

