    <section class="calc-section" id="overview">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Understand the result</p>
          <h2>${e(p.overviewTitle)}</h2>
          <p>${e(p.overview)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / WEIGHTED, NOT SIMPLE</span>
            <h3>100 at ₹250 and 50 at ₹200 average ₹233.33, not ₹225</h3>
            <p>Each purchase counts in proportion to how many shares it bought. ₹25,000 was spent at ₹250 and only ₹10,000 at ₹200, so the average sits much closer to ₹250 than to ₹200.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / THE PLANNER</span>
            <h3>100 at ₹500, buy at ₹400 to reach ₹460 — 67 shares</h3>
            <p>The planner works backwards: 100 × (₹500 − ₹460) ÷ (₹460 − ₹400) = 67 shares, costing ₹26,800, for a new average of ₹459.88. Rounding up lands just past the target.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / THE PROFIT OR LOSS</span>
            <h3>₹16,600 invested, worth ₹14,700 at ₹210</h3>
            <p>With today's price the tool also shows the current value, the profit or loss in rupees and percent, and how far the price must rise to reach your average — 12.92% on the sample.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p>Those tools measure returns over time. The <a href="/xirr-calculator/">XIRR calculator</a> works out the annualised return on irregular cash flows, the <a href="/cagr-calculator/">CAGR calculator</a> the steady yearly growth rate, and the <a href="/lumpsum-calculator/">Lumpsum calculator</a> what a one-time investment grows to. This tool is different: it answers the single question of what you paid on average for this share across your buys. For the tax side, the <a href="/capital-gains-tax-calculator/">Capital Gains Tax calculator</a> works out the tax — remembering that demat sales are matched first-in first-out, not by average.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

