    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the share average is worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The share average formulas</small>
          Average price = total invested ÷ total shares · Current value = total shares × current price · Profit/loss = current value − total invested · Shares to buy = current qty × (current average − target) ÷ (target − new price), rounded up
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The average
            <strong>(40 × ₹250 + 30 × ₹220) ÷ 70 = ₹237.14</strong>
            <span>Total invested divided by total shares. A simple average of ₹250 and ₹220 would give ₹235 — wrong, because more shares were bought at ₹250.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The planner
            <strong>100 × (₹500 − ₹460) ÷ (₹460 − ₹400) = 67 shares</strong>
            <span>Shares to buy for a target average, rounded up to a whole share. Costing ₹26,800, the new average is ₹459.88.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The check
            <strong>Sixteen published examples reproduce exactly</strong>
            <span>stocknotify: ₹237.14 and 67 shares. rupeescalc: ₹167.78. blogbanking: ₹1,378 and 37 shares. thebeststockbroker: ₹186.67. a2z: ₹233.33 and 200 shares.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹237.14 — the average price of 40 shares at ₹250 and 30 at ₹220.</strong>
          ₹16,600 invested for 70 shares; at ₹210 the holding is worth ₹14,700.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: brokerage, STT, stamp duty and other charges are NOT included — this is the price average before charges, so a broker's all-in figure can differ slightly. For Indian capital-gains tax, demat sales are matched first-in first-out, not by average — the average is for tracking a position, not for a tax return. And averaging down adds size to a losing position and increases the risk: a lower average is not a lower risk.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

