// Child Education Calculator (India).
// What a course will cost when the child is old enough to start it,
// and the monthly SIP (flat or stepped up) or the lumpsum today that
// builds the gap — worked out in the browser.
// - Rules (verified 14 November 2026 — see sources.md): the future
//   cost is the cost today x (1 + education inflation) ^ years to go,
//   where years to go is the age the course starts minus the child's
//   age now. Education inflation has no official figure: private
//   school and college fees in India have often risen faster than
//   general inflation, so planners use 8-10% (blogbanking FAQ) and
//   this calculator defaults to 10%. Existing savings grow at the
//   expected return, compounded annually. The gap is the future cost
//   minus the savings grown, floored at 0 — already fully funded
//   gives a gap of 0, a SIP of 0 and a lumpsum of 0.
// - The monthly SIP is a beginning-of-month (annuity-due) payment:
//   SIP = gap / factor, factor = ((1 + i)^n - 1) / i x (1 + i), with
//   i = return / 12 / 100 and n = years x 12. The step-up SIP is the
//   first-year monthly SIP when the SIP rises by the step-up % every
//   year: year k pays s x (1 + g)^k a month, and each year's 12
//   beginning-of-month payments grow to the end of the horizon. With
//   a 0% step-up the step-up SIP equals the flat SIP. The lumpsum
//   today is gap / (1 + return) ^ years (annual compounding). The
//   cost in today's money divides the future cost by 1.06 ^ years
//   (general inflation 6%, the top of the RBI's 2-6% tolerance band).
// - Totals invested use the rounded monthly SIPs: flat total = flat
//   SIP x months; step-up total = the rounded first-year SIP grown by
//   the step-up each year, summed over the years. Funded % is the
//   savings grown as a share of the future cost, capped at 100.
// - A 0% return makes the SIP the gap / months and the lumpsum the
//   gap itself; a 0% education inflation makes the future cost the
//   cost today. All money amounts round to the nearest rupee (half
//   up); percentages to 2 decimals (half up).
// - Anchors, checked 14 November 2026 against the independent Python
//   model (blogbanking.com/child-education-planner, fetched the same
//   day): the sample — child 5, course starts at 18, Rs 15 lakh
//   today, 10% education inflation, 12% return, Rs 1 lakh saved —
//   gives a future cost of Rs 51,78,407, savings grown to
//   Rs 4,36,349 and a gap of Rs 47,42,058; with nothing saved the SIP
//   is Rs 13,775 a month and the lumpsum Rs 11.87 L; with a 10%
//   yearly step-up the sample starts at Rs 7,821 a month. Starting
//   at birth (age 0): future cost Rs 83,39,876, flat SIP Rs 10,896,
//   step-up SIP Rs 5,786, lumpsum Rs 10.85 L. Waiting 3 years to
//   start (child 8) raises the SIP to Rs 15,409 a month. The example
//   courses for a 5-year-old: MBA (Rs 25 lakh, starts 22) — SIP
//   Rs 18,919; Medical (Rs 75 lakh, starts 18) — SIP Rs 68,874;
//   study abroad (Rs 50 lakh, starts 22) — SIP Rs 37,837. The
//   Rs 51.78 L needed is Rs 24.28 L in today's money at 6% general
//   inflation. thepocketsaving.com: a Rs 20 lakh course, child 5,
//   starts at 18 — future cost Rs 69,04,542, SIP Rs 18,367.
// - Near-matches, stated honestly (see sources.md): blogbanking's
//   "Total you will invest by SIP Rs 19,67,810" for the sample
//   implies a flat SIP of Rs 12,613; this calculator gives Rs 12,614
//   (total Rs 19,67,784) — a Rs 1 difference from their rounding.
//   krishnavriddhicapital.com quotes a SIP of about Rs 14,826 a
//   month for a Rs 20 lakh course over 14 years at 8% inflation and
//   an 11% return; this calculator gives Rs 14,692 — the site's
//   figure is stated as "about" and implies end-of-month
//   contributions, while this calculator uses beginning-of-month
//   payments.
// - Sukanya Samriddhi (for a daughter): 8.2% a year for
//   October-December 2026, set every quarter by the government, tax
//   free; deposit Rs 250 to Rs 1.5 lakh a year for 15 years; up to
//   50% of the balance can be withdrawn from age 18 for higher
//   education. Investing Rs 1.5 lakh a year from birth gives about
//   Rs 56.7 L by 18 (blogbanking). The SSY calculator on this site
//   works out the maturity value.
// - Course costs on this page are rough examples to start from, not
//   fee quotes: fees differ widely between government and private
//   colleges, states, quotas and countries, and change every year.
//   Look up the current fees of the colleges being considered and
//   enter that figure.
// - This is an estimate for planning only, not investment advice.
//   The return is an assumption, not a forecast; mutual fund
//   investments are subject to market risks. Nothing is sent
//   anywhere: every calculation runs in the browser.
// Conventions (stated on the page):
// - The client sends exactly the 7 CE_KEYS.
// - childAge is a whole number from 0 to 17; startAge a whole number
//   from 15 to 25, and after the child's age. courseCostToday is the
//   total course cost at today's prices, from Rs 50,000 to
//   Rs 2,00,00,000. eduInflation is 0 to 15% a year (default 10).
//   expectedReturn is 0 to 18% a year (default 12). stepUp is the
//   yearly SIP step-up, 0 to 25% (default 0). savedSoFar is what is
//   already saved for this goal, from Rs 0 to Rs 1,00,00,000.
export const CE_MIN_CHILD_AGE = 0;
export const CE_MAX_CHILD_AGE = 17;
export const CE_MIN_START_AGE = 15;
export const CE_MAX_START_AGE = 25;
export const CE_MIN_COST = 50000;
export const CE_MAX_COST = 20000000;
export const CE_MAX_INFLATION = 15;
export const CE_MAX_RETURN = 18;
export const CE_MAX_STEP_UP = 25;
export const CE_MAX_SAVED = 10000000;
export const CE_GENERAL_INFLATION = 6;
export const CE_KEYS = ["childAge", "startAge", "courseCostToday", "eduInflation", "expectedReturn", "stepUp", "savedSoFar"];
export function ceRound(x) {
  return Math.floor(x + 0.5);
}
export function ceRound2(x) {
  return Math.floor(x * 100 + 0.5) / 100;
}
export function ceAnnuityDueFactor(i, n) {
  if (i === 0) return n;
  return (Math.pow(1 + i, n) - 1) / i * (1 + i);
}
export function ceCompute(input) {
  if (!input || typeof input !== "object") return null;
  const childAge = input.childAge;
  const startAge = input.startAge;
  const courseCostToday = input.courseCostToday;
  const eduInflation = input.eduInflation;
  const expectedReturn = input.expectedReturn;
  const stepUp = input.stepUp;
  const savedSoFar = input.savedSoFar;
  if (typeof childAge !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(childAge) || !Number.isInteger(childAge) || childAge < CE_MIN_CHILD_AGE || childAge > CE_MAX_CHILD_AGE) return null;
  if (typeof startAge !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(startAge) || !Number.isInteger(startAge) || startAge < CE_MIN_START_AGE || startAge > CE_MAX_START_AGE) return null;
  if (startAge <= childAge) return null;
  if (typeof courseCostToday !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(courseCostToday) || courseCostToday < CE_MIN_COST || courseCostToday > CE_MAX_COST) return null;
  if (typeof eduInflation !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(eduInflation) || eduInflation < 0 || eduInflation > CE_MAX_INFLATION) return null;
  if (typeof expectedReturn !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(expectedReturn) || expectedReturn < 0 || expectedReturn > CE_MAX_RETURN) return null;
  if (typeof stepUp !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(stepUp) || stepUp < 0 || stepUp > CE_MAX_STEP_UP) return null;
  if (typeof savedSoFar !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(savedSoFar) || savedSoFar < 0 || savedSoFar > CE_MAX_SAVED) return null;
  const yearsToGo = startAge - childAge;
  const months = yearsToGo * 12;
  const futureCost = ceRound(courseCostToday * Math.pow(1 + eduInflation / 100, yearsToGo));
  const savingsGrown = ceRound(savedSoFar * Math.pow(1 + expectedReturn / 100, yearsToGo));
  const gapToFill = Math.max(0, futureCost - savingsGrown);
  const i = expectedReturn / 1200;
  const g = stepUp / 100;
  let flatSip = 0;
  let stepUpSip = 0;
  if (gapToFill > 0) {
    if (i === 0) {
      flatSip = ceRound(gapToFill / months);
      let denom = 0;
      for (let k = 0; k < yearsToGo; k++) denom += Math.pow(1 + g, k) * 12;
      stepUpSip = ceRound(gapToFill / denom);
    } else {
      flatSip = ceRound(gapToFill / ceAnnuityDueFactor(i, months));
      const a12 = ceAnnuityDueFactor(i, 12);
      let denom = 0;
      for (let k = 0; k < yearsToGo; k++) denom += Math.pow(1 + g, k) * a12 * Math.pow(1 + i, 12 * (yearsToGo - 1 - k));
      stepUpSip = ceRound(gapToFill / denom);
    }
  }
  const flatTotalInvested = flatSip * months;
  let stepUpTotalInvested = 0;
  for (let k = 0; k < yearsToGo; k++) stepUpTotalInvested += stepUpSip * Math.pow(1 + g, k) * 12;
  stepUpTotalInvested = ceRound(stepUpTotalInvested);
  const lumpsumToday = gapToFill === 0 ? 0 : ceRound(gapToFill / Math.pow(1 + expectedReturn / 100, yearsToGo));
  const costTodayMoney = ceRound(futureCost / Math.pow(1 + CE_GENERAL_INFLATION / 100, yearsToGo));
  const fundedPct = futureCost === 0 ? 0 : ceRound2(Math.min(100, savingsGrown / futureCost * 100));
  return {
    childAge,
    startAge,
    courseCostToday,
    eduInflation,
    expectedReturn,
    stepUp,
    savedSoFar,
    yearsToGo,
    futureCost,
    savingsGrown,
    gapToFill,
    flatSip,
    flatTotalInvested,
    stepUpSip,
    stepUpTotalInvested,
    lumpsumToday,
    costTodayMoney,
    fundedPct,
  };
}

