    <section class="calc-section" id="overview">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Understand the result</p>
          <h2>${e(p.overviewTitle)}</h2>
          <p>${e(p.overview)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / THE SCHEDULE</span>
            <h3>₹1,50,000 net: ₹22,500 by 15 June, ₹45,000 more by 15 September</h3>
            <p>Then ₹45,000 more by 15 December and ₹37,500 by 15 March. The percentages are cumulative — each instalment is the target less what earlier dates already required.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / THE INTEREST</span>
            <h3>₹2,925 on the sample: ₹1,725 under section 425, ₹1,200 under section 424</h3>
            <p>Paying ₹15,000 / ₹50,000 / ₹90,000 / ₹1,20,000 instead of on time leaves four shortfalls, and the interest follows each one. Paying on time costs nothing extra.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / THE RELIEF</span>
            <h3>12% by June or 36% by September switches the interest off</h3>
            <p>Pay at least 12% of the net liability by 15 June and the June interest is nil, even with a shortfall; 36% by 15 September does the same for September. Paying 90% by 31 March switches section 424 off.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p>Three tools cover the three questions. The <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax calculator</a> works out the year's tax; the <a href="/tds-calculator/">TDS calculator</a> works out what is deducted from it; this tool works out when to pay what is left and what late payment costs. For the tax on a sale, the <a href="/capital-gains-tax-calculator/">Capital Gains Tax calculator</a> works out the gain and the tax — remembering that a gain you could not foresee carries no section 425 interest if its tax is paid in the remaining instalments or by 31 March.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

