    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How advance tax and its interest are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The advance tax formulas</small>
          Net liability = estimated tax − TDS / TCS · Instalments: 15% by 15 June, 45% by 15 September, 75% by 15 December, 100% by 15 March · Section 425 = 3% of each shortfall (1% in March) · Section 424 = 1% a month from 1 April on the unpaid balance, when under 90% was paid
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The instalments
            <strong>(15% × ₹1,50,000) = ₹22,500 by 15 June; 45% = ₹67,500 by 15 September</strong>
            <span>Each instalment is the cumulative target less what earlier dates required: ₹22,500, then ₹45,000, then ₹45,000, then ₹37,500 — adding up to ₹1,50,000 exactly.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The section 425 interest
            <strong>3% of each shortfall: ₹7,500 → ₹225, ₹17,500 → ₹525, ₹22,500 → ₹675; 1% of ₹30,000 → ₹300</strong>
            <span>Each shortfall is rounded down to ₹100 first. Total ₹1,725 — exactly 1234tools' published figure for these payments.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The section 424 interest
            <strong>80% paid by March is under 90%: 1% a month for 4 months on ₹30,000 = ₹1,200</strong>
            <span>Total interest ₹2,925 on a total payable of ₹1,52,925. Sixteen published examples reproduce exactly.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹2,925 — the interest on ₹1,50,000 paid ₹15,000 / ₹50,000 / ₹90,000 / ₹1,20,000, the rest in July.</strong>
          ₹1,725 under section 425 and ₹1,200 under section 424, on a total payable of ₹1,52,925.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: section 423 interest for filing the return late is not included. The section 425(4) relief for a shortfall caused by capital gains, dividends or newly started business income you could not foresee is not applied — in that case this tool overstates the interest. And resident senior citizens aged 60 or over with no business or professional income are exempt from advance tax entirely; this tool does not model that exemption.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

