    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How brokerage and trading charges are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The brokerage formulas</small>
          Turnover = buy value + sell value · Brokerage per order (Zerodha: ₹0 delivery, lower of ₹20 or 0.03% intraday / futures, ₹20 options) · STT: delivery 0.1% both sides, intraday 0.025% sell, futures 0.05% sell, options 0.15% of premium sold · Exchange: NSE equity 0.00307% · SEBI ₹10 per crore · Stamp duty on the buy side · GST 18% on brokerage + exchange + SEBI
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The statutory stack
            <strong>STT ₹25.25 + exchange ₹6.17 + SEBI ₹0.20 + stamp duty ₹3.00 = ₹34.62 on the sample</strong>
            <span>STT is 0.025% of the ₹1,01,000.00 sell value; exchange charges are 0.00307% of the ₹2,01,000.00 turnover; stamp duty is 0.003% of the ₹1,00,000.00 buy value. These are the same at every broker.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The broker's fee and GST
            <strong>Brokerage ₹40.00, then GST 18% of ₹46.37 = ₹8.35</strong>
            <span>Intraday brokerage is the lower of ₹20 or 0.03% per order — ₹30.00 and ₹30.30, both capped at ₹20. GST is charged on brokerage, exchange charges and the SEBI fee, never on STT or stamp duty.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The net profit and the break-even
            <strong>₹1,000.00 − ₹82.97 = ₹917.03 net; break-even ₹1,000.83</strong>
            <span>Total charges ₹82.97, exactly tradeprofy's published figure. The break-even is solved for the exit price, because STT, exchange charges and percentage brokerage all scale with it.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹82.97 — the total charges on 100 shares @ ₹1,000 sold @ ₹1,010 intraday through Zerodha.</strong>
          ₹40.00 brokerage, ₹25.25 STT, ₹6.17 exchange charges, ₹0.20 SEBI fee, ₹3.00 stamp duty and ₹8.35 GST, on a net profit of ₹917.03.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: call-and-trade and auto-square-off fees, account maintenance charges, pledge fees, payment-gateway fees and interest on margin funding are not included. BSE transaction charges vary by scrip group — the calculator uses the standard group A/B rate of 0.00375%. And currency trades are not covered: the page models equity and MCX only, because those are the segments with verified published tariffs.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

