    <section class="calc-section" id="overview">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Understand the result</p>
          <h2>${e(p.overviewTitle)}</h2>
          <p>${e(p.overview)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">01 / THE FOUR LIMBS</span>
            <h3>₹25,00,000 is one limb, not the answer</h3>
            <p>On the sample the exemption is ₹6,40,000 — the leave-value limb — even though the payout is ₹15,00,000 and the ceiling is ₹25,00,000. The least of the four wins, and any one of them can bind.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">02 / THE DIVISOR GAP</span>
            <h3>26 raises the payout, not the exemption</h3>
            <p>On a 26-day divisor the same 240 days pay ₹7,38,461.54, but limb 4 still values them at ₹6,40,000 — the ₹98,461.54 gap between the two is taxed.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="card">
            <span class="number-label">03 / THE NEW REGIME</span>
            <h3>The exemption survives in both regimes</h3>
            <p>Section 115BAC(2) withdraws only the exemptions it lists — HRA, LTA and the Chapter VI-A deductions — and s.10(10AA) is not among them. One competitor page claims otherwise; it is wrong.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p>Three tools cover the three questions. The <a href="/gratuity-calculator/">Gratuity calculator</a> works out the other retirement lump sum; the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax calculator</a> works out the tax on the taxable balance; this tool works out the encashment and the exempt split. For the same figures under each scenario, see the <a href="#compare">scenario comparison</a>; for the four limbs in detail, see the <a href="#rates-table">limbs table</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

