    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How leave encashment and the exemption are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The leave encashment formulas</small>
          Encashment = (Basic + DA) ÷ divisor × leave days · Exempt = least of (amount received, 10 × average salary, ₹25,00,000 less prior claims, (Basic + DA) ÷ 30 × capped days) · Government employees fully exempt · During service fully taxable
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The payout
            <strong>(₹80,000 ÷ 30) × 300 = ₹8,00,000 computed — but ₹15,00,000 actually received</strong>
            <span>The daily rate is ₹2,666.67. The amount actually received is the employer's payout figure, and it — not the computed figure — is what limb 1 and the taxable balance use.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The four limbs
            <strong>₹15,00,000 · ₹8,00,000 · ₹25,00,000 · ₹6,40,000 — the least is ₹6,40,000</strong>
            <span>Limb 4 binds: only 240 of 300 days count, 30 days for each of 8 completed years, at ₹2,666.67 a day. Limb 2 is 10 × ₹80,000; limb 3 is the ₹25,00,000 ceiling with nothing claimed before.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The split
            <strong>₹6,40,000 exempt, ₹8,60,000 taxable — 42.67% escapes tax</strong>
            <span>The taxable balance is taxed as salary at the slab rate. The tax regime only sets the rate, not the exemption — the exemption applies under both the old and the new regime.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹6,40,000 — the exempt part of ₹15,00,000 received on retirement at ₹80,000 average salary, 300 days and 8 years.</strong>
          ₹8,60,000 is taxed as salary. The exemption is available under both the old and the new tax regime.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the calculator does not work out the tax rate on the taxable balance — see the Income Tax calculator for that — and it does not model TDS in the final settlement or state Shops &amp; Establishments Act accrual and carry-forward caps, because it takes the leave balance as given. The Code on Social Security 50% wages rule can change the payout base; the calculator takes Basic + DA as entered. And relief under s.89(1) is not modelled.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

