    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the EMI, the saving and the new payoff date are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The home loan prepayment formulas</small>
          EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1) · Interest this month = balance × r · New balance = balance − (EMI − interest) − part-prepayment · Interest saved = baseline interest − new interest
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The EMI
            <strong>₹50,00,000 at 8.5% over 180 months: EMI ₹49,236.98</strong>
            <span>EMI = P × r × (1+r)^n ÷ ((1+r)^n − 1), with r = 8.5% ÷ 12 ÷ 100 = 0.708333% a month and n = 180 months.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The prepayment
            <strong>₹5,000 a month on top of the EMI: the loan closes after 150 months</strong>
            <span>Each month the interest is balance × 0.708333%; the EMI pays the interest first and the rest cuts the principal, then the ₹5,000 cuts it further. The final EMI is adjusted down, never below zero.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The saving
            <strong>₹38,62,656 − ₹31,33,032 = ₹7,29,624 saved; ₹7,29,624 ÷ ₹7,47,485 = ₹0.98 per ₹1</strong>
            <span>Baseline interest 180 × ₹49,236.98 − ₹50,00,000 = ₹38,62,656. New interest ₹31,33,032. The difference is the saving; dividing by the prepayments gives the return per rupee.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹7,29,624 saved — ₹5,000 a month on ₹50 lakh at 8.5%, debt-free in May 2039 instead of November 2041.</strong>
          The saving is 18.89% of the interest the loan would have charged.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the rate is assumed fixed for the remaining tenure — a floating rate's actual saving differs if rates change; no prepayment charge is assumed unless you enter one; and the figures are estimates, not a lender quote. Prepaying also cuts the Section 24(b) interest deduction under the old tax regime — the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax Calculator</a> shows the other side of that choice.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

