    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the DA, the pay with DA and the arrears are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The DA formulas</small>
          DA = basic pay × DA rate %, rounded to the nearest rupee · DA a year = DA a month × 12 · Pay with DA = basic pay + DA · Arrears = (DA at new rate − DA at old rate) × months · NPS = 10% of the arrears (employees only)
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The DA
            <strong>₹56,100 × 63% = ₹35,343 a month, rounded to the nearest rupee</strong>
            <span>DA is basic pay times the rate in force, rounded to the nearest rupee — 50 paise and above rounds up. Pensioners' DR rounds up to the next rupee.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The pay with DA
            <strong>₹56,100 + ₹35,343 = ₹91,443 a month; ₹4,24,116 a year</strong>
            <span>The pay with DA is basic pay plus DA — before HRA, TA and deductions. DA is 38.65% of the pay with DA.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The arrears
            <strong>₹35,343 − ₹33,660 = ₹1,683 a month; × 4 months = ₹6,732</strong>
            <span>Arrears are the DA at the new rate minus the DA at the old rate, each rounded first, times the months paid at the old rate.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹35,343 a month of DA — ₹6,732 of arrears for 4 months — on ₹56,100 basic pay at 63%.</strong>
          The pay with DA is ₹91,443 a month.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the calculator does not model transport allowance — DA on TA applies only in two narrow cases — and it does not model HRA, which is its own percentage of basic pay. State government employees follow their own orders and rates; the 63% rate is the Centre's. Arrears are taxed in the year received.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

