    <section class="calc-section" id="methodology">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="method-box">
          <h2>About the method</h2>
          <p>
            DA = basic pay × DA rate %, rounded to the nearest rupee (fractions of 50 paise and above round up to the next rupee, smaller fractions are ignored). DR for pensioners is the same percentage of basic pension, rounded up to the next rupee. DA a year = DA a month × 12. Pay with DA = basic pay + DA. Arrears a month = DA at the new rate − DA at the old rate, each rounded first; total arrears = arrears a month × months paid at the old rate. NPS deduction = 10% of the arrears, for employees under NPS only. The current rate is 63% from 1 July 2026 (Union Cabinet approval 28 October 2026, PIB Release ID 2329484; Department of Expenditure OM No. 1/2(i)/2026-E.II(B) dated 29 October 2026); 60% ran from 1 January 2026 (DoE OM No. 1/1(i)/2026-E.II(B) dated 22 April 2026). Pensioners' DR follows the same rate (DoPPW OM No. 42/02/2024-P&amp;PW(D)/E-9475 dated 4 November 2026).
          </p>
          <p>
            The figures were checked 14 November 2026 against TabSalary, hrcalcy and indianpaycalculator. TabSalary reproduces exactly: basic pay ₹56,100 at 60% (January 2026) — DA ₹33,660 a month; the same basic at 46% (July 2023) — ₹25,806. hrcalcy reproduces exactly: ₹56,100, 60% to 63% — DA ₹33,660 to ₹35,343, arrears ₹1,683 a month, ₹6,732 for 4 months, NPS 10% ₹673, net ₹6,059; on a basic pension of ₹30,000, DR ₹18,000 to ₹18,900, arrears ₹900 a month, ₹3,600 for 4 months. indianpaycalculator reproduces exactly: ₹44,900, 46% to 50%, 2 months — old DA ₹20,654, new DA ₹22,450, arrears ₹1,796 a month, total ₹3,592. The rounding example also holds: ₹2,145.50 pays as ₹2,146 and ₹2,145.49 as ₹2,145. For related tools, the <a href="/salary-calculator/">Salary calculator</a>, the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">Income Tax calculator</a> and the <a href="/gratuity-calculator/">Gratuity calculator</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Last updated 14 November 2026.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

