    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the future cost, the gap and the SIP are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The child education formulas</small>
          Future cost = cost today × (1 + education inflation) ^ years · Gap = future cost − savings grown · SIP = gap ÷ what ₹1 a month grows to · Lumpsum = gap ÷ (1 + return) ^ years
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The future cost
            <strong>₹15,00,000 × 1.10^13 = ₹51,78,407</strong>
            <span>Education inflation of 10% a year for 13 years. The ₹51.78 L is ₹24.28 L in today's money at 6% general inflation.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The gap
            <strong>₹51,78,407 − ₹4,36,349 = ₹47,42,058</strong>
            <span>The ₹1 lakh saved grows at 12% for 13 years to ₹4,36,349 — 8.43% of the future cost.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The SIP — or the lumpsum
            <strong>₹12,614 a month, or ₹10,86,757 today</strong>
            <span>Beginning-of-month payments at 12% a year. With a 10% step-up the SIP starts at ₹7,821 a month.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>₹51,78,407 to fund — a SIP of ₹12,614 a month, or ₹10,86,757 invested today.</strong>
          The future cost is ₹24,27,839 in today's money.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: the return is an assumption, not a forecast — at 10% instead of 12% the sample SIP is ₹15,075 a month; the course costs are rough examples, not fee quotes; and the calculator does not model tax — gains on mutual funds are taxed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

