    <section class="calc-section" id="method">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">The formula</p>
          <h2>How the bedtimes and wake-up times are worked out</h2>
          <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="formula-banner">
          <small>The sleep formulas</small>
          Bedtime = wake-up time − onset − cycles × 90 min · Wake-up = bedtime + onset + cycles × 90 min
        </div>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="math-step">
            1. The cycles
            <strong>5 × 90 minutes = 7.5 hours</strong>
            <span>Five complete cycles is the recommended row for an adult. Six cycles is 9 hours; four is 6 hours; three is 4.5 hours.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            2. The onset
            <strong>6:00 AM − 15 minutes = 5:45 AM</strong>
            <span>Lights out is not asleep. The 15 minutes to fall asleep comes off first, before the cycles are counted back.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="math-step">
            3. The bedtime
            <strong>5:45 AM − 7.5 hours = 10:15 PM</strong>
            <span>Counting back five cycles from 5:45 AM lands on 10:15 PM — the recommended bedtime for a 6:00 AM alarm.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>10:15 PM to bed — 5 cycles, 7.5 hours, in the 7–9 hour adult range.</strong>
          Six cycles (9 hours) is 8:45 PM; four cycles (6 hours) is 11:45 PM.
        </div>

        <p>Three honest limits, and the page states them: cycles average about 90 minutes and vary from person to person and through the night (about 70 to 120 minutes); the recommended ranges are population averages, not personal prescriptions; and the calculator cannot detect sleep problems — loud snoring, stopping breathing or trouble sleeping for weeks needs a doctor, not a calculator.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

