// Emergency Fund Calculator.
// How big your emergency fund should be, how far your savings go
// already, and how long it takes to close the gap — worked out in
// the browser.
// - FORMULAS (verified 14 Nov 2026 — see sources.md): target =
//   monthly essential expenses x months of cover. Gap = target -
//   current savings, never below zero; surplus = savings - target
//   when savings pass the target. Coverage today = current savings /
//   monthly expenses, shown to 1 decimal place. Progress = savings /
//   target x 100, rounded to a whole percent. Status: "covered" when
//   savings reach or pass the target; "no fund yet" when savings are
//   zero and a gap remains; otherwise "short of target".
// - MONTHS TO REACH (optional monthly saving): ceil(gap / monthly
//   saving) when a gap remains and a positive monthly saving is
//   given; 0 when already covered; null when the gap remains but no
//   monthly saving is given or it is zero — the tool says so in
//   words and never shows a number.
// - MONTHLY SAVING NEEDED (optional build horizon): gap / build
//   months, rounded to the nearest rupee, when a gap remains and a
//   horizon is given — floored at Rs.1, so a gap that would round to
//   Rs.0 a month still shows Rs.1; 0 when already covered; null when
//   no horizon is given.
// - NO INTEREST IS COUNTED while the fund is being built. That is a
//   deliberate choice, stated on the page: krishnavriddhicapital.com
//   counts no interest either, while nivesh.org.in, calcmint.co.in
//   and moneymindtool.com project a return on the balance, so their
//   timelines are shorter. The differences are documented in
//   math-tests.md: nivesh's 9 months at Rs.15,000 a month (with a 5%
//   return) is 10 months here; moneymind's Rs.24,150 a month (with a
//   6.5% return) is Rs.25,000 here, and its Rs.11,800 a month over 24
//   months is Rs.12,500 here.
// - BREAKDOWN: efcBreakdownSum adds the eight optional itemised
//   fields (housing, food, utilities, transport, emis, insurance,
//   schoolFees, other); a missing field counts as 0, a non-whole or
//   negative field makes the sum null. The client fills the total
//   expenses field from the breakdown when any breakdown field is
//   entered.
// - VALIDATION (efcCompute returns null): expenses are whole rupees
//   from 1 to 10,000,000 (0 or missing means no result — the client
//   prompts for expenses instead); current savings are whole rupees
//   from 0 to 100,000,000; months of cover are a whole number from 1
//   to 24; monthly saving is null or whole rupees from 0 to
//   10,000,000; build months are null or a whole number from 1 to 60.
// - Anchors, checked 14 November 2026 against the independent Python
//   model (all fetched the same day): task sample Rs.50,000 x 6 =
//   Rs.3,00,000 target, Rs.2,00,000 gap, 2.0 months of cover;
//   nivesh.org.in: Rs.40,000 x 6 = Rs.2,40,000, shortfall Rs.1,40,000,
//   42% of target, 14 months at Rs.10,000 a month (1y 2m);
//   blogbanking.com: Rs.47,000 x 6 = Rs.2,82,000, Rs.1,00,000 saved
//   is 35% and 2.1 months of cover, Rs.30,333 a month builds it in 6
//   months; its table Rs.30,000/50,000/75,000/1,00,000 a month ->
//   Rs.15,000/25,000/37,500/50,000 a month to build 6 months in a
//   year; calcmint.co.in: Rs.40,000 x 6 = Rs.2,40,000 with a
//   Rs.1,40,000 gap, and benchmarks 3/6/9/12 months short by
//   Rs.20,000/1,40,000/2,60,000/3,80,000; moneymindtool.com:
//   Rs.50,000 x 6 = Rs.3,00,000, freelancer 12 months -> Rs.6 lakh;
//   krishnavriddhicapital.com: Rs.40,000 + Rs.15,000 = Rs.55,000 a
//   month, 6 months -> Rs.3.3 lakh, Rs.2.3 lakh still to build,
//   Rs.19,167 a month over 12 months.
// - THE 3-6 MONTH RULE: 3 to 6 months of essential expenses is the
//   most widely cited emergency fund target for people with stable,
//   salaried income. Households with a single income, irregular
//   income or dependants usually aim higher, up to 9-12 months.
//   Every competitor fetched agrees on this range; it is a rule of
//   thumb, not a regulation, and the page says so plainly.
// - WHERE TO KEEP IT (general guidance only, no product
//   recommendation): somewhere liquid and low-risk — a savings
//   account, a sweep-in fixed deposit or a liquid fund — so the money
//   is reachable the same day or the next working day. Bank deposits
//   are insured by DICGC up to Rs.5 lakh per depositor per bank
//   (blogbanking.com). Avoid equity funds and locked-in schemes.
//   This is a planning aid, not financial advice.
// - Nothing is sent anywhere: every calculation runs in the browser.
// Conventions (stated on the page):
// - The client sends exactly the 5 EFC_KEYS.
// - Expenses are whole rupees from 1 to 10,000,000 (default 50,000).
//   Current savings are whole rupees from 0 to 100,000,000 (default
//   100,000). Months of cover are a whole number from 1 to 24
//   (default 6, presets 3/6/9/12). Monthly saving is null or whole
//   rupees from 0 to 10,000,000 (default 10,000). Build months are
//   null or a whole number from 1 to 60 (default 12).
export const EFC_MIN_EXPENSES = 1;
export const EFC_MAX_EXPENSES = 10000000;
export const EFC_MIN_SAVINGS = 0;
export const EFC_MAX_SAVINGS = 100000000;
export const EFC_MIN_MONTHS = 1;
export const EFC_MAX_MONTHS = 24;
export const EFC_MIN_SAVING = 0;
export const EFC_MAX_SAVING = 10000000;
export const EFC_MIN_BUILD = 1;
export const EFC_MAX_BUILD = 60;
export const EFC_PRESET_MONTHS = [3, 6, 9, 12];
export const EFC_DEFAULT_MONTHS = 6;
export const EFC_BREAKDOWN_KEYS = ["housing", "food", "utilities", "transport", "emis", "insurance", "schoolFees", "other"];
export const EFC_KEYS = ["expenses", "currentSavings", "months", "monthlySaving", "buildMonths"];
export function efcRound1(n) {
  return Math.round(n * 10) / 10;
}
export function efcInr(n) {
  return "\u20b9" + Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-IN");
}
export function efcBreakdownSum(items) {
  if (!items || typeof items !== "object") return null;
  let total = 0;
  for (const k of EFC_BREAKDOWN_KEYS) {
    const v = items[k];
    if (v === null || v === undefined) continue;
    if (typeof v !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(v) || !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 0) return null;
    total += v;
  }
  return total;
}
export function efcCompute(input) {
  if (!input || typeof input !== "object") return null;
  const expenses = input.expenses;
  const currentSavings = input.currentSavings;
  const months = input.months;
  const monthlySaving = input.monthlySaving;
  const buildMonths = input.buildMonths;
  const isInt = v => typeof v === "number" && Number.isFinite(v) && Number.isInteger(v);
  if (!isInt(expenses) || expenses < EFC_MIN_EXPENSES || expenses > EFC_MAX_EXPENSES) return null;
  if (!isInt(currentSavings) || currentSavings < EFC_MIN_SAVINGS || currentSavings > EFC_MAX_SAVINGS) return null;
  if (!isInt(months) || months < EFC_MIN_MONTHS || months > EFC_MAX_MONTHS) return null;
  if (monthlySaving !== null && monthlySaving !== undefined) {
    if (!isInt(monthlySaving) || monthlySaving < EFC_MIN_SAVING || monthlySaving > EFC_MAX_SAVING) return null;
  }
  if (buildMonths !== null && buildMonths !== undefined) {
    if (!isInt(buildMonths) || buildMonths < EFC_MIN_BUILD || buildMonths > EFC_MAX_BUILD) return null;
  }
  const ms = (monthlySaving === null || monthlySaving === undefined) ? null : monthlySaving;
  const bm = (buildMonths === null || buildMonths === undefined) ? null : buildMonths;
  const target = expenses * months;
  const gap = Math.max(0, target - currentSavings);
  const surplus = Math.max(0, currentSavings - target);
  const coverageMonths = efcRound1(currentSavings / expenses);
  const progressPct = Math.round(100 * currentSavings / target);
  let status;
  let statusLabel;
  if (currentSavings >= target) {
    status = "covered";
    statusLabel = "Covered";
  } else if (currentSavings === 0) {
    status = "none";
    statusLabel = "No fund yet";
  } else {
    status = "short";
    statusLabel = "Short of target";
  }
  let monthsToReach;
  let monthlyNeeded;
  if (gap === 0) {
    monthsToReach = 0;
    monthlyNeeded = 0;
  } else {
    monthsToReach = (ms === null || ms === 0) ? null : Math.ceil(gap / ms);
    monthlyNeeded = bm === null ? null : Math.max(1, Math.round(gap / bm));
  }
  return {
    expenses,
    currentSavings,
    months,
    monthlySaving: ms,
    buildMonths: bm,
    target,
    targetLabel: efcInr(target),
    gap,
    gapLabel: efcInr(gap),
    surplus,
    coverageMonths,
    coverageLabel: coverageMonths.toFixed(1) + " months",
    progressPct,
    status,
    statusLabel,
    monthsToReach,
    monthlyNeeded,
  };
}

