// Plaster Calculator.
// How much cement and sand a plaster job needs, worked out in the browser.
// - Plaster only: area in sq ft or m2, thickness in mm (presets 6/12/15/20,
//   any value 3-50 mm allowed), cement:sand mix 1:3/1:4/1:5/1:6, sand type
//   river / M-sand / P-sand, optional rates (cement per 50 kg bag, sand per
//   cft or per brass) for a cost.
// - wet volume = area (m2) x thickness (m); dry volume = wet x 1.60, stated
//   on the page as the 1.33 dry-volume factor x 1.20 allowance for joints,
//   uneven surface and wastage (1.33 x 1.20 = 1.596, stated as 1.60).
// - cement (m3) = dry x cement parts / total parts; cement bags =
//   cement m3 x 1440 / 50 (0.0347 m3 per 50 kg bag; x 28.8).
// - sand (m3) = dry x sand parts / total parts; sand cft = m3 x 35.3147;
//   brass = cft / 100 (1 brass = 100 cft = 2.8317 m3); weight kg = m3 x
//   density; tonnes = kg / 1000; cement cost = bags x Rs/bag; sand cost =
//   cft x Rs/cft (or brass x Rs/brass when only a brass rate is given).
// - Sand types (bulk densities, the sand pack's densities reused verbatim):
//   river sand 1600 kg/m3, M-sand 1750 kg/m3, P-sand 1650 kg/m3.
// - The sample: 100 m2, 12 mm, 1:6, P-sand -> wet 1.2 m3; dry 1.92 m3;
//   cement 0.274 m3, 7.90 bags; sand 1.646 m3, 58.12 cft, 0.581 brass,
//   2715 kg, 2.715 tonnes.
// - DOCUMENTED DIFFERENCE (not an error): the site's own Sand Calculator
//   (entry 379) states 0.142 m3 / 5.03 cft / 235 kg of P-sand for 100 sq ft
//   of 12 mm 1:4 plaster — that figure comes from its 1.33-then-20%
//   sequence (1.33 x 1.20 = 1.596 effective). This tool states the dry
//   factor as 1.60, so the same job gives 0.143 m3 / 5.04 cft / 235 kg and
//   1.03 bags of cement — one rounding unit more sand (0.001 m3, 0.01 cft);
//   the weight and the cement match exactly. The page states this.
// - DOCUMENTED DIFFERENCES (not errors): the competitor calculators use
//   smaller dry factors, so they give smaller answers for the same job.
//   For 100 m2 of 12 mm plaster in 1:6: thecivilstudies and calcvind use
//   1.27 x 1.20 = 1.524 and give 7.5 bags / 55.4 cft (1000 sq ft: 7 bags /
//   51.4 cft); civilsite uses 1.33 with NO allowance and gives 6.6 bags /
//   48.3 cft; calculatorapp uses 1.35 with 10% wastage (1.485) and gives
//   7.33 bags / 1.527 m3. This tool (1.60) gives 7.90 bags / 58.12 cft.
//   The page prints the comparison and says the difference is the dry
//   factor — the allowance for joints, uneven surface and wastage.
// - Rounding is round-half-up with a 1e-9 nudge, as in the 379 pure file:
//   m3 to 3 decimals, cement bags to 2, cft to 2, brass to 3, kg to whole
//   kg, tonnes to 3, cost to whole rupees. Derived quantities are computed
//   from the unrounded intermediates, then rounded for display.
// - Validation: area 0.1-100000 (in the given unit); areaUnit "sqft" or
//   "sqm"; thicknessMm 3-50 (presets 6/12/15/20); mix "1:3"/"1:4"/"1:5"/
//   "1:6"; sandType "river"/"msand"/"psand"; rates 0-100000 (0 means not
//   entered). Invalid inputs return null, never a result.
