        <p>Honest limits: mileage is distance ÷ fuel, measured tank-to-tank — fill to a full tank, note the odometer, drive, then fill to a full tank again and divide the distance by the fuel that went in. One tank can be skewed by a short trip or a pump that is not perfectly calibrated, so repeat the test over three or four fills and average. Litres per 100 km is 100 ÷ mileage; US MPG is mileage × 2.352 and UK MPG is mileage × 2.825. Cost per km is price ÷ mileage, and the monthly bill is (km per day × days per month) ÷ mileage × price — the monthly figures on this page are the model's formulas, worked out from your own figures, and the yearly figure comes from the unrounded monthly cost, never from the rounded monthly figure. A price of 0 means no price was given, so cost per km, trip costs and the monthly and yearly bills show as —, never ₹0. Rounding is round-half-up. One documented difference: ap7am rounds the 300 km on 6.7 litres case to a flat 45 km/L and ₹2.58/km, while the exact division gives 44.78 km/L and ₹2.59/km — this page shows the exact figure and states the difference. The same unrounded rule is why ap7am's 15 km/L at ₹116.15 a litre case gives ₹7,743 a month and ₹92,920 a year: the rounded monthly figure × 12 would give ₹92,916, ₹4 less. Brochure figures come from lab test cycles and usually run 10–25% higher than real-world driving; the benchmark table on this page is the real-world yardstick.</p>
