    <section class="calc-section" id="how-to-use">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2>How to use this 7th Pay Commission salary calculator</h2>
        </div>
        <ol class="steps">
          ${p.steps.map(([t2, d2]) => `
          <li><strong>${e(t2)}.</strong> ${e(d2)}</li>`).join("")}
        </ol>
        <h3>The formulas behind the answer</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li><strong>Basic pay</strong> — from the pay matrix for your level, or from your pay slip. On the sample: Level 7, ${moneyText(sample.basicPay)}.</li>
          <li><strong>Dearness allowance (DA)</strong> — basic pay × DA rate: ${moneyText(sample.basicPay)} × ${cpc7FmtNum(sample.daRate)}% = ${moneyText(sample.da)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>House rent allowance (HRA)</strong> — basic pay × the HRA rate for the city class and DA tier (24/16/8 below 25% DA, 27/18/9 from 25%, 30/20/10 from 50%), or the floor (₹5,400 X, ₹3,600 Y, ₹1,800 Z), whichever is higher: ${moneyText(sample.basicPay)} × ${sample.hraPct}% = ${moneyText(sample.hra)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Transport allowance</strong> — the slab for the level and city, plus DA on the slab: ${moneyText(sample.taBase)} + ${moneyText(sample.taDa)} DA = ${moneyText(sample.ta)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Gross monthly</strong> — basic + DA + HRA + TA: ${moneyText(sample.basicPay)} + ${moneyText(sample.da)} + ${moneyText(sample.hra)} + ${moneyText(sample.ta)} = ${moneyText(sample.grossMonthly)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>NPS — employee share</strong> — 10% of (basic + DA) on NPS, ₹0 on GPF: 10% × (${moneyText(sample.basicPay)} + ${moneyText(sample.da)}) = ${moneyText(sample.npsEmployee)} on the sample. The government adds 14%, ${moneyText(sample.govtNps)}.</li>
          <li><strong>CGHS contribution</strong> — fixed by level: ₹250 (Levels 1–5), ₹450 (Level 6), ₹650 (Levels 7–11), ₹1,000 (Level 12+): ${moneyText(sample.cghs)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Taxable income</strong> — gross annual − ₹75,000 standard deduction: ${moneyText(sample.grossAnnual)} − 75,000 = ${moneyText(sample.taxableAnnual)} a year on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Income tax</strong> — new regime for FY 2026-27, with the Section 87A rebate to ₹12 lakh, marginal relief just above, surcharge above ₹50 lakh and 4% cess: ${moneyText(sample.taxAnnual)} a year (${money2Text(sample.incomeTaxMonthly)} a month) on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Net monthly</strong> — gross monthly − NPS − CGHS − professional tax − income tax: ${moneyText(sample.grossMonthly)} − ${money2Text(sample.totalDeductions)} = ${moneyText(sample.netMonthly)} on the sample.</li>
          <li><strong>Next increment</strong> — basic pay × 1.03, rounded to the nearest ₹100: ${moneyText(sample.basicPay)} × 1.03 = ${moneyText(sample.nextIncrementBasic)} on the sample, a rise of ${moneyText(sample.nextIncrementAmount)}.</li>
          <li><strong>Promotion</strong> — the next level's first cell: Level ${sample.nextLevel} starts at ${moneyText(sample.promotionBasic)} on the sample.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>What the city class is worth</h3>
        <p>The same Level 7 pay slip at 63% DA is ${moneyText(levelRows[3].r.grossMonthly)} gross in an X city. In a Y city the HRA is ₹8,980 instead of ₹13,470, so the gross is ₹88,035; in a Z city the HRA is ₹4,490, so the gross is ₹83,545. HRA is the only part of the pay slip that follows the city class: DA, transport allowance, NPS and CGHS do not change with it. If you are choosing between postings, or between government quarters and HRA, the HRA figure is the one to compare.</p>
        <p>On NPS versus GPF: NPS deducts 10% of basic plus DA every month and the government adds 14% to the pension account. GPF deducts nothing on this page, so the in-hand pay is higher — on a Level 10 pay slip at 63% DA, ₹1,12,012.58 a month against ₹1,02,868.58 on NPS — but no pension corpus builds up. NPS is compulsory for employees who joined on or after 1 January 2004, so most employees do not get to choose.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

