    <section class="calc-section" id="methodology">
      <div class="calc-wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2>Methodology and honest limits</h2>
        </div>
        <p>${e(p.methodology)}</p>
        <p>${e(p.modelNote)}</p>
        <p>Honest limits: the model assumes the basic pay you type is the one on your pay slip and the city class is the one your posting carries. It does not model any increment or promotion under the new matrix, because the 8th CPC matrix has not been notified. HRA after the revision has not been notified either — the tool applies the current 30%/20%/10% tiers as a stated assumption. It does not model the Level 1–2 transport allowance exception for basic pay of ₹24,200 or more, or the Level 14+ official-car transport allowance option. CGEGIS is not deducted, because the pay-level to group mapping is not officially verified. Income tax is an estimate under the new regime for FY 2026-27; the exact figure depends on other income and deductions. This is not financial advice.</p>
        <p>Documented differences: (1) BlogBanking rounds the projected basic to the nearest ₹100 (₹68,000 at 1.92 on ₹35,400) where this page rounds to the nearest ₹10 (₹67,970). (2) BlogBanking's "monthly total" is the projected basic × 1.24 — an HRA of 24% only — not the full gross; this page's gross on the same figures is ₹91,961. (3) EthPayCommission's estimated net for Level 7 at 1.92 is ₹98,160 where this page gives ₹99,732.08; their page publishes no component breakdown, so the gap cannot be reconciled from their page. (4) NineClip applies HRA at 24%/16%/8% — the pre-2016 rates — where this page applies the current 30%/20%/10% tiers as a stated assumption; on Level 6 at 2.57 in a Y city their gross is ₹1,07,334 against this page's ₹1,10,976. (5) Competitor figures are rounded as printed; differences under ₹2 on a component and under 0.1% on a total are rounding. No fitment factor or pay matrix has been notified as of 16 November 2026 — every figure here is a projection. Rounding here is round-half-up.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

